      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>ABLAZE WITH ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>ABLAZE WITH ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk The Only Cure For Fiery</p>
        <p>Rash On Hadds And Face.</p>
        <p>Describing to a representative of the "Car- Baryon and Denbigh Herald" how Zam-Buk alone cured her terrible eczema, Mrs. Hughes, who lives at Glan Gors, Llandzygarn, near Llanerch3-medd, Anglesey, said : "Tiny pimples came on my hands, accom- panied by intense itching. Soon a fiery rash broke out, and afterwards spread to my face. The affected parts were very red, and full of inflammation. There was no discharge, but little scales came over my hands and face. Several remedies proved unsuccessful, and I con- tinued to be disfigured and inconvenienced by the irritating eczema. "My sister then recommended Zam-Buk, and I noticed that the very first dressings did my face and hands a lot of good. Zam-Buk seemed to act quite differently to other ointments. I could see and feel it was doing me good. First, the irritation died away, and there was a dis- tinct improvement after each application of Zam-Buk. Zam-Buk removed the itchy scales and completed my cure by leaving my face and hands covered with healthy new skin." Zam-Buk has real medicinal power, and is unequalled for ringworm, bad legs, piles, poisoned wounds, sore, cracked hands, chil- blains, burns, scalds, cuts, bruises, &amp;c. Sold by all chemists and druggists. Prices 1/1i plus lid. war tax, and 2/9 plus 3d. War tax. The larger size contains nearly four times as much as the small box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>1 zantlo-</head>
        <p>1 zantlo-</p>
        <p>TWO HOURS LESS.</p>
        <p>Letter Box At G.P.O. To Close At 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>SPEEDING UP LETTER. WRITING.</p>
        <p>Those who have been in tile habit of linger- ing for a long time over their evening corre- spondence, then strolling down town and put- ting their communications into the head office box at 10 p.m. will receive somewhat of a shock by the latest announcement that the letter box there will be finally closed on and after Mon- day next at eight o'clock. Mr. H. Chambers, postmaster, has issued a notice to this effect. The exigencies of war are being brought home to us not with a startling revolution, but by a gentle and gradual development. Those who revelled in the possibility of catching the night mail by posting at ten o'clock must now "speed up" in order to benefit by the shortened time limit. It should be remembered that the cur- tailment only affects the letter :yox at our head office, and that the street boxes and sub-offices will remain at the usual time, seven o'clock. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ A quarter of a century ago, when probably letters were couched in more voluminous and expressive terms, the luxury of a quarter to eleven collection could be availed of. Then the hour of sailing the mail boats was reduced by some twenty minutes, and the Post Office authorities, not desirous of discommoding an easy-going public, were satisfied to make the last collection 10.30, depending upon an efficient staff to maintain the status in quo. When war came upon us necessity knew no law. A large number of reservists were called to the Colours and there was a reduction in the %umber of afternoon collections and deliveries. Then the demand for men continued and volunteers rushed to serve their country, the G.P.O. service responding nobly. Collections and de- liveries were further reduced. Although female assistance has been largely availed of—to a'very great extent, one might say—this particular branch of postal work felt most of all a shortage in thoroughly experienced assistants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>BUSINESS MEN'S TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>BUSINESS MEN'S TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>In a commercial community one naturally wishes to know how the latest announcement is likely to affect our big business concerns. As is generally known, large establishments have their own post bags, and collect and deliver their correspondence in bulk; so that two hours less in catching the night mail must mean a very appreciable difference in the matter of daily routine. On the other hand it is, how- ever, pointed out that the most important batch of business communications arrive by the morn- ing delivery, and that the whole day is avail- able to deal with it. Then the last delivery at 3.20 in the afternoon should give ample oppor- tunity for replying to mails arriving at an earlier hour. It may mean more employes in the correspondence departments, or more in- tense occupation during the reduced period, but, whichever and whatever remedy should be pre- scribed, we may feel assured that our mercan- tile experts will speedily arrive at a satisfactory solution of the difficulty. Any further diminution of our postal facili- ties is not anticipated for a long time to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ABSENT WITHOUT LEAVE.</head>
        <p>ABSENT WITHOUT LEAVE.</p>
        <p>Labourer Produces Doctor's Certificate</p>
        <p>Without Avail.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Munitions Court on Mon- day, Mr. T. J. Ridgway, who was assisted as assessors by Mr. G. P. Varley (St. Helens) for employers, and Mr. W. Bradbury (Warrington) for the men, heard a charge preferred by a local firm against one of their labourers, John Reilly, 3, Evelyn-street. The firm complained that the man had absented himself without leave for • several hours on the 9th, 10th and 11th October. A representative said that when Reilly did not come in to work before breakfast time he sent a messenger to him, and he stated that he overslept himself, and was unwell. Witness informed him that it would be necessary to get a doctor's certificate. The man produced a doctor's certificate dated October 20th, which showed that he was suffer- ing from heart trouble, but it was held that this certificate was not adinissible, as it did not deal with the dates of the alleged offence. A fine of 10s. was`inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>RIDE A</head>
        <p>RIDE A</p>
        <p>RFILt IGH</p>
        <p>THE FILL- 3TEEL BIC.YCLE</p>
        <p>Guaranteed for ever, and built entirely of the finest cold-rolled steel—no malleable iron castings at in other bicycles. Prices—From £7 10s. to £ll 10s.</p>
        <p>Send a Postcard for "The Book of the Raleigh."</p>
        <p>Widnes: C. Bingham, 7a, Victoria Road. Runcorn: IL Smith and Son, 22, Oreenway 'Road. St. Helens: W. Cook, 107, Church Street Earlestown: J. A. Churchill &amp; Co., Cross Lane. Ashton-in-Makerfield: S. Reeves, Warrington Road. Lawton St. Mary: Birkenhead Bros., 128-146, Newton Road.</p>
        <p>RALEIGH CYCLE CO., LTD., Nottingham.</p>
        <p>Spite and ill-nature are among the most expensive luxuries of life.—Samuel Johnson.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>located the artistic industry for a time, makes clear that the mechanical processes are of necessity confined to conventional designs, there being no restriction in the case of hand-made; hence, thanks to the spread of education and the provision made by County Councils, this has unmistakably revived of late years, buyers in general being no longer satisfied with trash, although they have much more to pay for the article of their choice.</p>
        <p>MOTHER GIBIES SYRUP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" Dickens' Popularity."</p>
        <p>Mr. Sydney Jeffery's letter under this head- ing afforded me considerable satisfaction; I am indeed encouraged by and grateful for it. While there are many more of the later generation than I had supposed who are with us in the conviction that in Dickens we have a goodly heritage, so far as I am personally concerned the lines have not latterly in this respect fallen unto me in pleasant places. Of course I knew about the Dickens' Fellowship; but the branches thereof that have extended to my part of the country at least are mostly withered and dying because the interest of the younger members was lacking. The other day I spoke to a youth who had just passed from the Secondary School into business life. I found him bright, intelli- gent, and remarkably well read, but utterly unable to appreciate Dickens; in fact, he positively confessed to me that he had made three serious attempts to read "David Copper- field," but found that immortal novel intoler- able! Small wonder that I was beginning to despair of the rising race. Names of Characters.</p>
        <p>A writer on the subject observed a while ago— "lt is not everyone who knows that Dickens always took a name which had existence in real life, and never manufactured one for the occasion." I don't believe it for a moment; on the contrary, I think he comparatively seldom used an actual surname. True, I have come across a Snodgrass, and I have heard of Pick- wicks, and even of Snawleys, Grummers, and Dubbleys; but these and a few others do mot go far in support of my friend. I have always understood, and certainly think it much more likely, that Dickens habitually noted all the curious names that came his way, and, when he required a fresh one, took the former half of one and the latter half of another from his collection, and with these supplied his need.</p>
        <p>His Worship. In the course of a few remarks reported„ in last week's issue, the Mayor of Warrington told of his occasional receipt of a letter to the /Affect that he had not satisfied everybody. 1114 Wor- ship seems in no danger of incurring the woe pronounced upon those of whom all men speak well. The nasty, spiteful, unsigned communi- cations he can well afford to disregard, while doubtless ever ready to 'Welcome a bit of manly, straightforward, well-informed criticism. I once lived in a borough which obtained its Charter of Incorporation while I was there, and the poorer inhabitants hailed the advent of the new authority as if it were invested with a magic wand under which their troubles would roll away and be heard of no more for ever. Their attention to and attendance upon the Mayor, whom they apparently looked upon— especially when he wore his official robes—a, a sort of fairy godmother, was for a time quite embarrassing. They called upon him at home with impossible requests which would have been very amusing if the evident seriousness of the applicants had not made them pathetic. His Worship assured me that a number of his poverty-stricken neighbours passed on the demand notes to him, some wag with a distorted sense of humour having told them that it was the rule for the Mayor to pay their rates dur- ing his first year of office! Afforestation.</p>
        <p>The good Norwegian law commended in last week's Farm column reminded me of many other such precautionary measures rigidly enforced in countries possessing valuable tim- ber. Think of the operations of the lumber kings of America. As one commentator put it not very long ago, imagine a train 100,000 miles long, made up of freight-cars laden with sawn timber. That is not the dream of 'a Mad Hatter, but the suggestion soberly offered by the U.S. Commissioner of Corporations to represent the amount of standing timber at pre- sent in the hands of three American owners. The Southern Pacific Railway holds 106,000 million feet; the Weyerhauser Timber Com- pany, 96,000 baillion feet; and the Northern Pacific Railway 36,000 million feet. So reported Mr. Herbert Knox Smith, in the course of the first instalment of what was described as "the first comprehensive and methodical investiga- tion" of the amount and ownership of the standing timber of the United States. The point I wish to make is that, while others are systematically conserving their timber—for they take care methodically to replace by saplings the trees they fell—we are rapidly ex- hausting ours and continuing to disregard the authoritative pleas for a national scheme of afforestation—l mean we persisted in this attitude when such plans were feasible; just now, of course, they are out of the question. Still pessimistic, you perceive. Perhaps we old fogeys look too much on the dark side. Any- how, I shall be pleased if someone who knows better will apply his superior knowledge after the manner of the London correspondent who wrote on Dickens' popularity.</p>
        <p>Zepp Time Bell. How the arrangements as to this at Lymm Parish Church would have interested a late octogenarian clergyman of ray acquaintance! He made a kind of hobby of clocks and bells, and, being wealthy and generously-disposed, he saw that three or four village churches within easy reach of his parish were duly equipped with these cosentials, as he deemed them to be. In one instance I was present at the dedication, and the donor himself preached. Moreover, he intro- duced a singularly appropriate and quaint old hymn which he said had come -down to him from his grandmother. There were twelve verses, each dealing with an hour, and the musical accompaniment was only mastered after considerable repetition. The congregation were. earnestly asked to sing this fearful and won- derful thing on each anniversary of that dedi- cation. I looked in on one occasion of the kind to see how they fulfilled their implied pledge, and I found that the Vicar, who was also choir- master, had chopped it into threP parts, thus making it more tolerable, and, incidentally, supplying .the hymns for the entire service.</p>
        <p>Esperanto. What the Generals of the Allies think of this, reproduced on page 2 of the last, issue, caused me to turn up my records of some other utter- ances on the subject. One mistaken critic is altogether beside the mark in declaring that "a universal language which would kill national languages would not only itself decay as a subtle instrument of expression, but would deprive the various nations of the better part of their per- sonality." And again—" Every nation has heralded the coming of its intellectual and moral greatness by an enthusiasm for its language. England could never have produced a Shakespeare had she been sleeping in Latin or Norman chains. It is equally certain that Russia could not have produced a Tolstoy or a Turgeney had she been sleeping in French or English chains." Granted an' it please you. And then what? As an argument against Esperanto it is as ineffectual as mere beating of the air. The simple answer is that Esperanto is not designed as a universal or substitutionary language but as an auxiliary language—" a second language in the homes of the people," as General Matton expresses it. I should be loth to suppose any serious student capable of deliberately mis- representing it, but certainly opponents—and there are many—who take the line above indicated do not understand t4e object at all. Hand-made Lace.</p>
        <p>Writing of this a week ago, " Marjorie " gave cumncy to the common and pardonable notion that the ancient and once flourishing industry in question perished as the yesult of the intro- duction of the Amachine-made artiale. The latter is pretty enough in its way- and incom- parably cheaper than the hand-made; but for those who have eyes to see it has its limitations. As I have mentioned previously, I have recently enjoyed the privilege of a clip into a highly in- teresting little book ,on the subject. It is entitled "Devon Pillow Lace: Its History and How to Make It," and ray authority, .while constrained to admit that machine work dis-</p>
        <p>(Continued in preceding column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>BAN I S HES</head>
        <p>BAN I S HES</p>
        <p>, -':: :. , : •,,,I, . , , i ~,,::. _ . 7' i s. : • '2.: "i V:: 'l4 i ", , I • •.,,, ..• • - ~., , .. . -?, ~,,,.:,'• , . ,et.i. i(‘'.).44,--::.!-..:;:‘,..,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</p>
        <p>MR. BEN FEW.</p>
        <p>Death &amp; Funeral Of Prominent</p>
        <p>Tradesman And Baptist.</p>
        <p>The greatest regret has been caused among a large circle of friends at the death of Mr. Benjamin Rew, a well-known provision mer- chant and Baptist, who resided at Laburnum House, Grappenhall-road, Latchford. Mr. Few passed away on Sunday morning, and his funeral took place on Thursday. It was a melancholy coincidence that a sale of work, in the preparation of which he had taken 'a deep interest, should have been fixed to be held at Golborne-street Baptist Church on the day of his interment, and this was postponed for a week.</p>
        <p>Mr. Few was taken suddenly ill while serving in one of his shops at Town's End a fortnight last Monday, and on the following Thursday was conveyed to Doctor Fox's Nurs- ing Home in Rylands-street. An operation was there performed by Drs. Robinson (his medical attendant), Fox and Langdale, and later it was found necessary to have another. For this pur- pose Dr. Newbolt, a specialist residing at Liver_ pool, was called in on Saturday, but it availed nothing, and Mr. Few passed away early next morning. The second son of the late Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Few, of Stockton Heath, he was 55 years of age, and commenced his business career as a grocer in the Co-operative Stores in Sankey- street, being promoted to manage that in Win- wick-street. In 1901 he purchased a prosperous provision shop opposite Hie Winwick-street Stores from Mr. Knowles, who went to live in retirement, and later the business was developed by the establishing of meat and confectionery shops, contiguously and also in other parts of the town. With the coming of the war he was hard hit owing to some of his employes enlist- ing and, being unable to obtain others, he was compelled to close his shops in Algernon-street and Scott-street. His only son, Alfred, entered the Army in March, and, being attached to the motor transport section, went to the Front three</p>
        <p>weeks after his enlistment. He arrived on Wednesday evening to attend the funeral. Apart from his business, Mr. Few's greatest interest lay in the welfare of Golborne-street Baptist Church, of which he was senior deacon. A devout gentleman, he regularly attended the services, and was a generous supporter. Only a few months ago he gave to the church a beautiful communion service in memory of his father and mother.</p>
        <p>Mr. Few was a manager of the Silver-street Day School, 'having succeeded the late Mr. Balmford about two years ago, and also held a similar position in connection with the Wycliffe Day School. Thirty years ago Mr. Few married Miss Margaret, the eldest daughter of the late Alderman Luke Ellison, of Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WAR PENSIONS,</head>
        <p>WAR PENSIONS,</p>
        <p>Mayor's Complaint Regarding</p>
        <p>Cost Of Administration.</p>
        <p>SHOULD BE A NATIONAL</p>
        <p>BURDEN.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), who was unanimously elected chairman, made an important speech at the first meeting of the local War Pensions Committee, in the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening, traversing what has already been done in the borough, criticising certain official regulations, and mentioning, in- cidentally', that some additional grants made to dependents will have to be stopped. He said: As you are aware, this is the first meeting of the Local Naval and Military War Pensions Committee.</p>
        <p>There has been considerable delay in holding a meeting, but the delay does not rest with the Town Council or the Local Committee. The Local Committee could not meet until it was properly constituted and the Committee was only completed on Tuesday last.</p>
        <p>The Town Council was one of the first to make a scheme for the constitution of the committee, which was duly approved by the Statutory Committee on the 9th day of May, 1916.</p>
        <p>• On the Bth day of June last a dispute as to the appointment of certain membeim was sub- mitted to the Statutory Committee, and their decision was only given on the 23rd September. It is satisfactory to know that the delay has not interfered with the work under the War Pensions Act, or caused any inconvenience to those whose interests were concerned.</p>
        <p>The work has been very ably continued by the Committee of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association and the Soldiers' and Sailors' Help Society, with the assistance of 'Mr. T.. H. Spinks, Mrs. Dakin, Mrs. Seeker and Mrs. Garnett, who have from the first Worked so ably and indefatigably on behalf of the soldiers and sailors and their wives and other dependents, and who have seen that every case has had full attention and that nobody has been neglected. Mr. Houghton and the Bor- ough Treasurer's Department have given all assistance possible. FULL JUSTICE TO BE DONE.</p>
        <p>I very much doubt whether the committee will be able to improve very much on the way in which the work has previously been done, and I only hope. that those who have so ably assisted hitherto will continue to give the com- mittee all the help they can. (Hear, hear.) There are new conditions which the committee will have to face, and which, I am afraid, they will find trying and somewhat irksome. I would almost call them the three R's—Rules, Regu- lations, and Red-tape. (Laughter.) You will have gathered from*the papers which have been sent to you the nature of the restrictions under which the Committee will have to work. I, for my own part, do not feel disposed to submit very quietly to the control of the Statutory Committee if I find that full justiee cannot be meted out to those whom the committee are appointed to serve by unnecessary or useless restrictions and conditions imposed by the Statutory Committee. In connection with the appointment of offi- cials we shall have to consider the cost of administration. I take it that in addition to a Secretary and Treasurer, one or more visiting or inquiry officers will be necessary. Offices and stationery will have to be provided and clerks to do the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TOWN'S SHARE.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN'S SHARE.</p>
        <p>It is impossible to estimate at this stage what the expenses are likely to be, but I should say that ..t5OO would be a very low estimate .indeed, and if the work is to be thoroughly and effi- ciently done much more may be required. In my opinion the whole cost of administra- tion should be borne by the State. We are dealing with a national matter, and the nation should bear the cost.</p>
        <p>The Statutory Committee have suggested that all local funds and subscriptions should be applied towards the cost of administration, and in increasing the Government doles where they are found to be insufficient. We resolutely decline to do this. I am not in favour of anything of the sort. We have £1,512 sub- scribed to our Local Relief Fund. It is a fund which is not likely to grow much more, and it may be found to be of great use in the relief of special cases. It would be a mistake to use such moneys in increasing pensions and per- manent allowances. We certainly would not agree to it. The country is prepared to pay full and adequate pensions and allowances so that nobody should suffer, and it is the duty of the Government to provide the funds. Referring again to administrative expenses, the Government, I believe, have 'agreed to contribute one-half, so that if we take the low estimate which I named of 1.500, it would mean that £250 must be provided from the rates. This committee has no funds and it will be for the Town Council to determine whether they will pay any and what proportion of the administration expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CERTAIN GRANTS TO BE STOPPED.</head>
        <p>CERTAIN GRANTS TO BE STOPPED.</p>
        <p>His Worship added that on and after Nov- ember Ist certain additional grants which had been made to dependents in Warrington would have to be stopped. Warrington had been most generous in making allowances which had never been legally granted, but the money had been forthcoming from the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association and other sources. Still there would be small items which would have to be stopped in some cases, and he felt the committee would be able to explain it to the satisfaction of the individuals concerned. The Mayor then introduced the Hon. E. H. Cozens-Hardy, who was ,present from the Statutory Committee in London, to explain the scheme under which the local committee is to work. Concerning the pensions, he said that the local committee could only pake recommenda- tions to the Statutory Committee, who would either .adopt or modify them, and the pensions would be paid by the Statutory • Committee through the ,Post Office. The local committee would really act as an Advisory Committee. With regard to supplementary allowances to soldiers' wives and families, they should be re- stricted to cases where, owing to exceptional circumstances, the separation allowance seemed to be inadequate, but it was not within the power of the committee, or of the Statutory Committee, to increase the general rate through the higher cost of living for that was a thing which affected the whole population and not merely the dependants. of those who were serv- ing. One of the first duties of the committee, when they took over the work on November Ist, would be to revise the grants of cases now on the books, and to satisfy themselves that they were within the regulations. It was also the duty of the committee to care for disabled officers and men who had left the service, including provision for their health, care and employment. That was becoming increasingly important. For the purpose of dealing with that work it was specially recommended that there should be a sub-committee, with added members from other bodies; which might be of assistance. Looking after the health of disabled then was not a short procedure at all, for in a great many cases the health of the men would vary over a long period, and they might want sending to institu- tions-and training for a year or two. In some cases, to get anything approaching the best results, treatment required to be carried on in special centres for a couple of years or even more.. In that way apparently Useless limbs might be restored. But not only had they got to get the disabled men back to health, but they had to provide them with training to suit them for earning a living. Regarding allowances to anyone, if there were any cases which the local committee considered to be very deserving and the regulations did not meet the circumstances, the Statutory Committee were only too anxious to deal with them. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>He Was.</head>
        <p>He Was.</p>
        <p>Tommy: "They takes me from 'ome an' brings me inter barracks—they takes away my clothes an' puts me inter khaki—they takes away my name an' gives me a number-535 they sends me ter church—an' after a forty minutes' sermon the parson see Number 535, Art thou weary, art thou languid?' I jumps up an' see 'Yus,' an' gets 14 days' C.B. for giving a civil answer."—" Grand Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>IMPRESSIVE SCENES AT FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE SCENES AT FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Church was filled with a mournful throng on Thursday afternoon when the deacons, amid a solemn hush, carried the coffin down the right aisle and laid it on a purple dais within the Communion rail. Among those present was the Mayor (Alder- man Peter Peacock). The mourners were:— Lance-Corporal Alfred Few (son), Messrs. Joseph and Walter Few (brothers), Messrs. Cotterill, James Johnson, J. S. Green, W. Watts, W. H. Ford, Joseph Ellison and David Ellison (brothers-in-law), Mr. Arthur Ellison (nephew), Messrs. Tom Ellison, J. Chesters, and J. and S. Albinson (cousins). The bearers were Messrs. H. Buckby, J. H. Pepper, J. S. Owen, D. Michael, A. Still (deacons) and S. Fernley an ex-officer of the church). Among those present were Councillor R. Henshall, Messrs. J. E. Balmford, E. Balmford, Frank Bate, T. Longshaw, J. Longland, Levi Rich- ardson, E. Lloyd Jones, J. B. Doncaster and W. T. Wilkinson. _ _</p>
        <p>_ The Pastor (the Rev. H. Ogden) conducted the service and took the opportunity of testi- fying to the sunny character of Mr. Few and his devotion to the church. Several hymns were sung, and as the body was borne away Mr. S. Rickers impressively rendered the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>The interment took place at Hill Cliffs, when the last rites were performed by the Revs. H. Ogden, G. Curtis and P. Rogerson, the last- named of Little Legh. There was a brougham of wreaths, these being sent by: "Widow, Alfred and Annie," Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Few (Southport), Mr. and Mrs. Walter Few (Altrincham), Mr. and Mrs. Coterill (Newton-le-Willows), Mr. and Mrs. Watts (Chorlton), Mr. and Mrs. Johnson (Dews- bury), Mr. and Mrs. David Ellison .(Preston), Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Ford, Mrs. Massey, Mr. and Mrs. Jepson (Orford), Joe, Sam, Tom and Alfred Albinson, Dr. and Mrs. Robinson, Alderman and Mrs. Peacock, lfr. and Mrs. Norman Balmford (Manchester), Mr. and Mrs. Webb (Cheadle), Mrs. Dannett and "Carrie," Pastor, Officers and Congregation, Golborne- street Church ;, Sunday School Scholars, Mrs. Davey and family, Mr. and Mrs. Metcalfe, Mr. and Mrs. John Strange, Mr. and Mrs. J. Bow- den, Mr. and Mrs. Bayley, Mr., Mrs. and Miss Potter (Crewe), Mrs. J. Bamforth and family, Warrington Bakers' Union, Mr. and Mrs. Ken- rick (St. Helens). Mrs. and Miss Roberts and Miss Richards, Mr. and Mrs. T. Hankey and family, Mrs. D. Hewitt and family, "Grace. Rose, Elsie and Cissie," Mr. and Mrs. Walkin, Mr. and Mrs. A. Law- less, Mr. and Mrs. E. Coates, Mr. and Mrs. Fred Davies (Penketh), Mr. and Mrs. Hawthorn and family, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Lees, Messrs. Shaw and Sons, Mr. and Mrs. Hayes and family, Mr. and Mrs. Buckby, Mr. and Mrs. Williams, Mr. and Mrs. T. Hewitt, Mrs. Roscoe and Miss Bullen, Mr. Thomas Raybould, The Misses Fieldhouse, Mr. and Mrs. Hayes and family, Mr. and Mrs. Bithell, Mr. and Mrs. Dunn and "Lily," Mr. and Mrs. Kershaw, Mr. and Mrs. J. Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. -Howard, Mr. J. Barlow, Mrs. H. Stewart and family-, Mr. and Mrs. J. Stewart, Mr. and Mrs. T. Ellison. Several wreaths and floral tributes were re- ceived without names. Messrs. Joseph Mounfield and Sons had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Beswick presided over the usual meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, when the address was given by Mr. J. S. bßichards, and the soloist was Miss Brough, who sang "I heard the voice of Jesus say." Both speaker and soloist were greatly enjoyed. Mr. C. Cotton and Private A. Price (South Lancashires) took part in the meeting; C. Norris (South Lancashires) sent a message of good wishes to the brethren. A vote of sym- pathy was passed to Private Fred Brandwood, in hospital in London, having been invalided home sick from France. The Secretary requested the brethren who had any desire to send Christmas puddings to their dear ones at the Front to send them through "The Warrington Examiner" Fund, to be sent in money. He also asked them not to forget their own Christmas cheer for their own lads away from the Wycliffe constituency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>RYLANDS BROS.'BOWLERS</head>
        <p>RYLANDS BROS.'BOWLERS</p>
        <p>Season's Successful Competitors</p>
        <p>Receive Their Prizes.</p>
        <p>THE NEED FOR RECREA- TION.</p>
        <p>A highly successful smoking concert was given in Rylands's Recreation Club Pavilion on Saturday evening, on the occasion of the distribution of prizes, by Mr. W. Peter Rylands, to the successful competitors in the bowling section during the past season. Mr. W. Webb presided over a large attendance of members and their wives, and was supported on the platform by Mr. Rylands and Messrs. J. Critchley (lion. secretary), C. Worthington (hon. treasurer), G. Monks (chairman of the bowling section), W. H. Phillips (secretary of the bowling section and organiser of the con- cert), T. Clare (captain), I. Newton, R. Tilley, W. Cottam, and J. Woods. A -capital musical programme was rendered by Bandsman Hughes, Mr. H. Edwards, Mr. J. Wrench, Mr. A. Wilde, Sergeant Leadbetter, Mr. T. Humphries, Mr. MacGregor, Mr. G. Gough, Mr. J. Hands, Mr. Prescott (recitation), and Mr. W. Wainwright and Mrs. Woods (accom- panists). The income from the concert was over £5, which will be handed over to the Prisoners of War Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the prize-winners:- Opening Main: 1, I. Molyneux; 2, W. Ball; 3, W. Roughley ; 4, H. Birchall ; 5, T. Humphreys; 6, Jack Woods; 7, L. Bate; 8, H. Potter; 9, T. Clare; 10, G. Harrop; 11, C. Hughes; 12, T. Barnes; 13, T. Newton; 14, A. Littlewood; 15, J. Darbyshire; 16, B. Hallows. Individual Merit Competition: 1, E. Black- ledge; 2, T. Humphreys (presented by Mr. Ti. Ashton); 3, J. Unsworth (presented by Mr. C. Hughes); 4,11. Langley; 5, J. Gleave; 6, J. Nev- ton; 7, H. Broadhurst; 8, S. Garnett; 9, J. Pitch- ford; 10, J. Warburton, son.; 11, J. Roberts; 1'..:, J. Darbyshire; 13, J. Woods; 14, C. Hughes; 15, W. H. Phillips; 16, J. Campbell. Inter-Department Competition. - Winners- Ropery : I. Molyneux, W. Chadwick, J. Curtiss, T. Platt, E. Gibbons, E. Gleave, T. Bibby, H. Appleton,. W. Carter, J. Bolesworth. Runners up-Church-street No. 2: E. Millington, J. War- burton, A. Woods, A. Watkins, T. Smith, C. Sandiford, L. Bate, T. Archer, J. Roberts, J. Tulberg. • Closing Main: 1, A. Jolley; 2, It. Maskett; 3, W. Stretford; 4, E. Gleave; 5, G. Monks; 6, S. B,roadhurst ; 7, J. Campbell; 8, W. Kerfoot; 9, E. Millington; 10, S. Massey; 11, J. Meakin; 12, H. Potter; 13, J. Woods, sen.; 14, W. Carter; 15, W. Cottam; 16, A. Watkins. .</p>
        <p>The prize; we're distributed during an interval in the musical programme, and on the motion of Mr. L. Bate, seconded by Mr. Wilkinson Haigh; a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Rylands. Mr. Bate said they hoped the club would make for itself as great a name among clubs as the firm enjoyed in the world of trade; while Mr. Haigh hoped that the day would come when Mr. Rylands would be member for War- rington. (Applause.) "We have been at vari- ance on political matters at other times," he said, "but we shall have to sink the old colours in the future and go for the best man. Ido not think Warrington could put up a better man than Mr. Rylands." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MR. RYLANDS ON THE WAR-AND AFTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Eylands, acknowleding the vote, 'said his only regret was that he could not visit them at their club more frequently, but his time was so much occupied that it was a matter of difficulty to fit in all his engagements. He shuddered to think what would be his position if he responded to the kind suggestion of Mr. Haigh that he should represent Warrington in the House of Commons. Wei were passing through dreadful times, and it became more and more necessary that those who weie working at home should Avail themselves of all the opportuni- ties there were for reasonable recreation and healthy outddor pursuits. He congratulated the club upon the way in which they were meet- ing that need and upon the excellent season they had had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Germany's Sinister Aims.</head>
        <p>Germany's Sinister Aims.</p>
        <p>German speakers, proceeded Mr. Rylands, were making continuous efforts to :make it appear that the war was thrust upon Ger- many. But we had abundant proof that Ger- many had for years been preparing for a great conflict; that she was envious of our position in the world; and that she believed we were effete and she could, and would, replace our greatness by what she was pleased to call her kultur. Thinking that Great Britain would remain outside the struggle, she sought a quarrel with Russia and France, believing that with the two latter out of the way the</p>
        <p>knell of isolated Britain would be sounded. The German idea was that we 'should be con- fined to our on borders—and we could im- agine what that would mean to a great indus- trial country like ours. If Germany had got possession of our Colonies nothing but German goods would have been allowed to enter. It was clear that had we stood aside and allowed the German programme to succeed the conse- quence* to us and our Empire would have been absolutely disastrous.. It was clearly our bounden duty to see this fight through at whatever cost—(loud applause)—because to make peace would only be to allow Germany to re-gather her resources to strike again at the first convenient opportunity. The Allies were resolute not to sheath the sword until such a success had been achieved as would make it certain that Germany would not be able again to disturb the peace of the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Burden To Be Faced.</head>
        <p>The Burden To Be Faced.</p>
        <p>After the war complex problems—concerning both the people and the governing classes-- would confront the nation demanding settle- ment. We hoped that as the issue of this war, and the result of wise judgment and ad- mibistration, we should be able to ensure for ourselves such a position in the commercial world as would enable us to bear the fearf.il financial burden which would be placed on our shoulders. We would have a national debt without question of something like 4.000 millions—a figure which simply staggered the imagination—and only prudent and wise policy would enable us so to conduct our manufac- turing activities as to stand that strain. Only by production could that huge burden be borne. We should also have to face social and labour problems. Everything was going to be different, and we should have to grapple with these questions in a way in which they had never been tackled before. He believed that this war, dreadful. as it is, and frightful as its consequences are, will do much to help our country, and to stimulate our race. (Hear, hear.) It will tend to make all of us look a little bit more outside ourselves. The nation as a whole, during the 20 years preceding the war, was inclined to get selfish. All classes were apt to. think solely of their own interests, not realising that they were members of a tremendous Empire organisation. He confi- dently believed that this war will lead us to appreciate the reality of things, to feel that there is something more than what exists in our immediate surroundings, and that we shall appreciate each other's point of view better. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was also passed to the artistes and to Mr. Phillips. on the proposition of Mr. W. Cottam, seconded by Mr. C. Worth- ington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Sunday.—Church Parade; fall-in at Head- quarters at 10 a.m. prompt; the Band will attend.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Band Practice at 8 p.m. prompt.</p>
        <p>Tuesday and Friday.—Fall-in at 7.30; Physi- cal and Company Drill; Signalling and Ambu- lance Classes.</p>
        <p>Orderlies.—Sergeant 11. Timmins and Cadet H. Orford.</p>
        <p>J. B. STARKEY, Commanding Officer.</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Major W. S. Cartwright has, after four years' service, tendered his resignation to Captain J. B. Starkey.</p>
        <p>Cause For Thankfulness. Margaret: "Have you seen Agnes' new baby ? It's a peach!" , Marion: "No, but I guess they're pretty glad it isn't a pair."</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>13.5. %. COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>13.5. %. COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. FAREHAM. A team of the Stockton Heath Platoon of the 6th (Knutsford) Battalion of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, were drawn against the "A" team of the Fareham (3rd Hants) Volunteer Regiment in this competition, which is organised by the SocietY Miniature Rifle Clubs, there being nearly four hul2- dred entries. The conditions are: Six men aside, 10 shots at a two-bull target, 5 on each, 11 inch black With a :I inch centre (10), possible score -100, at 25 yards, with open military sights and no slings. The colr- petition is on the knock-out principle, and as vall be seen the Stockton Heath Platoon on their are round with a margin of 25 points. Scores:— Fareham.—A. Woolridge 97, A. S. Gate 92. "c• Tatford 90, E. Edmunds 89, F. May 87, E. Solclawei 85; total 540. Stockton Heath.—A. Shepherd 97, M. A. Piei.r? 96, R. M. Swinton 95, J. F. Fergusson 95, J. Mcve-- mid 92. J. H. Fletcher 90; total 665.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COMPANY (7TH LANCS.) V. 67</p>
        <p>COMPANY (3RD NOTTS)•</p>
        <p>Warrington won by 42 points. Scores:— Warrington.—Sergt. Garside 90. Corpl. Wright Lce.-Corpl. Gorton 91. Pte. Allman 95. Pte. A. Hill 96. Pte. Hinde 96; total 560. • Notts.—Tansley 91. Clarke 90, Edsen 82, Burnharej 93, Woodlands 79, Harris 83; total 518.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>IVOULD BE UNEXPLAINABLE.</head>
        <p>IVOULD BE UNEXPLAINABLE.</p>
        <p>It Occurred In Earlestown And Doubt</p>
        <p>Couldn't Be.</p>
        <p>When so many in our own district have agej° and again given just the same assurances 10_4 could doubt of an Earlestown case be explaineu It could not. The very earnestness of the message in cluesi tion will convince the most sceptical. Lea every word and then pass judgment. On March 14th, 1911, Mrs. M. Haste, of 232 i Earle-street,!Earlestown, said :—"After my 1861 confinement kidney complaint crept upon me. gradually got worse, and about five years atgii, dropsy set in. My body filled with water ancti.. had swellings all over me. There were swe'A ings, too, under my eyes, and my back cause: me fearful pain. I could not think of beadle-It down. If I stooped the pain was unhearable,„lo seemed as if a sharp knife Was being driven ile't my back. I used to come over so dizzy tbat, I dared Pot-go out. My head ached fit to !Wei I went to an infirmary in Liverpool, •whe, was told I should have to undergo an operntle79 I refused, however, and started to use, Dee% Backache Kidney Pills, which I had be' advised to try. the "I noticed an improvement, almost from, to first, so I took the pills regularly, determinetho give them a fair trial. As I continued withrrhe medicine all my aches and pains left me. ',le swellings gradually disappeared and I becl normal again. Now I feel lam quite cured,,e's I can honestly say Doan's Pills have Tesibe new woman of me. I shall always praise ,s medicine and hope my experience with De? lint Pills Will induce others to try this exce remedy." (Signed) "M. Haste." later On February 16th, 1916—nearly five years -A- a. —Mrs. Haste said :—" I am keeping w°f,,-vi fully well at present. I shall never knew, for to fully express my gratitude to Doan's the welcome change they made in my F hea'''r. ostet, Of all dealers, or 2/9 a box, from McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-e.tirlaceep London, W. Don't ask for backache or Itt-4 pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Pills, the same as Mrs. Haste had. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>IS GOD DEAD?</head>
        <p>IS GOD DEAD?</p>
        <p>Striking Question Asked By Mr. R. Ellison Scores Further The Rev. T. T. James. Successes.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James preached at the even- ing service at Wycliffe Church last Sunday the first of a series of sermons on "God and the War," and spoke upon the question "Is God dead?" He said he had taken that title to draw attention, in a striking way, to the deep trial of faith through which many minds were pass- ing to-day. One of the things we were lament- ing in our Churches was the fact that the war had not restored a great many men and women who had bebome detached from religious organisations, and had not resulted in a re- ligious awakening, flooding our Churches with eager and anxious congregations. A type of question which was frequently asked is: "If God be indeed the God of Love whom we have learned to see in Jesus Christ, how is it that He allowed this war to begin, and why does He not stop it? If this world, with all its marvellous story, in which man has gained such immense victories over matter, in which he has learned not merely to sail the seas, but to navigate the air, not only to speak languages, but to send messages by wireless from one place to an- other, not only to use steam, but to harness lightning to his purposes: if all this is indeed God's world, how is it that He allows the finest products of human genius to be devoted to the ends of wanton destruction and inhuman savagery?" An All-Sufficient Answer.</p>
        <p>To his mind, the all-sufficient answer wo : God has made us free human beings. He has given us the power to do right or to do wrong. He has given us the power of choice between one course and another. Now He could very well. have made us otherwise; He could have made us so that it was impossible for us to do wrong, so that we were compelled always to do one thing, and one thing only, but it would have been at the cost of our manhood; we should have been machines instead of men. He could have Made evil impossible to us, so that we could never have welcomed it, only we should no longer have been free agents determining for ourselves, but mere automata, mere machinery,' working according to a will imposed upon us. Now the greatest gift God has bestowed upon YOU and me is just that power of choice between right and wrong, between good and evil, between false and true, and though it leads us often, when we make the wrong choice, by roads of. infinite un- happiness and misery, it is the one thing that makes this life of ours that intensely vivid and real thing which we feel it to be. And God does not prevent this war, and He does not stop it, beeause He will by no means interfere with that free choice that He has given us. He Will, not interfere with it from the outside. The only way in which He will stop wars, and stop them for ever, is when He is welcomed bv all His children in their hearts, and it becomes of their own free choice their delight to do His will."</p>
        <p>The full succes,ful results of last session's chemical examinations now being to 'hand, we are delighted to be able to chronicle that Mr. R. Ellison, technical chemist, of Messrs. J. Cros- field and Sons, has once more distinguished him- self, and added another list of achievements to his already brilliant, if short, academical career.</p>
        <p>Of the five examinations at which Mr. Ellison sat, five distinct successes have been allotted to him in the advanced grades of Inorganic, Phy- sical and Organic Chemistry (Board of Educa- tion), and in Honours Alkali Manufacture'(City and Guilds of London Institute). We cannot but regret a little that these honours won for the town should not have been gained through the educational centre here, but that Widnes should claim the coaching of this clever young chemist, who, it will be re- membered, through the same medium of train- ing, brought home last year the Bronze Medal, awarded for the first place in the kingdom for Alkali Manufacture (ordinary grade), the</p>
