      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Britain Not to Blame</head>
        <p>Britain Not to Blame</p>
        <p>"It is just because of this that wars happen in the world," said the reverend gentleman, because the hearts of men are by no means yet in line with the wily of God, because of their own free will men choose to go by ways con- trary to His purpose. And God permits them to be because He has made man a free and self- determining creature, and because He will by no means interfere with that independence. And when I say this, I do not by any means intend you to understand that I believe all nations to be equally guilty in this world tragedy. The. rights and wrongs of this war will be sorted out before the Great White Throne, and I am con- tent to leave them there. The great Anglican Church is holding a Mission of Repentance and Hope, and we shall wish for it great blessing to that Church and to the whole community. There are many sins of which we need to repent in England, and we want less and less of the Pharisaism which broods over its own righteous- ness. At the same time I want to say that under God I do not believe this war is something of which we as a nation need to repent. The re- sponsibility lies somewhere else. It is not on our consciences. I should repent.indeed if we had stood aside. We shall not let this war destroy our faith. God knows all about it. Out of liii its pain and, sorrow He will be able to work His purposes. He knows all about the sorrow and suffering and heartbreak. 'ln all our affliction He is afflicted, and the Angel of His Presence saves us.' God will stop all wars at last. It will be not by some interference from without; it will be through the Kingdom of Heaven within, through men getting their minds and wills in line with the mind and will of God."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MR. R. ELLISON</head>
        <p>MR. R. ELLISON</p>
        <p>Salters' Cos' Prize, and Other successes. It is remarkable that, even at the Widnes Centre this year, Mr. Ellison's achievements in techni- cal Chemistry far outnumber those of Any other student preSented, at the examinations, and he carries away eveiw prize offered by the Widnes Higher Education Committee in connection with these higher branches of chemical technology. His achievements, up to date, form a progres- sive and striking series, covering as they do such a varied field. They include certificates in Heat Engines, Practical Mathematics, Practical Inorganic Chemistry (Board of Education), the full gamut of U.L. and C.I. chemical work, Higher Theoretical, Inorganic, Physical and ,Organic Chemistry (Board or Education), and Alkali Manufacture, Ordinary Grade and Hon- ours (City and Guilds of London Institute). Considering the few years over which these successes have been spread, it is all the more remarkable to know that these same years have been crowded with such varied occupations, re- merabering, as we do, his chief tenor parts in Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons' operas, &amp;c., and •during all this his versatility has been turned to pleasing account in composing some ambitious and charming pianoforte music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SOON DETECTED.</head>
        <p>SOON DETECTED.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Mission League Of Help And "Sponging."</p>
        <p>In presenting his report at the annual meet- ing of the League of Help associated with the Bank Quay Working Men's Mission, the Secre- tary (Mr. J. Dutton) said that he did not know of any work which was greater than that of trying to help those who were placed in an un- fortunate position. The work of the League during the past year had been up to its usual standard, despite many drawbacks, and since its inauguration V2lB in grants had been paid out, in addition to gifts of clothing, bedding, Sze., the value of which for the past twelve months had been about £4o._</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the report, Mr. T. Dale spoke on the value of the work in general and of the good results which were to be seen in the Mission's district.</p>
        <p>The gathering was presided over by Councillor G. Dale; who, in the course of a short address, said he was glad to notice that the interest and enthusiasm among the members were as intense to day as they were when the League was first inaugurated. They had endeavoured to help to brighten the lives of those who were less for- tunate than themselves, and the testimony they had received to the value of their work for those who had been thus assisted had been a splendid stimulus. He felt that the touch.:of .real sym- pathy and a revelation of the fact that someone cared for them had been the means of lifting many unfortunate people from the verge of despair. In speaking upon the idea of the Church taking up philanthropic work, Mr. Dale said that no one had a greater claim upon the Church as his spiritual home and shelter from the storms of life than the poor man. It might be argued that the Church's generosity would be taken advantage of, but, while be admitted it might be so in a few cases, yet with. properly organised committees of investigation, people who were addicted to that kind of thing were soon detected. After speaking of the work ' as one carried out on undenominational lines; , the Chairman concluded by referring to the fact 'that one of the principal phases of such work was to be seen in the moral uplifting of the, com- munity,. which was to be seen in ..the. wider spheres covered by the Citizens' Guild of Help. They, as a Mission, were glad to be associated with such an organisation, and, while many suggested amalgamatidn, yet those who under- stood the position felt that co-operation only was preferable. . The election of officers resulted as follows President,' Councillor G. Dale; Vice-president, Mr. A. Tilling; Secretary and Treasurer, Mr: J. Dutton; Auditors, Messrs. R. Jones and T. Ball; Committee, representatives from various organisations in the 'Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FENNINGS'</head>
        <p>FENNINGS'</p>
        <p>BRILLIANT CHEMIST.</p>
        <p>4. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4.. .:. 4. 0 .:. • ..... .:. 4. 4. 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4. .:. 4. .:. .:. 4. 4:. 4. 4. .:. 4. .:,. ic. .:. .:. 4. 4, .:. i. .:. .:. .:. .:. ..) 4. • .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. *4. .:. .:. 4. ..:,, . 4, .:. .:. .:. .:. .:, .:. .:. 4 0 „:. .:. ,1. .:. 4. .:, .:. • .:. .:. .:. 0 + 0 .:. .:. .:. • . .:. .:.</p>
