      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>_ – IA M.:. IX'S WORLD. e.1.•..4*.</head>
        <p>_ - IA M.:. IX'S WORLD. e.1.•..4*.</p>
        <p>•i• +</p>
        <p>millinery, and very handsome it is in appear- ance; moreover, it is durable. Ostrich feathers are again relegated to a secondary position, in- deed the nodding plumes of a few seasons ago have completely vanished. Tiny ostrich feathers are still used, however, round the crowns of hats, although ospreys are preferred in that position. Veils have once more come to the front, and almost every hat is provided with one; on some of the small velvei toques the sole trimming consists of the veil. The yash- mak is the latest idea; it is very full, covers only a part of the face, and is more bizarre than becoming and graceful. The veil that is circular in shape and covers the whole hat is decidedly prettier than the yashmak. A curious new hat trimming consists of A certain species of sea moss, unknown in Europe, but growing freely in the United States,•where tha fashion began. Hats of the Second Empire are to have a vogue this winter.</p>
        <p>Preserving Fruit Without Syrup. Three different methods of preserving fruit in bottles without using syrup are practised at Battersea. The first is the simple method of the bottling machine with a thermometer attached; the second the oven process; and the third the use of a fish kettle. Fruit for bottling should be as far as possible of the same size, a little under ripe, free from stalks, and not punctured (if . cherry stalks are taken . out roughly the fruit breaks in the bottles) and then lightly packed, a wooden stick being used to press it. down. If the oven is used: the grass jar is filled, being slightly heated first The. fruit is then put in and left until it turns colour;. if ;the oven is too hot the fruit will burst at once. Boiling water should be poured' on immediately; the jar is taken out of the oven, and then filled up with a layer of mutton fat. The bottles should not be moved, or the fat will run down. Lard should not be used.</p>
        <p>The Nature of Ptomaine Poisoning. Ptomaines are poisons formed by the action of putrefactive germs upon the flesh. They are among the most deadly of all known substances. Many of them closely resemble the venom of poisonous snakes. These poisons are sometimes ready formed in the food, as in canned meats that are spoiled, and particularly in canned fish. Thy are also sometimes developed in cheese and in milk under special conditions giving rise to cheese poisoning and to ice cream poisoning. They are more frequently, however, produced in the intestines itself as the result of the action of putrefactive germs upon un- digested fragments of meat which are retained long in the colon. In the treatment of ptomaine poisoning the most important thing is to empty the alimentary canal as quickly as possible and to give the patient an abundance of water by mouth and by repeated enemas. The collapse may be combated by fomentations, hot and cold applications to the spine, and cold sponging. The most important thing, however, is to flood the tissues with water by the means suggested. —" Good Health."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Canadian Girls for the St. John Ambulance Brigade. .</p>
        <p>Sixty girls have lately come over from Canada to work as members of the St. John Ambulance Brigade. Thew. are volunteers, and have paid their travelling exPenses for seven thousand miles, in order to do their "' bit -" for the Empire. Sixty were wanted, but thrice that number came forward, a proof in itself of the enthusiasm across the Atlantic for the cause of the Allies. But, as the Canadians say, they are fighting for themselves as much as for the Motherlynd, Kultur and Prussian militarism, not to mention German frightfulness, do not appeal to the Dominions. These girls have all come from well-to-do families, and have already gone through a course of training in military hospitals at home. The age of the party ranges from twenty- three to thirty-eight, and each probationer must have sufficient private means to support her- self in case of necessity. The girls come from Montreal, Toronto, Ottawa, Fort Carey, Regina, London ,(Ontario), Victoria (8.C.), St. John, and Saskatoon. All are self-reliant, the greatr number are highly educated, and accomplished. Not a few are French-Canadians, who rank among the most loyal citizens of the British Empire. " Lend a Hand."</p>
        <p>The elder members of the Girl Guide organisa- tion have rendered yeoman service in hospitals as messengers, and in helping with the mend- ing, laundry work, and cooking. In order to raise five hundred pounds for a recreation hut in France, which the Guides determined to erect themselves, they gave all sorts of enter- tainments, darned,socks at a penny a pair, and collected newspapers for sale to butchers and tradesmen, and the necessary sum was raised. In addition, the girls send parcels to prisoners of war, flowers to hospitals, eggs to wounded soldiers, and help other people at all times. There is no copying of the military, either in dress or rules. The girls' uniform consists of a navy blue dress, felt hat, trefoil badge, and pale blue tie. " Madam " is the mode of address to captains. Each patrol is named after a flower, and has its leader, who must be nver fifteen, whilst each captain must be over twenty-one. A new movement called the "Brownie," has been started for the younger girls, who are not old enough to be Guides. "Lend a hand" is their motto, and each must, among other things, be able to plait her hair, and to knot her tie.</p>
        <p>Hats and Trimmings. Hatters' plush is to take pride of place over any other fabric for fashionable autumn</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>4.4)4••:•++, .:. ++.:..:.::. +4,4.4.4.4. 4.4. 4., .:. .:. .:. •I: Children' 8 Corner t:- • • • ,„ .:. • „...____ • ~:. • • • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. + .:. 4.•:••:—:—:••:••:•+4.4—:—:-:•+++4.4.4.4-4•44</head>
        <p>4.4)4••:•++, .:. ++.:..:.::. +4,4.4.4.4. 4.4. 4., .:. .:. .:. •I: Children' 8 Corner t:- • • • ,„ .:. • „...____ • ~:. • • • Conducted by UNCLE JACK. + .:. 4.•:••:—:—:••:••:•+4.4—:—:-:•+++4.4.4.4-4•44</p>
        <p>TRAFALGAR DAY.</p>
        <p>'My Dear Children,— I have had a large number of letters from you bubbling over with the true patriotic spirit respecting Trafalgar Day. There has been a great competition among the scholars at Evelyn-street, Hamilton-street and the Beamont Schools, and I have had great difficulty in de- ciding to whom I shall award the prize. Be- cause there were so many competitors like last week I have decided to give two prizes (equal) to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>877 Niece Lorna Reading,</head>
        <p>877 Niece Lorna Reading,</p>
        <p>27, St. Barnabas'-place, Lovely-lane, who at- tends Evelyn-street School; and 1019 Thomas Atherton, 50, Scott-street, who attend 4 Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>Several of the best essays dealt with the life of Nelson, and, of course, I asked you to write about why and how Trafalgar Day is celebrated. I suppose you read in "The Examiner" last week that . this year single Christmas puddings cannot be sent to our soldiers at the Front. The only way is to send donations to the Army Christmas Pudding Fund. In this fund £60,000 is being raised in order to send 3,000,000 puddings to the Front in big loads, which is the only way the Government will carry them. "The Examiner" has been asked to collect enougla money to send sufficient pud- dings to serve the South Lancashires on active service. Every sixpence given will supply one man, and there is no charge for carriage. • I shall give a prize this week for • Suggestions How To Raise Money For Christmas Puddings For The Gallant South Lancs. That is a nice competition, I am sure. Your loving, UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL ESSAYS.</head>
        <p>THE SUCCESSFUL ESSAYS.</p>
        <p>By Niece Lorna Reading' 26, St. Barnabas'- place; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School:— TRAFALGAR DAY—HOW WE CELEBRATE IT, AND WHY.</p>
        <p>"Trafalgar Day is the day on which the famous Lord Nelson died, the greatest sailor the world has ever known. It is called Trafal- gar Day in memory of the battle he fought and won, and in which he died. It is commemor- ated by all English sailors every year, and also by the scholars of the schools. The school- children write essays on Trafalgar Day when it draws near. The Union Jack is hoisted in every, town and village, mostly on high build- ings. Ships that are lying in. Portsmouth Harbour fire a shot, for Nelson's ship, the Victory, is there. - Everyone should honour Trafalgar Day, for it makes you think of the Battle of Trafalgar, which, ever since, England has been Mistress of the Seas.'"</p>
        <p>By., Nephew Thonias Atherton, 50, Scott- street, Hamilton-street School:— "October 21 is called Trafalgar Day because on October 21, 1805, Horatio Lord Nelson fought and won the battle of Cape Trafalgar, which saved England from destruction and disgrace. By the winning of this battle, England -won for herself the .title of ' Mistress of the. Seas.' For this we must be thankful not only to our Navy. of , that time, .but also to God, 'for; if at the present limas England had not been 'Mis- tress' of the Seas,' she would surely, have been crushed by such a powerful enemy as Germany. "We celebrate Trafalgar Day by hanging out flags and decorating the streets. Most 'of the schools in the United Kingdom have some kind of festival, which is generally half the day at singing patriotic songs. Nelson's memorial column, with its four stone lions, in London, is decorated. Special memorial services are held in St. Paul's Cathedral in honour of Lord Nelson."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>A great many other boys and girls have written on the subject. Most of the Writers draw at- tention to the call Nelson made to duty on the part of us all, and to the lasting eftect his example has had upon the nation. Below I have tried to give extracts from the letters to show some special fact or idea expressed by the writer quoted:—</p>
        <p>Nephew Wni. Warburton, 7, Heaton-street (Hamilton-street School).—" A sailor's duty at the present time is to follow Nelson's excellent example and also to obey his famous signal, 'England expects every man to do his duty.'" Niece Doris Gregory, 9, Mill-lane (Evelyn- street School).—" Some people think that but for Nelspn our Navy would never have been so powerful."</p>
        <p>Niece Eva Moores, 12, Cowdell-street (Bea- mono.--,-" Trafalgar Day is the anniversary of the death and victory of one of our greatest sailors, Lord Nelson, whose love and devotion</p>
        <p>for his country England will never forget; also his noble example and bravery, which make us understand what love and duty means." Nephew Geo. H. Bradshaw, 85, Birchall- street (Eamilton-street School).—" Nelson was a true Britisher, not knowing fear; he did his duty to the utmost."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Whittle, 123, Cartwright- street (St. Barnabas' School).—" Inland towns do not celebrate the anniversary of Trafalgar in the same way as seaport towns." Niece May Jones, 10, Chorley-street street School).—" 'England expects. every man to do his duty.' A good • motto in England's hour of need. It has'given many a man courage to fight for their King and country." •</p>
        <p>Niece Lily Gandy, 64, Dallam-lane (Wycliffe). —" We keep those wonderful words in memory of him to this day, and all true Britishers should act upon them."</p>
        <p>Nephew Peter Appleton, 23, Heaton-street (Silver-street School).—" Nelson's last words were, 'Thank God, I have done my duty.'" Nephew Colin Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street ,School), who is only nine years of age, writes a nice letter to say that wreaths are put round the great Nelson monument in London every year on the 21st of October. Com- petitions are held in the Public schools, and a shield is given for the best essay written on Trafalgar Day.</p>
        <p>Niece M. A. Lunny, 108 a, Bridge-street (Arp- ley-street School).—" Trafalgar Day is one of the greatest events in our history." Niece Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash-grove, Latch- ford (Bolton Council School).—" Nelson's body was brought to England and interred in St. Paul's CathedrAl on the9th January, 1806, where a monument is 'erected to his memory." Niece Ivy Odell, 1, Myrtle-grove, Latchford (Bolton Council School).—" It is a hundred and eleven years • since Nelson died, but he is not, and never will be, forgotten." Nephew Harold Dutton 4, Owen-street (Ham- ilton-street School).—" Nelson's famous ship, the 'Victory,' is still in the harbour at Portsmouth. It is a very big ship and made of wood." Niece Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel•street(Evelyn- street School).—" We celebrate Trafalgar Day because Nelson was the greatest seaman that England has ever had. He won many battles, but the greatest were the Nile, Copenhagen. and Trafalgar. It had been part of Nelson's prayer that the British Fleet might be distin- guished by humanity. Setting an example himself, he twice gave orders to cease firing on the ship called 'Redoubtable.' From this ship, which he had thus spared, he received his death."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Freaney, 107, Cartwright- street (Sacred Heart School).—" But for Ad- miral Nelson, Napoleon would probably have landed his troops on British shores. Nelson is one of England's greatest heroes and his vic- tory will be remembered as long as the world lasts."</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest Hughes, 37, Marsh House- lane (Beamont School).—" Nelson's great deeds will never .fade. Jack Cornwell was a second Nelson for his fame."</p>
        <p>Niece Beatrice Hughes, 37, Marsh House- lane (Beamont School).—"Nearly all the schools talk about Nelson on Trafalgar Day." Niece Doris Calvert, 47, Grafton-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Nearly every school child writes an essay on this great day. Nel- son's monument, in London, is decorated and the Lord Mayor and the great men of London show their respects. for Nelson by walking round the monument."</p>
        <p>Niece Doris Whittaker, 62, Baxter-street.— "They buried Lord Nelson in St. Paul's Cathe- dral, beside the Duke of Wellington." Nephew John H. Singleton, 24, Gandy-street (Hamilton-street School).—":jany people cele- brate Trafalgar Day by putting • a bit öt tri- coloured ribbon in their breast and singing patriotic songs." Nephew John Berry, 83, Algernon-street (Parochial Boys' School).—"The children at the schools have concerts and sing songs on Tra- falgar Day in memory of Nelson." Niece Margery Watkinson, 54, Lancaster- street (Evelyn-street School).—" Our greatest seaman, Nelson, fought and died in the Battle of Trafalgar. That is why .we celebrate the day." Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street (Parochial School).—" Some schools are 'lucky enough to have a treat in memory of the great fight." Niece Dorothy Cotton, 117, Liverpool-road. Niece Olga Brühl, 24, St. Barnabas'place (Evelyn-street School).—" Every ship at sea or in port remembers Trafalgar Day. The ships that are in Portsmouth Harbour fire a volley to celebrate the occasion." Niece Doris Payne, 86, Bramhall-street (Evelyn-street School).—" The monument of Nelson is decorated with wreaths and flowers, brought by the people in memory-of our great hero."</p>
        <p>Niece Amy Berry, 83, Algernon-street (Fair- field Girls' School).—" We celebrate Trafalgar Day in memory of the famous Admiral who gave the good command and did his duty well."</p>
        <p>Nephew Tom Sid d le, 70, Battersby-lane (Hamilton-street School).--" Every British</p>
        <p>boy's- mind should realise from Nelson's ex- ample that it is his duty to help his country." Niece Mabel Langley, 178, Padgate-lane (Bea- mont School).—" When Horatio Nelson was twelve years old he went to sea with his uncle and sailed away to the West Indies. From this time nearly all his life was spent on ships, and he took part in many sea fights. He lost an arm and one eye in these fights, and was made Lord Nelson by the King for his brave deeds."</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson.—" Nelson's old flagship, the 'Victory,' now lies in Portsmouth Harbour, fully rigged as when she last drOPped anchor. Naval court-martials are still held on board." Nephew Harold Cooper, 159, Cnniberland- street (Bolton Council School). 'Niece Ethel Shaw, Sunnyside, Chester New- road • (Bolton Council School).—" England ex- pects- every man this 'day to do his duty.' In the year 1895 England expected, and her expecta- tion was splendidly fulfilled. Again in 1914 she has fouhd• it necessary to call her .gallant sons to arms once more. They have gone forth Cour- ageously in the spirit Nelson bore when he went into battle, fearing nothing, feeling certain that victory would be ours."</p>
        <p>Niece Bessie Bandies, 76, Cartwright-street ;Evelyn-street).</p>
        <p>Nephew Frank Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane (Hamilton-street School).—" Every British boy should honour Nelson's peerless name, because he kept us safe." Niece Florrie Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane. Nephew Joseph Spencer, 139, Laira-street.— "In Warrington the Town Hall hoisted her flag up to the top of the mast. It was a wonder that the place was not decorated with flags." Nephew Jas. Darbyshire, 35, Tilley-street (Hamilton-street School).—" Nelson's monu- ment was decorated with wreaths 15 feet long, one of them being from our great d?,..dmiral, Jellicoe."</p>
        <p>Nephew Chas. L. Geddes, 51, Laira-street (Hamilton-street School).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>1042 James Hill, 55, Chorley-street ; Hamilton- street School. 1043 Percy Gregory, 105, Orford-lane; Hamil- ton-street School. 1044 William Bibby, 21, Scott-street; Hamil- ton-street School. _ . .</p>
        <p>1045 Harold Britton, 69, Birchall-street; Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>1046 William Taylor, 90, Battersby-lane; age 13; Hamilton-street School. 1047 Frank Holmes, 1,. Hamilton-street; age 12; Hamilton-street School. 1048 Ada Shipley, 67, Cartwright-street. 1049 Harold Cooper, 159, Cumberland-street; age 11; Bolton Council School. 1050 Emmaline Spencer, 58, Winwick-street; age 12; Silver-street Schoch. 1051 Ellen Taylor, 7, Neston-street; age 12; Silver-street School.</p>
        <p>1052 Nellie O'Brien, 22.5, Winwick-road; age 13; St. Benedict's School. . 1053 Ruth James, 30, Oxford-street, Latch- ford: age 10; Bolton Council School. 1054 E. Blackledge, 111, Forster-street; age 12; Hamilton-street Boys' School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LAST WEEK'S PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's three prizes appear to have given great satisfaction to those who received them. I have received the following acknow- ledgments:— " 74, Lythgoes-lane, Warrington. "Dear Uncle • Jack,—On Saturday morning when I opened 'The Examiner' I was surprised to find I had a prize. Your book, which I re- ceived at noon, is very interesting as far as 1 have read. I heartily thank you, and I will keep it clean.—l remain, your loving nephew, FRANK OVEREND, Hamilton-street School."</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—Just a few lines to thank you for the lovely book you sent me. lam pleased to. say I have read a lot of it, and it is such a nice prize., I hope I shall win .again. —With lots of thanks, from your loving niece, AMY BERRY', Fairfield Girls' School. "83, Algernon-street, Warrington."</p>
        <p>"123, Cartwright-street. . " Dear Uncle Jack,—Thank you verY much for the beautiful book you sent me on Saturday morning as a prize for the drawing I sent ,you. I do feel proud of my three books.—Your loving nephew, HAROLD WHITTLE, St. Barnabas' School."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DUTY AND INCLINATION.</head>
        <p>DUTY AND INCLINATION.</p>
        <p>"Stay at home," said Inclination. "Let the errand wait"; "Go at once," said Duty sternly, "Or you'll be too late."</p>
        <p>"But it snows," said Inclination, " And the wind is keen"; " NeFer mind all that," said Duty, "Go and brave it, Jean."</p>
        <p>Jean stepped out into the garden, Looked up in the sky; Clouded, shrouded, dreary, sunless— Snow unceasingly.</p>
        <p>"Stay," again said Inclination; "Go," said Duty. "go." Forth Went Jean with no more waiting, Forth into the snow.</p>
        <p>You will smile if now I tell you That this quiet strife, Duty conquering Inc4nation Strengthened all her life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>NATIONAL MISSION.</p>
        <p>Call To The People To Change Their</p>
        <p>Attitude Towards Life.</p>
        <p>This week several of the parishes in the Winwick Rural Deanery haVe been holding services in connection with the National Mis- sion, and success is reported generally. The Revs. S. F. Leadley Brown, C. R. Lead- ley Brown, and T. R. Musgrave have been the Bishop's Messengers at the Parish Church and services have been held on two or three occa- sions during each day. On Sunday evening the Rev. S. F. Leadley Brown strongly denied that the leaders of the Church were calling upon the nation to repent its action in enter- ing into the war. That was an imriression which had gained ground in some quarters, he said, but nothing could be farther from the truth, for we were proud we had taken such a stand. At the same time we must admit that there was much in the national and individual life that called for repentance.</p>
        <p>At St. Paul's the Mission opened on Wed- nesday of last week, when the Bishop's Mes- senger (the Rev. C. R. Slee; of St. Thomas', Wigan) delivered an impressive address. The services were continued on Saturday afternoon, when he spoke to communicants and church- workers. On the Sunday there was a celebra- tion of the Holy Communion at eight o'clock. At the morning service the Bishop's Messenger explained that repentance was simply a chang- ing of the mind leading to a change of con- duct and outlook. He illustrated this in a graphic way from the war by pointing out that we were continually repenting of previous convictions and also developing afresh our organisations for victory. That was the kind of repentance to which the Church called us; repentance must be individual and social, and IChristianity must be applied to the whole of our national life. On Monday afternoon there was a service for women, and in the evening the Bishop's Messenger dealt with the problem of suffering in the light of the Cross of Christ. The Bishop's Messenger at St. James' Church was the Rev. U. Chorlton, of Higher Bebi'ngton. On Friday afternoon there was an address to women on "Home Influence," and there were good congregations at both services on Sunday. At St. Barnabas' Church, in addition to the 10 o'clock service on Sunday, when the Bishop's Messenger (the Rev. G. R. Oakley, vicar of Dearnley, Rochdale) appealed for help for coming Mission work, there was a children's service in the afternoon, followed by a service for Men and lads. At the latter the Messenger spoke on "Purity." The mission service was splendidly attended. Mr. Herbert Iddon was the organist. The Rev. J. Ryle Jones, Vicar of Great San- key, preached. at both services in St. Peter's Church. Mr. Little addressed the combined Bible Classes in the afternoon, and Miss Bessie Dutton was the speaker at a service for women on the following eveninl. On Tuesday even- ing Captain Holland spoke to the children, and other services vere held during the week. At Orford Church the Vicar (the Rev. G. Vale Owen) delivered fervent and impressive addresses. He spoke of the reconstruction of the Empire on sober and Christian lines and urged the adoption of definite religious in- struction in day schools.</p>
        <p>At Thelwall the Bishop's Messenger (the Rev. H. Howson, 'vicar of Newton Moor, Hyde, near Manchester) addressed large congrega- tions. The services commenced on Saturday evening and concluded on Tuesday morning with a Celebration of the Holy Communion. They included an address to women and a service for children, and were most devotional throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>GERMANY.</head>
        <p>GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Cruel, apostate nation drunk with pride. Yet posing as a model, and a guide; To lands of nobler mould ; Lost is the Christ for whom thy Luther fought, Lost is the chivalry thy Schiller taught, Lost in the lust of gold. ,</p>
        <p>Tear down the eagle from thy palace walls, And place there in its stead a thing which - crawls, A scorpion girt by fire; Behold the circle lessening as it glows, Until. the flames around their victim close: The world's avenging ire. R.P.D., in "-f- Great Thoughts."</p>
        <p>Of all the riches that we hug, of all the pleasures we enjoy, we can carry no more out of this world than out of a dream—.Bonnell.</p>
      </div>
