      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER S. 1916</p>
        <p>OUR FIRM RESOLVE.</p>
        <p>Viscount GREY, in a very notable speech at a luncheon of the Foreign Press Association, emphasised the importance of our " recollect- ing, recalling, and never for a -moment for- getting, what was the real cause of the War." He pointed out that the conditions of peace are vitally affected by the conditions which produced war. The Allies cannot consent to lay down their arms until they have obtained absolutely secure and impregnable safeguards and guarantees against the possibility of a war over again, being forced upon Europe by Prussian militarism. It is well, too, that we should have recalled to us from time to time the causes of the war, the unprecedented "frightfulness " with • which it has been waged on the side of the Central Powers, and the supreme importance of the objects for which we are fighting, because the efforts and the sacrifices which are yet necessary for the realisation of these objects are far greater than all but very few, if any, of us could have foreseen in the earlier periods. As Viscount GREY has said, it may seem hard that the present generation in its prime should be called to sacrifice so much, for the sake of the generations that come :after, but unless we carry on the war to the complete overthrown!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>of Prussian militarism, and the establishment of security for the future peace of the whole of Europe, .all our sacrifices will have been made in vain.</p>
        <p>The origin of the war can be told and ex- plained in a single sentence. "It was the story of 1870 over again." The British Empire and all the Allies are resolved that the Pruisian policy, which in 1914 was just what it was in 1870, shall never have an oppor- tunity of playing its sinister game again. All the. preparations for war had been advanced in Berlin to a stage beyond that of any other country, and then, when the chosen Imoment came, a manceuvre was made to provoke some other country (in this case Russia) to take a defensive step, and that defensive step was then tafien for the pretext of an ultimatum which. made war inevitable.</p>
        <p>Not onty has Germany proved herself once more the. great -enemy to ,the world's peace, but she has introduced into modern Warfare 'methods which, until she exploited them, civilised nations would have deemed in- Credible:. ' The -sinking of neufral ships, the use of poisonous gas, the indiscriminate destrUction of great cities by high explosives, the 'introduction of disease, the, barbarous ill- treatment of prisoners and of the inhabitants of Conquered districts, the deliberate attempt —tolerated, if not incited by Germany—to ex- 'terminate the Christian population of Turkey —all these things .brand Prussian milifarism as " the great anarchist who has let loose on the world a , greater and a more terrible anarchy :than was ever dreamt of by any indi- vidual anarchist."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>None of those,who saw an aeroplane pass over Warrington on Saturday afternoon realised that the observer in it was Second Air Mechanic John- Fairhurst, son of Mrs. Fairhurst, of 32, Kniitsford-road. He is now home on, leave, talking of how Warrington looks from aloft. I may add that Fairhurst took his first flight with Lieutenant Power, who was killed a few months ago in an aerial expedition.</p>
        <p>Private Banner, who has won a Military Medal, is the father of eight children. Regard- ing him the Germans may sing the "Him of Eight !"</p>
        <p>The Warrington Football Committee, always anxious to do good to the game, have hit upon a very practical way of furthering "the cause?'</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the "gate" at Wilderspool would number between 2,000 and 3.000. and I heard several gentlemen from St. , Helens say' that if they could only get so many spectators at three matches combined they would be able to carry on,</p>
        <p>I was sorry for the envious eyes they cast upon what, after all, was only a moderate affair, as "gates" go at Warrington. But they will doubtless find consolation in a proposal that the Warrington Committee have made.</p>
        <p>The. Warrington Committee met on Tuesday night and decided to pay St. Helens their day's expenses out of the takings on Saturday. Of course, the disappearance of St. Helens will do away with the necessity (and the expense) of Warrington travelling to play the away return match.</p>
        <p>• But they have gone a step further than this and offered to entertain St. Helens on the re- turn date at Wilderspool; and, if the offer is accepted, they *ill divide the "gate" with them. They: propose .;:that other clubs with , -fixtures should treat St. 'Helens in like manner, and I am 'glad -to. notice- that ',Swinton have decided to pay St. Helens' expenses to-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>If the scheme works, St. Helens will be able to weather this season without havink to face the financial burden they have wisely refused to carry. The only drawback would be in respect of the home spectators. All the matches would be away. But there are no home spec- tators this year; otherwise there would be no as to going on as usi.ll.</p>
        <p>A barber: was steering round a pimple on my chin on 'Monday, when he broke the news that henceforth my shaves woula cost me twopence instead of threehalfpence. I thought of the precarious position of the pimple and pro- tested as pleasantly as a phlegmatic personality Permitted.</p>
        <p>His excuses eventually easily evoked the extra halfpenny. "It's this way, you see," he said (a barber always Says, ," You see" when he has bunged your eyes up with lather) "Warrington was about the only place where you could get a .shave for threehalfpence. We should have liked it to remain so, but the cost of living necessitates our charging more. We have been badly hit by the war. The young men who used to come daily have gone to the Front, and the middle-aged and old men left are not nearly so' keen on their personal appearance. To them a few . days' growth on their chins is not a stigma; they /have " bearded " their girls long ago.</p>
        <p>one time we tholight that as munition workers are getting more money they would shave more regularly, but they don't. They cling to the one and only Saturday night shave '—and, in these days, threehalfpence isn't enough for ploughing' ;through the over- growth. ,It drives one's , razors to despera- tion." "</p>
        <p>Another innovation of the hairdressers this week has been the introduction of a dinner hour from 11.30. No one grudges them what every other tradesman• has himself, and takes with benefit. On Saturdays the dinner hour is not to be enjoyed, for then they are too busy. I know ,one who usually starts on his "mid-day meal" at 10.30 and often, through a continual stream of customers, does not conclude it until after two o'clock. I have advised him to try chewing gum.</p>
        <p>Colonel Brereton Fairclough has completely recovered from his injuries and returned to his command at the Front.- 'Major Max Woods is over on sick leave. Lieut. Ingram went back on Thursday.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Warhurst, :lady superintendent of the Nursing Division of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, writeg me:— , ".:You ask .for.-news cif any :ambulance worker on duty. I myself have been .nursing at the Ist Southern Hospital, Birmingham, .where we have accommodation—for-2,000 patients, Since July 12th. Other .nurses belonging to our War- rington Division and the hospitals at which they are on duty are :—Miss Burgess, Devon, port Military -Hospital; Mrs.. Kempton, Rock Ferry; Miss—Banning,. Wallasey; others are at Whitecross Hospital, Warrington. "Several who volunteered were unable to accept the call because they were school teachers."</p>
        <p>My deare,t friend is a Special Constable and, having been told that the Zepp " buzzer " had gone, set out warning the whole people in hi; district to lower their lights. On his arrival at the Police Station he learned that ,the hooter had sounded for a small fire on the 'other side of the river, and it was in a spirit of embarrass- ment that he narrated his story of alarums. The Sergeant in charge said, "Well done!" Not a pattern for others to follow!</p>
        <p>Other than dressmakers will be interested to know that the joke is modelled on " Weldon's Patterns."</p>
        <p>In The Casualties: After six years' devotion to the making of fully-charged shells, a Blue Andelusian hen passed away at the Drill Irall on Thursday. It is certified as having died of old age, and has been given to the -ferrets. The suggestion that the feathers might augment those of the Prince of Wales *as promptly negatiied, as was also 'the idea thatthey shOuitt be distribued among the loCat lads to•sleep ,upon at the Front. Feather beds in the trenches!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>XMAS PUDDINGS.</head>
        <p>XMAS PUDDINGS.</p>
        <p>For Your Boys On The Fighting</p>
        <p>Fronts.</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" FUND.</p>
        <p>The Christmas Pudding Fund for the soldiers at the Front, whieh we opened last week, has had a fair start, for to-day we announce donations amounting to over As the scheme becomes better known money will, we doubt not, flow in plentifullY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>POINTS TO BE REMEMBERED.</head>
        <p>POINTS TO BE REMEMBERED.</p>
        <p>' The War Office will not guarantee the de- livery of Christmas Puddings sent to indivi- dual soldiers abroad. _ _</p>
        <p>They have asked the Army Christmas Pud- ding Fund to provide all the:necessary pud- dings in bulk for convenience' sake. They will guarantee delivery in this case. -u The Examiner" is the ,authorised local Antre to carry out this work. You 'could not send the smallest parcel to the Front for less than One Shilling in postage alone. That 1/- would pay for two puddings under our scheme. Unless you subscribe to our Fund your boys will stand to go without their Christmas Pudding, or they will have their Christmas Pudding through the generosity of the people of some other part of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>BATTALIONS RESERVED.</head>
        <p>BATTALIONS RESERVED.</p>
        <p>We have received a letter from the Secretary of the Army Christmas Pudding Fund, London, thanking us for acting as a local -centre for Warrington, and Saying they have reserved for us the "Battalions we have named. We want £2l for each of those Battalions, and we want it quickly, as Christmas is rapidly approaching.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SUPPORT FROM THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>SUPPORT FROM THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter 'Peacock) writes to the Editor "Mayor's 'Parlour, Town Hall, 'Warrington, October 25th, 1916.... • "I enclose you cheque A. 2 2s. towards your Christmas Pudding: Fund. "I have no doubt your appeal will be suc- cessfultnotwithstanding.the .many other calls.</p>
        <p>" Yours -faithfully. "PETER PEACOCK."</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY HELP,</p>
        <p>The " Prince of Wales" Lodge and the "Olive Branch" Lodge of the Independent Order of --Oddfellows (Matiehester unity) have each sent a guinea to the fund, an example that will, we hope, be followed by other similar organi,ntions.</p>
        <p>GIFT FROM PLYMOUTH. Private' Walter Paget, S.B.R. Mess, R.N. Tio- pital, Plymouth, writes:— " Dear Sir,—Enclosed pleas 2 find-- small dona- tion towards your Army Christmas, Pudding Fund from a Warrington reader at present sta- tioned at the Naval Hospital, Plymouth. Wish- ing the fund every success." It should be placed on record that. the first reader to contribute was Mr. B. Farrar, of ,Wiliis-street, who brought his contribution to "The Examiner" Office last Saturday morning soon after we had opened for the business of the day. . _</p>
        <p>Like other offices in town our staff is d.Tleted. and we cannot canvas for donations. The cause is its own t•anvasser.</p>
        <p>DONATIONS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>d. s. The Mayor (Alderman I'. Peacock) 2 2 0 Mr. C. E. Parker 1 1 0</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur CrOsfield</p>
        <p>"The Examiner" "Prince of Wales" Lodge, 1.0.0 (M. 17.</p>
        <p>1 1 0</p>
        <p>"61-i-v-e-Branch" Lodge, 1.0.0. ()Lt.) 1 1 ,</p>
        <p>iMr. B. Farrar</p>
        <p>0 4 .0</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Clayton 5/- each:— Private Walter Paget, ,Mrs. Mrs. Mason, T. Weston, H. 80nd... 1 5 0 2/6 each : R.C.L., H. Teare, C. Dakin, J. H.</p>
        <p>, Walker Mrs. Allen</p>
        <p>1/- each:— Mrs. W. P. Ashton, Miss Gillebraml, R.W.S., T. Frain, J. Garratt, J. B. Jackson, P. Hewitt, M.Satterthwaite, W. H. Nichols, W. Old, M. Knox, R Motley, H. Jones, A. Hampson 0 14 0 6d. each : D. 11. J., 11.. A.D., K., W.J 0 3 6 J.G.</p>
        <p>Total to date £l4 7 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WOOD'S COLLEGE APPOINT-</head>
        <p>WOOD'S COLLEGE APPOINT-</p>
        <p>MENTS.</p>
        <p>Appreciative Testimonials Copied From The Originals By The Proprietors Of This Paper.</p>
        <p>21st October, 1916, Hood-lane, Sankey. Dear Mr. Wood,—l am pleased to inform you that I have taken up an appointment with a large business house in the district. I shall, needless to say, always recommend "Wood's College" wherever I go. Thanking you for the efficient instruction and attention I have received.—Yours sincerely. 19th October, 1916, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Wood,—l write to inform you that I was successful in securing an appointment at a commencing salary of £1 per week with the firm to whom you sent me. The tuition im- parted in the• Emergency Course at Wood's College is most satisfactory, and I feel that I have greatly benefited from taking up the,. training.—Yours, &amp;c., 19th October, 1916, Frodsham.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr., Wood,—You will be pleased to hear that I have secured the position to which you recommended me at a commencing salary of 20s. per week. I think that the tuition obtained at Wood's College is excellent in every respect. I shall always recommend the College to my friends.—Yours si`ncerely. Daresbury, 16th October, 1916. Dear Mr. Wood,—You will be interested to know that as a result of, ray interview I have been offered and have accepted a clerical posi- tion wish a firm in the district at a good com- mencing salary. I feel that I owe to yourself and staff many thanks for excellent coaching, and should occasion arise I Shall be only too pleased to recommend your College. Again thanking you, and with kind regards.—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>PHILLIPS--WALL I \ GTON.</p>
        <p>A pretty though quiet wedding was solemnised at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Tina May morning, when Mr., Sidney Phil- lipS, Of the Home Office, 'Liverpool, was Mar- ried to Miss Annie Wallington, daughter' of Mr. .George Wallington, of Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath; The Rev. T. T. James con- 'ducted the ceremony, Mr. W. H. Payton, at the organ; playing • several appropriate selections. The bride was given away by her father, her brother, Mr. Thomas Wallington, acting as best man. The bride was tastefully attired in a bottle-green costume, trimmed with skunk fur. Her black hat contrasted effectively. The honeymoon is being spent at Llandudno.</p>
        <p>The poor box in Warrington Police Court on Tuesday amply justified the saying that " 'tis an ill-wind that blows nobody good." Two motorists who came from Eccles and Altrincham respectively, were summoned for showing head lights and not showing licences.</p>
        <p>It was proved to the magistrates' satisfac- tion that the offences were due more to ignor- ance than design, and the Chairman (Coun- cillor Henry Roberts) said no conviction would be recorded if defendants paid the costs and each subscribed El to the poor box.</p>
        <p>The donations were given, but it is not known if the. phrase concerning "a cheerful giver" could be applied to the transaction.</p>
        <p>The coldest day last week was on Saturday, when only 34 degrees were registered. The volicemirn-uttbfasket Gate.' agreed -with, me Ahat constables should carry Thermos flasks instead of truncheons.</p>
        <p>VIGILANT,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
