        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>Many Other Distinctions for Warrington</p>
        <p>Men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>OTHERS TO FOLLOW.</head>
        <p>OTHERS TO FOLLOW.</p>
        <p>We are authoritatively informed that the fighting in France since July Ist has brought to the front many local heroes, end that the cases which are dealt with in this artiele are not by .any Means the only ones that are to be officially announced. Warrington will look forward in 'anxious- pride to the publication of these further proofs that Warrington men are ever to the fore when honour calls.</p>
        <p>qua FIRST V.C.</p>
        <p>WAR OFFICE, October 26th.</p>
        <p>His Majesty the King has been graciously pleased to award the Victoria Cross to the undermentioned :</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Gabriel George Coury, South Lancashire Regiment, For most conspicuous bravery. During an advance he was in command of two platoons ordered to dig a communication trench from the old firing line to the position won. By his fine example and 'utter contempt of</p>
        <p>MILITARY MEDAL.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE T. MULLIN.</p>
        <p>Threw Burning Bombs Away From</p>
        <p>A Trench.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Mullin, stretcher-bearer, R.A.M.C., recently wrote home ,to his wife, who with her six children resides at 41, Hoyle-street, stating' that he had been in action again ad was very nleased to say he had "come out all right." He was then having a rest. "We had," he stated, "a terrible experience a few nights ago. We were sent to bring in a wounded man and we took the wrong road. . . . We suddenly discovered a box of bombs on fire at one end of the trench. I got hold of it and</p>
        <p>very glad to see me bad( again. I am very pleased to get such an honour." A product of the old ,Volunteers, Private Banner is 42 years of age, and before re- enlisting, immediately the war broke out, was in the employ of Mews. H. D. Pochin and Company, chemical manufacturers. For many years previously, however, he was "puddling" at the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. Ltd. He crossed to France in February, 1915, and was wounded in the neck during the charge at Hooge.-</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL R. STEPHENS.</p>
        <p>Military Medal For Conspicuous</p>
        <p>Bravery When Wounded.</p>
        <p>The little community of Stephenses who re- side in various parts of .Bewsey have this week been the recipients of congratulations owing to the fact that a relative, Lance-Corporal Richard Stephens, 84, Hoyle-street, has been awarded the Military Medal. The gallant N.C.0., who is in the South Lancashire Regiment, was the last one moving up a trench with, a Lewis gun,, when a shell burst and wounded him. Word was passesl ,along asking if anyone was struck, s and Stephens - sent back a message "All right," and struggled "along with his gun. Later he was found to be wounded, and, when asked why. he had sent a wrong message he replied: "I didn't want to upset the others." Happily, after a few weeks, this fine soldier —a soldier to the marrow—was able to return to his battalion. While in hospital he re- ceived a letter from his officer (Mr. Dickson) stating: "Just a line to say how very much</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LIE-UT. G. G. A. COURY, V.C.</head>
        <p>LIE-UT. G. G. A. COURY, V.C.</p>
        <p>clanger he kept up the spirits of his men and completed his task- under intense fire. Later, after his battalion had suffered severe edivalties and the Commanding Officer of „another unit had been wounded, he went out in front of the advanced position in broad daylight and in full view of the enemy, found the Commanding Officer, and brought him back to the new advanced trench over • ground swept by machine-gun fire. He net -Only carried out his original task, and saved the Commanding Officer, but also assisted in rallying the attacking troops. when they were shaken and in leading them forward.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Cpury, who is only twenty years of age, is the, .Son of • the late Mr. 'Raphael Coury, formerly a well-known Liverpool Cotton merchant, and of Mrs. CouiT, 43, Canning- street", Liverpool, and more 'recently !of Waterloo. Although a native of Liverpool, and although .he originally enlisted in the, King's :Liverpool Regiment, he • -.obtained a commission in 'the South Lancashire Regiment and all his active soldiering, covering a period of twelve months, has been done alongside local men, and latterly in the 'Flying Corps. Lieutenant Coury; V.C., was educated at Blackburn and .Stonyhurst College, and is the fourth Stonyhurst boy to win the V.C. He distininished' himself in all kinds of sports at College, winning various. trophies, and is spoken of as one of the most' popular pupils of the school. Recently he was transferred from the South-, Lancashire Regiment to the Royal Flying- Corps, and has since made a number of daring flights over the enemy lines. On previous occasions he had distinguished ! himself.' by his bravery and coolness while lunder lire, and had earned the commendation lof the Commanding Officer of his battalion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>V.C. FOR BISHOP'S SON.</head>
        <p>V.C. FOR BISHOP'S SON.</p>
        <p>Captain Noel Godfrey Chavasse, MC., M. 9., R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>'Poi. most. cOnspicuous bravery and devo- tion to duty. During an attack he tended the wounded in the open all day,- under heavy fire, frequently in view of the enemy. Dur- ing the ensuing night he searched for wounded on the ground in front of the enemy's lines for four hours. Next :,day he . took one stretcher-bearer to the advanced •trenches, and. under heavy shell-fire carried an urgent .case for 500 yards into safety, being wounded in the side by a shell splinter during the jOurney. The same 'night he took' up a party of twenty volun- teers, rescued three wounded mew from a shell hole twenty-five yards from the enemy's trench, .buried, the, bodies of two officers, and collected many ,identity discs, although ;fired, • on by' bombs and Machine guns. Altogether he saved the lives Of some twenty badly- wounded men, besides the. ordinary cases, which passed through his hands. His .age and self-sacrifice were beyond praise. Captain Noel Chavasse is the second son of the Bishop of Liverpool, and is attached as surgeon to a battalion of the Liverpool Regi- ment. He gained the Military Cross last year for conspicuous service in - -colleeting the wounded after the charge at Hooge. He is 31 years" of age. Captain Chavasse was ;attached to the O.T.C. at Oxford, and -after taking 'up an appointment as house physician and sur- geon at the Southern Hospital, Liverpool, in 1912, was 'attached ;:as surgeon to the Liver- pools. He will shortly. complete, two years' service at the Front. His brother has been to Fiance as chaplain and is now a divisional chaplain in this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN PRI\ ITE CAPTURES 102 ILIIIIINS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PRI\ ITE CAPTURES 102 ILIIIIINS.</p>
        <p>No. 11000 Private Thomas Alfred , ,Jones, Cheshire Regiment. For most conspicuous , bravery. He was with ins company consolidating the defences in front'of a village,, 'and, 'noticing an enemy • sniper at 200 yards' distance, he went out, and, though .'one bullet 'went through his ,helmet and another through his coat, he , returned the sniper's' fire and killed him. He then saw two more 'of the enemy ..firing at him, although displaying' a White flag:Both of these he also shot. On reaching the enemy trench he found several occupied dug-outs, and, single-handed, disarmed 102 of the enemy, including three or four officers, and marched them back to our lines through a heavy barrage. He had been warned of the misuse of the white flag by the, enemy, but insisted on goini, out after them. Private Xhomas°Alfred Jones, Cheshire Regi- ment, of Princess-street. Runcorn, who has also been awarded the, Victoria Cross, is the son- of a Wesleyan local preacher and a mem- ber of a well-known Runcorn family.. He is 36 years of age and a bachelor. He is the first 'member of the Cheshire Regiment to win the Victoria Cross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>OTHER DISTINCTIONS.</head>
        <p>OTHER DISTINCTIONS.</p>
        <p>Other distinctions gained lately by local men in- clude one by a Warringtonian named Clare, who has been awarded the Military Medal for the part he took in assisting ?rivate Taylor, who recently won the D.C.M. for bringing in a wounded. officer. We should be glad if Clare's relatives would communicate with us. ,</p>
        <p>_ Compsatr-Sergermf-Major Wheeler, who has won thealedal of St. George Igt°Class-and the D.C.M.. is a Colchester man, although in the South Lancs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>raraUnititivnte</head>
