        <p>A.B.C. Railway Guide.</p>
        <p>The Warrington A.B.C. Railway Guide for November will be published at noon on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>A Lucky Thirteen.</p>
        <p>, The ekees§ of births over deaths in the borough last week was thirteen, there having been twenty-five born and twelve died. For the corresponding week of last year, forty-one w?re born and eighteen died.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Last Post—Eight O'clock.</head>
        <p>Last Post—Eight O'clock.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the -reduction in staff due, to the war, it is found necessary to close the letter box at the Head Office at 8 p.in. finally for the night mail._ _</p>
        <p>The alteration will come into force next Men, day.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festivals.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held on Sunday at Elmwood-avenue Church and the Railway Mission, Bank Quay. At the Railway Mission Miss Bogstrom and Mrs. A. Smith rendered duets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Mr. Fevt's Memorial Service.</head>
        <p>Mr. Fevt's Memorial Service.</p>
        <p>At the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening a memorial service in honour of the late Mr. Benjamin Few will be conducted by the Pastor, the Rev. H. Ogden. Favourite hymns of Mr. Few's Will be sung at the service.</p>
        <p>Another Advance in Price of Bread. The Warrington Equitable Industrial Co- operative Society, Ld., announce that, owing to further advances in cost of flour, the price of bread will be advanced one penny per 41b. loaf on and after Monday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Foreign Missionary Anniyersary.</head>
        <p>Foreign Missionary Anniyersary.</p>
        <p>Foreign—missionary sernions will be preached at the liewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow by the Rev. F. Hudson. Mr. T. B. Duckworth will preside Over a meeting to be held on Mon- day evening, when the Rev. F. Hudson will speak.</p>
        <p>Golborne Street'Baptist_Sale of Work:</p>
        <p>Next Thursday and saturday the postponed sale of work in connection with Golborne- s.treet Baptist Church will be held. Friends sincerely trust that, owing to the tragic neces- sity of postponement, the event will in nowise suffer. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Sales by Auction.</head>
        <p>Sales by Auction.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon Mr. Herbert Johnson will sell by auction the Gee-street stables, Winwick-road. Particulars can be found in our advertisement columns.</p>
        <p>Messrss. W: A. Brooke and Co. /will sell by auction a quantity of household furniture at Thornleigh, Whiston, on Thursday next.</p>
        <p>Popular_ Service at Bold Street Church.</p>
        <p>Al Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, a special monthly popular service was held. There was special music by an aug- mented choir, a quartet, "0 come every one that thirsteth " (" Elijah "), Mendelssohn, and a chorus, "Then shall your light" (" Elijah ") being • rendered. Two solos, "Sing ye a joyful song" (Dvorak) and "For ever blessed " (Handel), were sung by Mr. P. L. Richardson. The preacher was the the Rev. Percy meson, whose subject was 4' God:---the Creator."</p>
        <p>Richmond's Employes Abroad. There are nearly .500. employes of the Rich- mond Gas Stove Co. 'fighting on the variPus fronts, and the works war relief fund cora- mittee are desirous of sending them, year, a special Christmas parcel. An adver- tisement in another column asks the friends and relatives of -these- men to- asosist -the. com- mittee .to get the parcels addressed correctly by sending the correct names, rank, regimental number and battalion to the Secretary, Mr. John Fairclough, Richmond's Works, Grappen- hall, Warrington. as last</p>
        <p>Making Incandescent Gas - Mantles. Before the war we did a great trade with der - many in incandescent gas mantles; indeed, probably the bulk of our supply came from that quarter. Mantles, it is true, were made in England, but since the outbreak of hostilities their manufacture has been greatly stimulated. As little is known Of the process, a film which is to be shown at two of the picture houses Warrington shortly should arouse interest. The picture will illustrate the making of Zeimar mantles at Bredbury, near Stockport, where some 300 persons are employed. The whole pro- cess will be shown, and the film will serve to emphasise the fact that the Zeimar mantle, despite its foreign sounding name, is entirely 9 British product.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Death of Mr. Leslie Hodkinson.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. Leslie Hodkinson.</p>
        <p>Widespread regret will be felt at the news of the death of Mr. Leslie Hodkinson, ho passed away at the residence of his mother, "Sunnyside," Orford. Mr. Hodkinson was in his twenty-second year; and has served as a member of the office staff at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He T)elongs to a well-known Warrington family, being the oldest grandson of Mr. Peter Gerard, who was the leading cabinetmaker at Messrs. Rylands Bros. The funeral took place on Saturday at the Warrington Cemetery. The service, which was conducted by the Rev. Vale Owen, was held in Orford Church, and the organist gave a. rendering of "I know that my Redeemer liveth " a the body was carried from the church.</p>
        <p>Among the mourners were tirs. Hodkinson (mother), Mr. W. J. Hodkinson (brother); Mies Annie and Miss Maggie Hodkinson (sisters), Mr. E. Hodkinson, Mr. Walter Massey, Miss Whitlow and Mrs. Brown, of Altrinehain.</p>
        <p>Many handsome floral tributes m ere received from members of the family and friends. Among them were wreaths inscribed: "Mother. Sisters and Brother," "Aunt• Jennie and .Cissie," "Uncle Ted and' Aunt Minnie," "Cousins Walter, Bella and Bessie," "Aunt Nellie and family," Mr. and Mrs. Williams and family, Mr. and Mrs. Carpenter, Mr. and Mrs' Brown, Mr. and Mrs. Guy Taylor and family, "Dolly," Mrs. .Brookes and family, Mr. Fry. There was .also a wreath inscribed, "From hi=4 colleagues of the office staff of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles." Messrs. J..Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Dr. Carpenter's Visit to Cairo Street</head>
        <p>Dr. Carpenter's Visit to Cairo Street</p>
        <p>Chapel. The visit of the ,Rev. J. Estlin Carpenter to Cairo-street Chapel to-morrow is of some in- terest to old Warrihgtonians. Dr. Carpenter the nephew of Dr. Philip P. Carpenter, wbo from 1846 to 1858 was the minister of Cairo - street Chapel, and who is still loved and revered by those who remember him.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. Estlin Carpenter has had a tinguished scholastic caiver, having been prin- cipal of Manchester College, now at Oxford, where the old Academy Library now is, from 1875 to 1906. He has published many books.a considerable importance :—" The Life and, Work Of Mary •Carpenter, Philanthropist, "The First Three Gospels," "The Bible of tha 19th' Century," "The Life of Dr. Martineau," and he has been joint editor with Professor Rhys, Davids in the publication of much Buddhistic literature. He has written muck about comparative religion, and is regarded as one of the pioneers of that subject. It is in- teresting to call, to mind the fact that the Carpenters have a page in the supplement to Galton's famous work, "Hereditary Genius." It is there pointed out that Dr. Lant Carpenter, the father of Philip Carpenter, attained con- siderable eminence in the scholastic and scientific world. His three children also possessed great genius: Wm. B. C. Carpenter, becoming a famous physiologist; nary Car- penter, philanthropist and student of social welfare, much loved for the Work she did in, Bristol; and Philip Carpenter, minister and scientist, so closely associated with Warring- ton. The present Dr. ,Carpenter, who visits Warrington to-morrow, was a student when that book was published, but he is referred tn, as one who' showed promise of his ;relatives genius. Dr. Carpenter is. this year. the president of the British and Foreign -Unitarian Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
