      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS MULLIN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS MULLIN.</p>
        <p>threw if/ over the trench. It had no sooner left my hands than there was an explosion, but we got our .comrade and party away. .. . It was a miracle we were not all blown to pieces." A later letter .brought the news that Private Mullin had been awarded the Military Medal for gallantry in the field. "So," he said, "you can tell all at home how happy I feel. .. . I only did my duty." . .</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Mullin is the son of Mr. Thomas Mullin, 2, Cyril-street. He . was a reservist, haying been in the Army since he was nineteen years of age, and he is now 33.- When the war broke out he was .engaged by .Messrs. Pearson and Knowles in No. 4 Hoop Mill. He. attended St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street; and was well-known at the, Liberal Club, where he Was -a member of the committee. He served in the Boer War.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PRIVATE F.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE F.</p>
        <p>BANNER.</p>
        <p>Father Of Eillht Children Gels The</p>
        <p>MI Wan Medal.</p>
        <p>A father of eight children, Private Fed Banner, a stretcher-bearer in the South Lancs. Regiment, whose home address is 36; Plnmpton- street, has been awarded the Military, Medal for courage and presence of mind dispfayed while under fire in July. About that time Major belonging tp anothef Lancashire Regi- ment, wrote'. to the commanding officer of Private Banner's battalion as follows: "We had rather a bad time the other day from a heavy trench mortar, and I want to thank you for the !xcellent: work put in by Private Banner, of your battalion, stretcher-bearer. The Com- pany Commander reports to me that Private Banner was the first on the scene, and _bound up a very badly wounded man, and was gener- ally of great iasststace in attending to the wounded. Will you please convey to Private'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>PRIVATE FRED BANNER.</p>
        <p>Military Medal.</p>
        <p>Banner my thanks and the thanks of all ranks Of this battalion ?"</p>
        <p>Mrs. Banner also got a letter from her hus- ba.nd, describing the incident; It was as fol- lows : ,</p>
        <p>• “, I and my platoon were working making dug- outs .when the 'enemy began to,shell .us. lam very glad to Say that we all got out. of it, but sorry to say there were Some .of the, ,</p>
        <p>- regiment in. . 'There was a call .fOr stretcher- bearers, and my officer told me to go, and I did. When I got there I found a wounded man. I bandaged him up and took him out while the' shells were flying aroun.d us. But I did not care; I wanted to get the man out, and I got him out. I got great praise from the major." 'Writing to Private Banner under date 18/p/16, Captain Brian T. Dickson says: ". My dear Ban- ner,—My very heart3i.' congratulations and those of the other officers on your having won. the Military Medal. It was well won and lam very glad it has been awardel to you. It has come late, but better late than never." .</p>
        <p>-A letter signed A. Jeffs and Company, "A" Company, ran as follows :—"Dear • Old Com- rade,—lt is with feelings of great pleasure and pride that I write these few lines, carry- ing with them my hearts- congratulations and those of the N.C.O.'s of "A' Company, on yoUr having gained the Military Medal. Dear Comrade, it means one more honour for the best company in the whole British Array. . . . We are proud of you, my boy, and hope soon to see you again in good health and spirits. I am sure you will be glad to learn that Q.M.S. Wheeler has been awarded the Russian Order, Ist Class." _ . _</p>
        <p>Since rejoining his regiment, Private Ban- 'ner his 'written to 'his Wife :—" When I gbt back •te my company ,the Captain, Bergeant—, Major, .and Quartertnaster-Sern•cant all came and shook hands with me, and said they were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL R. STEPHENS Military Medal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>• we appreciated and admired your bravery in not reporting the fact that you had been wounded and in sticking to your gun so long. It was a very noble act and I have brought it to the notice of the Commanding Officer. It is . men like you we can ill-afford tO lose, and I only hope your wound will prove noth- ing more than a ' Blighty.' " Lance-Corporal Stephens was working. in the glycerine , department of 'Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd., when war broke out. This is the second time he has been wounded, the first time being at Hooge on June 16th' of last year: He has a wife and three children, Was educated at Heathside School, and At- tended St: Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>MERITORIOUS SERVICE MEDALS.</p>
        <p>- Congratulation to the following local who have been awarded the Meritorious Service Medal:—</p>
        <p>Company-Quartermaster-Sergeant W. Tynan, 12, Windsor-street;</p>
        <p>Sergeant J. W street; -</p>
        <p>Aekroyd, 14, Edgeworth-</p>
        <p>Sergeant M. A. 0. Voisey, 35, Warburton- street, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Pound Day at Thelwall Heys.</head>
        <p>Pound Day at Thelwall Heys.</p>
        <p>"Pound Day " was •held at Thelwall Heys HosPital on Thursday,. when maay and various gifts of the total value of about 5.3 were re- eeived. • Chieeamongst these were grocerie's and notepaper.</p>
        <p>Baptist Missionary Services. The annual missionary meetings of the Bap- tist Churches will be :held to-morrow. The missionary deputation is the Rev. G. W. Gibson, of India. On Sunday morning he will conduct the service at Golborne-street Church, in ' the afternoon he will address a united scholars' ser- vice at Hill Cliffe, and in the evening will pro- deed to Latchford Baptist Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Visiting Soldier-Husband in Switzerland.</head>
        <p>Visiting Soldier-Husband in Switzerland.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Matthews, of Hallfields-road, Orford, has sent word that she arrived in Switzerland on the 24th inst. She has gone thither to see her husband; who has been transferred from Ger- many, where he was a prisoner of war. Mr. T. H. •Spinks,,of the Parr Hall, very kindly made the, arrangements with the British, tied Cross Society. • .</p>
        <p>Mr. Jack Barnes' Message from AVitienberg.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Jack. Barnes, who wa-; manager of the Star 'lCinema when war broke out, and has been a prisoner of war. in-Germany for ,a long time, has recently written a 'letter to'-hiS-Ivife, Mrs. Ellen Barnes, of 2, Friars'-lane, Bridge- street„stating that he is well, and enclosing a portrait of the cemetery where many prisoners have been buried. There is shown a splendid monument in masonry, "In memory of the British officers, N.C.O.'s, men and civilians who died at Wittenberg, 1914-15." On it we can dis- tinguish the names of Major W. B. Fry and Captains A. A. Sutcliffe and S. F. Field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Girl Burned to Death; Unlucky Thirteen.</head>
        <p>Girl Burned to Death; Unlucky Thirteen.</p>
        <p>While lifting a kettle from the kitchen fire the clothes of Ethel, the thirteen-year-old daughter of Mr. and Mrs. James Kendall, of 49, Hopwood-street, caught fire and she died at the Infirmary on Saturday morning, three days after the accident occurred. At the inquest on Tuesday night Mrs. Kendall said that her</p>
        <p>daughter went downstairs at 6.30 on the Wed- nesday morning to light the fire and soon after- wards she was beard to shout,, " Mummie, I'm burning." On running downstairs witness saw that the girl's clothes were all ablaze, and ,g-reat difficulty was ,experienced in extinguishing the flames. Several times the girl remarked that it was done while' Bhe was lifting the kettle from the fire. Verclict: "Accidental death." Both the Coroner (Mr, S. Brighouse) and jury expressed .sympathy with Mr. and Mrs. Kendall in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Liberal Club's Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>Liberal Club's Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Liberal Club was held on Monday evening, Alderman Tinnion presiding. The balance slieet, submitted by the Hon. Secretary, Mr. Herbert Johnson. having been adopted, the Chairman remarked that con- sidering that. they had gone through two years of war, they should be very thankful to find the club still going. Many members of the club having joined the Army, it was left for a minority to carry on the institution. Sir A. H. Crosfield, Bart., was again elected president, and Alderman TinniOn, Councillor Wilkinson and Mr. Thomas Pemberton, vice-presidents; Mr. H. Johnson, hon. secretary; and Mr. P. H. Brown and Mr. C. Nurse, auditors. Mr. J. Perry retired from the committee, and Mr. S. H. Smith was elected in his place. The other members of the committee are: Messrs. J. T. Locker, F. Lindley, Joseph Wilkinson, W. Haves. F. Mullery, G. Marshall, F. Walker, J. T. Smith and T. Drew.</p>
        <p>Answers tp Questions. Waitregg (to Tommy home from the Somme, whose friend's eyes, just show through Sandages): ":Has ytmt friend been wounded?" Tommy: "Oh, no miss. Tfe'cut 'isself shaving this morning."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>• THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</head>
        <p>• THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</p>
        <p>DIED FOR THEIR eOUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>wood-avenue, is now officially report t•il to have inet his death on that date. •</p>
        <p>Corporal Boscow, who was twenty-live years of age, enlisted in October, 1914, and went to the Front in the following November. He came home on leave in December, 1915, and after re- turning to the Front, was wounded in Febru- ary. 0 . He again returned to the Front after a few Months in England, and a few weeks ago entered the bombing section. lle was educated at the _ National School, and was formerly employed as a wiredrawer by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. He was a good bowler and billiard player, and, by his prowess in these directions, Won several prizes.</p>
        <p>BOMBARDIER R. W. COOK.</p>
        <p>News Of Double Tragedy Contained In Same Letter. The tragic letter conveying Ow news of the death of Signaller IL Daniels, R.V.A., also brought word of the death in action of Bombardier Robert Walter Cook, R.F.A., son of Mr. and Mrs'. Cook, 3, Myrtle Grove, Latchford. Bombardier Cook enlisted on February 16th of last year, and had been in France twelve</p>
        <p>r i</p>
        <p>SIGNALLER HARRY DANIELS.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>dug-out up and they were killed' instantly. We found poor-Harry in the debris, and be will be buried today. , Harrf*as -very well MEM bY everyone, and was always ready' to liven .us uP when' things were dull." The foregoing letter, coneeriiing- S-ignalicr</p>
        <p>Harry Daniels, R.F.A., was written by one ( f his comrades to his home at 271, Knutsford- road.</p>
        <p>Formerly a senior clerk in the goods depart- ment on the London and North-Western Rail- way, Signaller Daniels was educated first at Padgate C.E. Schools, and later at Wash-lane School. Twenty-three years of age, he enlikted on 6th August of last year, and had been at the Front for many months. He also attended Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W. ROSE 'Killed.</p>
        <p>to give. In the recent successes Of the allied forces your husband played a fearless Part, doing his duty fearlessly and well."</p>
        <p>Private Rose Was a ,native of RuUcorn, .but had lived in Warrington for six years. His wife and on child now live In Clegg-street, hut formerly resided at 6,- Birchall-street. 'Thirty- three years of age, Private Rose, who enlisted on 28th December, 1914, and had been at the Fiont fifteen months when he was killed, was employed on the wharf by Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd., He was educated at the Board School, Runcorn, --t.lnd has a brother, David, serving with the forces at Gibraltar. He has also four nephews in the Army.</p>
