      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>PRIVATE W. E CHETTER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. E CHETTER.</p>
        <p>Further Details As To How He</p>
        <p>Met His Death.</p>
        <p>A further letter, containing full details of the death of Private William Edward Chetter, R.A.M.C., son of Mr. and Mrs. Chetter, 42, Pierpoint-street, has come• to hand through Private S. Burn, who says I don't think there is a man in the ambulance unit that does not deeply regret his death, for he was liked and respected by everyone, N.C.O.'s and men; in fact, he was always so cheerful under all circumstances that one could not help admiring him. I think he did more than his share of work in the advance. He was carry- ing in a wounded man when the piece of shell struck him. Of course, he was at once attended to by the other•-bearers and brought straight to the collecting post. It was there I saw 'him on the stretcher, and he smiled up at me and said, 'Hullo, Stan, I'm all right for Blighty now.' I thought the same at the time; in fact, his wounds were nothing to worry about, only flesh wounds in each leg, and I cannot think yet what can have caused his death." Private Chetter formerly worked at the Bew- sey Wire Ropery, later going to the Whitecross Co., Ltd. He also was a former member of the Bewsey Boy Scouts, and attended St. Paul's Bible Class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PRIVATE T. RICHARDSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE T. RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>Comrade Sends The Sad News</p>
        <p>Of His Death.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Richardson, of the South Lancashire Regiment, 7, Mill-lane, -Sankey Green, was killed in action October Ist, and leaves a widow and four children, the sad news having been gleaned from a letter written ,by</p>
        <p>son of Mr. and Mrs. Hazlehurst, 35, Bewsey- street.</p>
        <p>Captain Ronald Law wrote on the lith. inst., as follows I am sorry to say that I have to send you very bad news about your Son. He was on duty in the- telephone pit when an explosion occurred-through no fault of anyone, and he was badly burnt indeed: He was removed at once to hospital, and has been sent down tq the base, but I am afraid I cannot hold out any hope of recovery. To do so would- only give you 'false hope.' As much as it pains me to tell you, the doctors all say he can-not get dyer it. I cannot tell you how grieved", all the battery is, for he was one of the most popular lads here, and I, personally, knew him very well, as 'I have spent many a day and night with him in obser- vation posts. He was a fearless soldier, and, always did his- duty cheerfully, \and ,was respected by all, officers and- men." One of his comrades wrote: "He and another lad were sleeping at the Telephone Station, and the 'man put a lighted candle on a sandbag which had a lot of - ballistite inside, but of which they were not aware. The candle burnt very low, and the man was getting another candle when the bag burst into flames, burning your son and the other man very badly."</p>
        <p>Official news of his death was received yester- day, the notice stating. that he died on the 17th inst.</p>
        <p>Signaller Hazlehurst, who was nineteen years of age and of a quiet and retiring disposition, joined the Army in March of last year, and went to the Front in November i - He was educa- ted at Wycliffe , School, and was formerly employed in the Tinsmiths' Department at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and ,'s works.-</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>..... -4dminw-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>1 WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>1 WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>1)111% ER E. BUTTERWORTH.</p>
        <p>Much Liked By The Boys Of His Old Battery.</p>
        <p>Driver Ernest Butterworth, R.F.A., and son of Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Butterworth, 234, Thel- wall-lane, is reported to have been wounded in the mouth, and has lost three or four teeth. He is now in a Reading hospital. Driver H. A. Bishop, a comrade of Driver Butterworth, wrote under date October 15th: "We were conveying ammunition to the battery one night, when a shell burst close to the team we were driving. When the other two drivers picked themselves up, both having been knocked off their horses, they looked round and shouted for him, but found, no trace of him, and all the information we can get is that he was picked up by passing infantrymen. One Of them took him to the dressing station, wounded in the mouth. We</p>
        <p>Hanson, Paradise-place, an employe of the Firth Co.' has had a brother, Private Richard Hanson, of the A.S.C., in France for two years. He has several brothers-in-law on service an&amp; in training. As a' boy he attended Sankey School.</p>
