      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PRIVATE W. PYE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. PYE.</p>
        <p>AVounded On His First Day In The )Trenches.</p>
        <p>A field card was the first news that Mrs. Pye, who resides at 36, Wakvfield-street, had concern- ing her husband, Privata Walter I'ye. York and Lancs. Regiment, who has been wounded and slightly crushed. It was his fir4t cLy in The trenches when he received his wounds. , He is now in a convaleacent camp at Rouen. Writing on October 17th, he said: "We had fp go over the top, and I thank God I was one of the lucky ones to be spared. lam very com- fortable in ,hospital." . He wrote again on October 19th "I have been moved to a con- valescent camp, and do not know how long I shall remain here." . . .</p>
        <p>Private Pye, who was one of a draft of men transferred from the South Lancashire Regiment to the York and Lancs. Regiment, is a son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Pye, 24, Roome-street, and en- listed seven months ago, having only been at the Front a fortnight. He was an "old boy" of</p>
        <p>r dm...m..1</p>
        <p>Adams, 14, Wakefield-street. Ile was educated at Heathside School, and formerly worked for Messrs. joseiih Crosfield and Sons, I.td. jleis twenty-tiyc .years of age, and used to attend MAY Trinity GitUreh. Tih 'brofher; Gunner William, is in the R.F.A;,-</p>
