        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL T. BENION 'Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PRIVATE A. WILCOCK.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE A. WILCOCK.</p>
        <p>First Reported Missing ; Now Said To Be Killed.</p>
        <p>One more gallant member of the, South Lancs. Regiment, Private Alfred Wilcock; has made the supreme sacrifice in France. He was first reported missing, but now is reported to have been killed on July 22nd.</p>
        <p>Private Wilcock-, who was a brother of Mrs. Morgan, 17, Algernon-street, was 35 years, of age. When he enlisted he was living :with .his sister, Mrs. Wide; of Sheffield, where he was Aroiking. An "old boy" of St. James' School, he for- merly worshiped at St. James' Church, Latch- ford.- He enlisted in March of last• Year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>L INCE-CORPORAL G. HAMBLET.</head>
        <p>L INCE-CORPORAL G. HAMBLET.</p>
        <p>One Of Three Soldier-Brothers Wounded In Six Places;</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Robert Hamblet, 360, Knutsford- road, Latchford, have received news_ of the wounding of their son, Lance-Corporal Gilbert Hamblet, King's Own .Scottish Borderers. He has been wounded in six places, and is now in Whalley Range Hospital. Lance-Corporal Hamblet enlisted soon after war broke out, and went to France thres montlyg ago. An "old boy" of Christ Church Schoor, Latchford, he was formerly in the employ of Messrs. -Rylands Bros., Ltd., and is 30 years of -age. " He has two brothers with the Forces, Private Thomas, who is now in India, and Private John, South Lancs. Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DRIVER A. CARLEY.</head>
        <p>DRIVER A. CARLEY.</p>
        <p>Royal Engineer Struck In The Head.</p>
        <p>Driver Abel Carley, West Lancashire Division of the Royal Engineers, and husband of Mrs. M. Carley, 93, Church-street, was wounded in the head by shrapnel on September 27th, sus- taining a fractured skull. He is now in a Glas- gow hospital, and has recently been able to get up from his bed. Yesterday he informed his wife that he was making rapid progress to- wards recovery, and expected leave to come home very shortly. Driver Carley joined the R.F.A. on the out- break of war, but, after a short training, was discharged as medically unfit. He was rejected a few months later at the Warrington Recruit- ing Office, but when the West Lancashire Division of Royal Engineers opened an office in Bridge-street he succeeded in passing as a driver, being sent to France the beginning of the present year. When he enlisted he was employed at the Warrington Wire Rope Works, but was an old employe of Messrs. R. Rowson and Son, )3arbauld-street, with whom he was for many years. He alsO acted as groom in the stables of Sir Gilbert Greenall for a short time. Two brothers, Henry and, John, are serving at Salonika, one a bombardier,, having passed through Mons and other big battles on the 'Western Front. His other brother is a sergeant in the Veterinary Corps. Driver Carley is also a member of the "Olive Branch" Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PRIVATE H. HULL.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. HULL.</p>
        <p>Six Feet High Guardsman Wounded In The Neck.</p>
        <p>A fine young fellow standing over six feet in height, Private Harry Hull, of the Scots Guards, has been wounded in the nedk, and is also suffer- ing from dysentery. He has written from an Exeter. hospital to his parents, Mr. and Mrs. John Hull, 236, . Manchester-road, informing them that he is progressing favourably. A native of Blackpool, Private Hull, who is twenty-four years of age, enlisted in April last year, and has been at the Front about twelve months. He was formerly employed at Lipton's. Bridge-street, and later by the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway Company in their luggage office at Blackpool. He came to Warrington, along with his parents, four years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MISSiNG.</head>
        <p>MISSiNG.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. ISHERWOOD.</p>
        <p>Wounded Near German First Line ; Now Reported Missing.</p>
        <p>A grandson of Mrs. Birchall, 67, Birchen- street. in the person of Private Joseph Isher- wood, King's Own Scottish Borderers, is reported missing. Writing home, Private G. H. Jones, a comrade of Private Isherwood, said, "Just a few lines to inquire about Joe Isherwood, as I believe he is reported missing. He gave me your address just before we went into action on September 15th, and I promised him that I would let you have any news. The man he was near to when going across 'No Man's Land,' has told me that he was wounded before they reached the first line of enemy trenches:" - An old employe of Messrs. Caldivells, Ltd.. spade manufacturers, Stockton Heath, Private Isherwood enlisted on the outbreak of war, and has been out at the Front eighteen months. He is 24 years of age, an "old boy" of Hamil- ton-street School, and a former worshipper at St. Peter's Church. Private Isherwood's father has been with the Colours for twelve months, while his brother, Private G. W. Isherwood, is also serving. Mrs. Birchall has another grand- son, Able Seaman R. Birchall, serving with our watch dogs in the North Sea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Entertainment At Warrington Infirmary.</p>
        <p>Another enjoyable concert, arranged by Mr. W: B. Randall, was given to the wounded " Tommies" at the Infirmary on Wednesday evening, the following artistes taking part : Mrs. Sanders (contralto), Master N. Davies (soprano), Mr. Beswiek (tenor), Mr. Moon (baritone), Mr. W. Harrison (humorist), and Mr. Wastinage (violinist). All the artistes excelled themselves in their items and were loudly applauded by the audience. Private Markham (one of the inmates) and Mr. W. B. Randall accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>A concert was given in the theatre of White- cross Militaiy Hospital by Nurse Parker, R.C.S., 1 and party, on Wednesday evening. The pro- gramme was a long one, and the efforts of the artistes were fully appreciated, shown by Con- tinuous applause and calls for encores. The opening chorus by the members of the party, was the prelude to the many delights of the evening. Miss M. Murray contri?mted "Carry on" and " Tennessee " with good effect. A duet, "Waste not, 'Want not," was rendered by Nurse Dodds and Nurse Parker, and called forth loud applause. Miss M. Murray, as an elocutionist, delighted her hearers with a reci- tation. Sergeant S. E. Collins (Liverpool Scottish), sang "Kentucky home" in amusing style. The songs, Come and cuddle me," &amp;c., by Nurses Dodds, met with due appreciation. Solos and choruses by the party were well re- ceived. Mr. Milton caused loud and long laughter by his droll stories. The Misses Parker in a duet, "Ten little soldier lads" (composed for the occasion), were the means of prolonged mirth. Private Burrows sang "Long, long trail," in pleasing style. At the conclusion C.S.M. Blakeley called for a vote of thanks to the artistes, and the " Tommies " with vigour showed their apprecia- tion.</p>
        <p>• On Thursday evening i variety concert was given at Whitecross Military hospital by Mr. Kilduff. and party. The theatre Was densely packed, and the artistes' efforts were applauded with vigour. Encores were greatly in, demand. The first event was an.overture•" Faust," piano end fiute, rendered by Miss A. Kilduff and Mr. Podmore, in brilliant style. Mr. W. Kilduff. in songs and musical monologue, caused much fun and laughter. Duets by Misses A. and J. Kilduff met with full appreciation. Mr. Pod- more, •on flute and. piccolo, contributed some delightful solos. Professor Baxteau (conjurer), interested the " Tommies " with tricks.</p>
        <p>- For the songs. "Naiting for thee" and " Ten- nessee " Miss J. Kilduff was loudly applauded. violin kilo Os skilfully executed by Mr. • !Jaynes. Mr. W. Kilduff and 'Miss A. Kilduff sang a. duet, "Down the vale." Pte. Tweedall was a successful humorist. Miss A. Kilduff in the- song "Grace Darling" was highly appre- ciated. Mr. Brooks (chairman) called for a vote of thanks to the artistes, which was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PREFERRED FRANCE TO "NAGGING."</head>
        <p>PREFERRED FRANCE TO "NAGGING."</p>
        <p>Her husband, Robert Butler, of 24, Phillip- street, having been fined 40s. for an aggravated assault upon his wife, Mrs. Butler, at the, Borough Police Court on Thursday, secured a separation order and alimony of 15s. per week, she having custody of the three children. The jinan4'dedlared that he" would rather be. in I France than live with a woman like ker.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>IN HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>IN HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>ARTIFICat K. A. STUBBS.</p>
        <p>rhirteen Years' Army Service To His</p>
        <p>Credit.</p>
        <p>•Artificer Kenneth Albert Stubbs, A.S.C., hus- band of Mrs. Stubbs, 23, Ellesmere-street, is now' in a Leeds hospital sick and generally run down. He is an old soldier, having served for thirteen years, spending eight years in India, and then passing to the reserve, with which he was when called up crn the outtreak of war. He was</p>
        <p>drafted to the Front immediately with the first Expeditionary Force. Artificer Stubbs, who was working as an elec- trician at the Longford Wire Works when called up 4 was formerly with Mr. H. Rogers, electri- cian. He is an "old boy" of People's College, and is 31 years of age. He attended Holy Trinity</p>
        <p>PRIVATE K. A. STUBBS</p>
        <p>Church. His wife had six brothers serving, but one has been killed:</p>
        <p>SAPPER GUY</p>
        <p>M. TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Contracted Enteric Fever In Mesopotamia.</p>
        <p>To Mr. and Mrs. D. G. Taylor, 3, Walter- street, off Padgate-lane, has come .another official notice from the War Office respecting one of their three soldier-sons—Sapper Guy M.. Taylor, Royal Engineers. It is to the effect that he now lies in hospital suffering from enteric. Sapper Guy Taylor is the youngest of the three sons •of Mr. and Mrs. Taylor who have donned khaki to fight for their King and country. He is 22 ' years of age, followed the occupation of a wheelwright, and received his early education at the Padgate Day School. He is exceedingly well-known and liked in the Padgate district and was also a very regular attender of the Padgate Wesleyan Church. It is about 18 months ago. since he joined the Royal Engineers, and after, several months' training was drafted out to Mesopotamia in</p>
        <p>February last, where he was serving when. stricken down with enteric. The patriotism of this family is shown by the fact that Corpl. Len. Taylor, the. eldest of the sons, and also a member of the R.E., has been in France and Belgium since the first week of the war, and during the whole of that time has only visited this country on two short furloughs. He has had a number of narrow escapes, as will be readily understood when it is known he took part in the retreat from Mons, the Battled the Marne, and.:cither big engagements- right ..up to the present time. The second son—Trooper Sidney George Tay- lor, of the 3rd Dragoon Guards—gave his life for his country in the memorable charge on the Prussian Guards at Ypres in November, 1914. He came over with the troops from Egypt, where he had been for nearly three years, and was only at the Front a few days when he was killed. Still another -:relative of- Mr. and Mrs. Taylor is Bombardier C. Leaper, tif the Royal Field Artillery, a nephew who so distinguished himself' at the Front that he has been awarded the D.C.M. Much sympathy is extended toyoung, Taylor's family, and hopes entertained for his recovery. A sad feature of the case is that his friend, an Orford lad, died about the same time as the news was received of his illness, and was buried on Saturday at the Warrington Cemetery.</p>
