      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SWEIZ0)11i0V11(11101(</head>
        <p>SWEIZ0)11i0V11(11101(</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT BY WOUNDED SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Jewellery Missed At Lymm After Visits By Winssick Patient.</p>
        <p>Wearing the uniform of a Colporal of the Forces, Joseph Cadosa, a semi-coloured man, was charged before Mr. Frederick Loekyer, at the Police Court last Wednesday morning, with entering the premises of Mr. John Hardy Holt, Kildonan, Cherry-lane, and stealing jewellery to the value of £7 19s. 6d. Mr. Holt, giving evidence, stated that prisoner came to his house on Saturday, October 7th, and was introduced to him by his family. He was a •wounded soldier, and came from Winwick. They had tea, and he told him that he was just about to have ten days' furlough, and was going to spend them with some friends at War- rington. He had also been in hospital at Harrogate. He then said that he would like to spend his furlough at Lynim. and Mr. Holt told him that providing he could obtain sleep- ing accommodation, he would be welcome to come to his house for meals. He went out and returned shortly afterwards, and told him that he had obtained lodgings at Mrs. Kennedy's, Austin Cottage. He came to his house pretty regularly during the next fortnight. On Saturday, October 21st, when they had had dinner, they told him that they were going out, but they would be back again at 5 p.m. for tea. They went out about 245 p.m., and he (Mr. Holt) returned home about 3.45 p.m. He let himself in by the front door, and on going to .answer a knock at the• back door he found that it was not locked. He then noticed that the scullery window was also undone, and on going outside he noticed some marks on the wall that had been probably made by a man's foot. He thought nothing about it, as his son had gone away that afternoon, and he thought probably he had forgotten something, and on returning and finding the house locked he had got in that way. Later on in the evening, about seven o'clock, they missed a gold watch, and later on Mrs. Kennedy sent for him and told him she was afraid the soldier had gone. without paying his lodge. At 11 p.m., when his daughter was retiring, they found other articles were missing, including a lady's dress- ing case, valued at £2 10s. It was then too late to do anything, but in the morning he rE,- ported the matter to Acting-Sergeant Griffiths. Acting-Sergeant Griffiths stated that after receiving the complaint he made inquiries, and found that the prisoner had been seen going in the direction of the station carrying some of the missing articles. He made further in- quiries, and found that 150IIIP of the articles , had been pawned in Knutsford-road, Latch- ; ford. He then heard that prisoner had been apprehended by the Leeds police, and he went' to Leeds and brought him back. On being charged he replied, "All right." The articles recovered, a gold lever watch, gunmetal watch, gold albert, and lady's dress- ing case, were identified by Mr. Holt as belong- ing to his family. Two bangles and one pen- dant are not yet recovered.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Davies then made an application for a remand until 2nd November. On being asked if he had any reason,. to show why he should not be remanded, Nisoner replied, "No, but I would like the matter finished with."</p>
        <p>Remanding him until the second of next month, the magistrate remarked that he was very sorry to see him there, and it was a pity he had not more sense than to take advantage of a friend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
