      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>• is iSTiCT• , • •". • ' *1 V</head>
        <p>• is iSTiCT• , • •". • ' *1 V</p>
        <p>LIYMM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC. Don't forget to shop early next week.</p>
        <p>Don't leave your shopping until the last minute, or I am afraid you will have to go hungry. .</p>
        <p>Early closing comes into operation on Mon- day.</p>
        <p>Don't get annoyed with your baker or grocer if he refuses to serve you after eight o'clock. He will only be carrying out what the law demands.</p>
        <p>If he does serve you then he commits him- self, and makes himself liable to a penalty.</p>
        <p>What the law will demand next, even a per- son with the wildest imagination could hadly foretell.</p>
        <p>Another new Order is the "Lights on Vehicle's" Order. This, I believe, has been in force in some counties quite a long time.</p>
        <p>Cheshire gets it now, and Lymm being under the Cheshire Constabulary, will be drawn in.</p>
        <p>You will not be allowed to have too much light on your cycle in future, or else—" 2s. 6d. and costs."</p>
        <p>Nowadays you hardly know what to do. if you have no light at all you are liable, and if you have too much light you are also liable.</p>
        <p>The best thing to do is to obtain a copy c f the regulations and study it.</p>
        <p>Colliding with lamp posts, &amp;c., on dark nights can now be avoided.</p>
        <p>I notice that one genial shopkeeper is selling a luminous button.</p>
        <p>It can be worn in the hat, coat, or anywhere, and when it goes dark it gives off a blue light.</p>
        <p>Don't wait for a dark night before you obtain one, because if it happens to be after closing time you •won't get one.</p>
        <p>Special services were held at the Congrega- tional Church last week-end fo celebrate the anniversary of the Rev. Shields Gill's ministry.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of Sunday evening's ser- vice, the hymn, "Now the day is over," was sung in a new tune composed by Mr. Albert Leather, the church organist.</p>
        <p>It went very well, too, and has every prospect of becoming quite a popular tune.</p>
        <p>One last word, "Don't forget the early clos- ing on Monday."</p>
        <p>We were again favoured. with a visit from that well-known MancheAter company, the "Queries in Querieland," last Thursday 6-en- ing.</p>
        <p>Although three of the artistes were unable to appear, Mr. Geo. Stubbs, the producer, rose to the occasion, and deputised them all.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Geo. Stubbs is as fine an artiste as her husband, her singing was admirable, and her acting perfect.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards possesses a fine bass voice, and his singing of " Lighterman Torn" was in itself a model.</p>
        <p>Mr. James 11. Plant was respon.sible for a great deal of the humour, and Miss Marie Tudor delighted the audience with several fine songs.</p>
        <p>As an amateur production, I think the "Queries in Querieland " could hardly be ex- celled.</p>
        <p>• Well done, the Specials! You did very well, considering!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>REV. SHIELDS GILL.</head>
        <p>THE REV. SHIELDS GILL.</p>
        <p>First Anniversary Of His Lymm Ministry.</p>
        <p>It is just twelve months since the Rev. Shields Gill was appointed minister of the Lymm Congregational Church, and to celebrate the event his parishioners held a special week- end of services last week. ,The Sunday services were conducted by PrOfessor Sanders, of Nottingham College, the pastor's late master. Both services were ably rendered, and were enjoyed by fairly large congregations. On Monday evening a public tea meeting was held, and was followed by a lecture by Professor Sanders on the growth of modern Germany. Sir Arthur Haworth, Bart., who occupied the chair, said that he had known the Lymm Con- gregational Church for some considerable time, and it always gave him pleasure to do any- thing for them. We were going through a very strenuous time, • and were all called upon to make sacrifices. We should always, he said, let our eyes go back to the supreme sacrifice that was made nearly 2,000 years ago. If we would only remember this we would be stirred to do things that we had never done before. Professor Sanders having given his lecture on the growth of modern Germany, Mr. J. H. Holt, who proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and Lecturer, reminded the audience that they were present to celebrate the anniversary of the Rev. Shields Gill's ministry. Their pastor was a good minister, and "one of the best" in the village. (Ap- plause). Mr. Geo. Willan seconded,, and the Chairman and Lecturer suitably responded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Shields bill said he thanked Mr. Holt for his kind words. He took his duty as he found it, and he thought we had had some result. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>"OUR DAY."</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY."</p>
        <p>Another Concert By The "Queries In Querieland."</p>
        <p>• The Drill Hall on Thursday night was the scene of a grand concert given by that well- known Manchester company. ".The Queries in Querieland," in aid of •"Our Day" Fund, and, judging by the large audience, • the fund will be considerably benefited. The large, spacious hall was well filled with •a good-humoured 'crowd. Mr.. George Stubbs and his company of Queries were in • their best form and gave a first-class entertainment. Every item on the programme iras "tip top," and well de- served the thunderous -rounds of applause that almost lifted the large roof away. This is the third performance the company have given in Lymm. Since the war began they have given a total of 148 concerts for the 8.R.C.5., all of which have been voluntary. By these means they have raised about £6OO. '</p>
        <p>The concert was organised by the Working Men's Committee, and the following members acted as M.C.'s: Messrs. A. H. Hudson (hon. treasurer), K. N. Tweedale‘ (chairman), A. Halladay (secretary), T. H. West, T: Readitt, J.. Walker, H. Clare, S. Appleton, W. Seymour, and R. B. R. Hartley. The expenses of the con- cert are being met by private individuals, so the entire result will go to the fund. We are asked to mention that owing to enlistments there is Still a few vacancies ' for collectors. Order was maintained throughout the perform- ance by the efforts of the special police, under Leader W. G. Groves.</p>
        <p>Dog Injures Cyclist.—Mr. Herbert Talbott, Rush Green, was cycling along Booth's Hill in the direction of the Nag's Head Hotel, and when opposite the Cherry-lane corner a dog ran into his front wheel and threw him with con- siderable force to the ground. His cycle was completely wrecked, and he himself was severely bruised and-shaken. His eye was so badly cut that Dr. Thorpe found it necessary to insert several stitches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PRIVATE KERFOOT AND THE "TANKS."</head>
        <p>PRIVATE KERFOOT AND THE "TANKS."</p>
        <p>‘Vould Think The Whole German</p>
        <p>Army Had Given Up.</p>
        <p>Writing to Mr. David Daniels, confectioner, Booth's Hill, Private Ted Kerfoot sends the following letter :—" I have had a week in hos- pital. It's the first time that I have been ill since I enlisted, and I have been out here about 13 months, so I think that I have been very lucky. If the,weather will only keep fine we shall be able to tell you some good news 'before. long. Since July we have fairly been among it, and if we only keep on we shall very soon have all his men prisoners. The other day I came across two or three thousand that had just been taken, and they looked scared to death. No doubt you have heard a lot just lately about the Tanks. They.are a fine thing, I. can assure you. If you only saw the results after they had been in action you would think that the whole German Army had given up. They can do almost anything and get anywhere they want. I keep dropping on one or two Lymm lads, but they are not in my regiment. I have just seen Stanley Ma irs, from Cherry- lane; and ,Herbert Buckley, from Rush Green. I have also seen Tom Moston, who was reported ill in hospital in 'The Examiner' some time ago, and I was pleased to see that he had got better."</p>
        <p>Private Ted Kerfoot is a well-known Lvmm man, and is at present serving with the South Lancs. Regiment somewhere in France. He is a married man, and his wife resides at Sandy-lane, Lymm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PRIVATE JACK SHAW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JACK SHAW.</p>
        <p>Receives Shrapnel Wound In The Head.</p>
        <p>News has this week been received to the effect that Private Jack Shaw, Cheshire Regi- ment, has arrived in England suffering from</p>
      </div>
