      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916. GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916. GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, Nov. 4th:— Monday.—Musketry at range, 7.30 p.m. Range Officer, Private F. W. Grounds. Tuesday.—Musketry Instruction at Head- quarters, 7.45 p.m.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Squad drill at headquarters, 7.45</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at range for recruits, 7.30 p.m. Names of those required to attend will be posted at Headquarters. Range Officer, Sec.- Corporal F. Fox.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—Sec.-Corporal E. Pearce, Private L. Appleton. By order, F. A. FROST (Lieutenant) O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival At Christ Church.—At Christ Church on Sunday, harvest festival services were held. The morning preacher was the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson. The choir rendered the anthem, "Thou visitest the earth," the solo being taken by Miss Critchley. In the afternoon a children's service was con- ducted by the Rev. J. Hirst, when gifts were brought for wounded soldiers. The evening service was conducted by the Rev. J. Hirst, and</p>
        <p>a sermon was delivered by the Rev. IL W. Campbell Baugh, of Liverpool. The anthem, "I ivill feed My flock" was tastefully rendered, Mr. Wilfrid Fryer singing the solo. On Mon- day evening the church decorations were sold in the Church House in aid of the local War Relief Association.</p>
        <p>Burns's Sincerity.—The literary meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Thursday even- ing, when a paper on "Robert Burns" was given by the Rev. A. J. Morton. The meeting was presided over by Dr. Bennett, who gave a warm welcome to the young members. Mr. Morton said that the poet was born in a prosy age and his school days were very limited. His father was poor, but very strong and intelligent. Burns had the power of mak- ing Men's lives more venerable, but was un- able to regulate his own. His love of nature was portrayed in his poems "To a mountain daisy" and "To *a mouse." The great sin- cerity of his writings arose from actual ex- perience and was no sham or imitation. His poem " Twa Dogs" shows that even the poor may be full of nobility and high thoughts. This period of his writings includes the chief and best known of his' works, except "Tam o' Shanter," which was a one day's creation, written in answer to a challenge to produce a ghost story. In his later poems man becomes secondary. His "To the Unco' Guid" is a plea for sympathy, less criticism and no dog- matic teaching. His "Cotter's Saturday Night" is claimed by the world in general as his greatest poem. Burns was no material- ist; he was God-surrounded. He was always on the side of • freedom and the rights of the weak, this being illustrated in his poem "A man's a man for a' that."</p>
        <p>Funeral of Miss H. Hindley.—The funeral of the late Miss IL Hindley, whose death was reported in our last week's issue, took place at Christ Church, Padgate, on Saturday after- noon. The Rev. J. Hirst (viear of the parish) was the officiating minister, and he was assisted by the Rev. J. Glover, Minister of the Presbyterian Church at Risley. Miss Hindley had for many years ,been a day school teacher at the Risley Council School and a Sunday school teacher at the Padgate C.E. Sunday School, and many friends gathered round the graveside to pay their last respects to .one who had so recently been an active worker amongst them. The chief mourners were: Mrs. 'Lindley (mother), Mrs. Swindells (sister), Mr. Swin- dells (brother-in-law), Mrs. Savin (aunt), Mrs. Mellor, Mrs. Stafford, Mr. Buckley, Mr. Geo. Wood and the Misses Wood (cousins), Mr.- H. Bouchier (chairman of the local Education Committee), Mr. H. Clarkson (headmaster of Risky Council School) and many other friends. Wreaths and flowers were sent by "Her loving mother, sister, and brother-in- law," "Cousin Annie," "Loved ones at Bir- kenhead," the Rev. J. Hirst and Miss Turner, Mr. and Mrs. Clarkson, "All at The Hollies, Fearnhead," Miss K. Birtles, Mr. and Mrs. Worrall, "Cottage Homes," Mrs. Potter and family, Mr. and Mrs. Ashcroft, Mr. and Mrs. Knox, Mr. and Mrs. J. Daxon, Mr. and Mrs. Powell, Mrs. B. Gibbons and Miss Yates, Mr. and Mrs. Stringer, Mr. and Mrs, Andrews, Mr. and Mrs. Snailham and family, Mrs. Whittle, Miss Wightman, Teachers and Scholars of Risley Council School, Sunday Scholars of Padgate C.E. School, and Teachers (Past and present) of Padgate C.E. Sunday School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGINIENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGINIENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 29th October : Sunday.—Route March, Delamere; fall-in Victoria Square, 9.45; one ration to be carried and great coats slung; 5.45, dismiss. Monday.—Company and Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Tuesday.—Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Wednesday.—N.C.O.s' Class, 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Thursday,—Recruits' Drill, Signalling Class, and Musketry instruction to the new men allocated to No. 1 Platoon, Headquarters, 7.45. Friday.—Swedish Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Shoot v. "D" Company, let Norfolk V.R., Drill Hall, 8 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Orderlies for the week :—Sergeant J. F. Fer- gussou and Private F. Lowe.</p>
        <p>FRANK WAR,BI7RTOT, Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>The Large and continual flow of persons who come to me for Spectacles through the recommendation Of their friends is proof of the benefit derived from lailLion4qmgh system of sight-testing in which is combined scientific knowledge, long experience, and the greatest of care. My prices for spectacles are as low as possible consistent with the highest quality.</p>
        <p>SIGHT TESTING AND SPECTACLES FREE TO SOLDIERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Chairman, Mr. T. J. Ridgway; Board of Agri- culture representative, Mr. Newton Worthing- ton; military representatives, Messrs. A. de C. Parmiter, F. E. Evans, and Captain Hawkins, R.E.</p>
        <p>Produces Medical Board's Rejection Certificate. An applicant, an employe of the Urban Dis- trict Council, who produced a Medical Board's rejection certificate, was granted total exemp- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Single Badged Men.</head>
        <p>Single Badged Men.</p>
        <p>A married man, aged 34, who appealed on personal grounds, and asked for total exemp- tion, stated that by trade he was a painter and decorator, but owing to ill-health he left his trade and went into a gentleman's service. If he was called to the Colours he would suffer serious hardships as he had a delicate wife and child. Since he had lodged'his appeal he had gone into a munition works at Manchester.— Replying to questions, applicant said he was making barbed wire. He was not badged. He did not know that barbed wire making was worse for health than painting. He went into munition works to be doing more.—Taking it into consideration that the man was married and had only passed for Class 82, the Tribunal decided to grant conditional exemption until January Ist, subject to his employer in the meantime 'releasing a single badged man who had passed for general service. Coal Discharger Gets Conditional Exemption. A Manager of a coal wharf who appealed for a man, stated that the man had been with the firm for 14 years, and his duties were discharg- ing coal from boats. The military authorities had taken one man, and if he had to go it would be a serious matter.—You don't suggest that the people of Lymm could not get coal, do you? —I do suggest it, sir.—On being informed that the man had only passed Class 82, the Tribunal granted conditional exemption, subject to V.T.C. service. Grocer and Provision Dealer.</p>
        <p>A grocer and provision dealer who applied for exemption, stated that he had an old- established business, and he had no other male help. His wife helped him a little, but was not able to do much. ,Before the war he employed two men, and he had two brothers in the Army. —lf it was necessary to employ two men before the war, how do you manage now F—My wife helps a little, and I have a young lady.—Now your wife has had this experience, don't you think she could maiaage?—No, sir, I would not think of it for one minute, I would rather sell up.—Adjourned until November 3rd for Medical Board's decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Farmer Who Cou!d Manage At a Push.</head>
        <p>Farmer Who Cou!d Manage At a Push.</p>
        <p>A farmer who appealed for a man, 36, stated that he was a good farm hand and could dl any work connected with a farm. He had 96 acres, 30 of which were arable, and four work- ing horses and one colt. He at present had two men and two boys working on the farm.—lt was stated that the appeal in respect of the other man would be heard next week.—Could you not manage with one man and two boys?—Well, yes, at a push.—Adjourned for a week so that both men could appear together. Father Subject To Fits.</p>
        <p>A man who asked for total exemption on per- sonal grounds, stated that he was a clerk in a soap manufacturer's office. He was the only son, and his father, who was 58 years of age, was subject •to fits. He helped to support his father and mother and one sister.—Dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Discharged, But Called Up Again.</head>
        <p>Discharged, But Called Up Again.</p>
        <p>A window cleaner who had once voluntarily enlisted but had been dischargea as one not likely to become an efficient soldier, appealed for total exemption on business grounds.—The Tribunal moved that now he had reattested they were powerless, and dismissed the case. "Thought He Would- Be On The Safe Side." A single man (21), who appealed for exemp- tion, stated that through ill-health he had not been to work for the last seven months. He was an electrical engineer, and worked for a controlled firm, and was a badged man:—Have you got your certificate ?—Yes.--Why did you appeal?—Thought we would be on the safe side.—The Chairman said the appeal wag' un- necessary; if he was debadged he would have to look after himself sharp.</p>
        <p>Butcher and His Man.</p>
        <p>A butcher who appealed for a man (25), single, stated that he was the Only man left of a staff of four. They killed four beasts a week, 20 sheep, and about three pigs. He also had about 300 sheep lying out that needed at- tention. He cold not do slaughtering himself, as he had a weak heart.—Could not you buy your meat from a wholesale slaughterman?— Impossible, there no other butcher in the district who would do the slaughtering for you ?—I don't think so.-.--Suppose the military authorities found you a couple of slaughtermen you would raise no objection ?—Well, sir, my man is very trustworthy and handles a lot of money.—Temporary exemption till January Ist, and in meantime man to go before Medical Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Mother For Son.</head>
        <p>Mother For Son.</p>
        <p>A widow; who is a butcher by trade, appeared to ask for -exemption for her only son. He was a partner in the business, and since his father died he had managed it. The business was a very old one, and had been in the family for a period of 60 years. They killed • two beasts a week, eight or ten sheep, and *several pigs in season. On an average they supplied about 300 customers weekly,' mostly poor people. They also covered a lot of outlying districts with a horse and. trap. He was the only man in the business, and if he went it would fall through.— Mr. G. L. Welford, solicitor, representing applicant: What steps have ,you taken to get another man ?—My brother-in-law has tried, but unsuccessfully.—Conditional exemption till January Ist, subject to V.T.C. service, and in the meantime efforts to be made to get another man.</p>
        <p>"Nothing But Leaves."—Under the auspices of the Young People's Christian Endeavour a public lecture entitled "Nothing But Leaves" was given on Tuesday at the Baptist Church, Higher-lane, 'by Mr. Charles Barrett, of Lymm. The lecture was, illustrated by ex- tremely interesting lantern slides drawn and produced by the lecturer himself. A collection was made in aid of the Soldiers' Christmas Plum Pudding Fund and the Y.M.C.A. Huts. The Pastor (the Rev. Edward E. Lovell) operated the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>TREAT FOR WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon twenty-five wounded soldiers from .the Whitecross Hospital were the guests of the lady members of Stockton Heath Adult School at their headquarters, Greenbank. The visitors were hospitably entertained to tea by the ladies, led by their President, Mrs. W. H. Sherburn, and this was the prelude to a most enjoyable whist drive and musical evening. Attractive and useful prizes were offered for competition, cigarettes, walking sticks, &amp;e., and the games were well contested. Mr. Sherburn presided at the concert, which was given by Mr. Levi Richardson, Mr. Potter and Mr. Kay, while Miss Sherburn's songs were, as usual, highly appreciated. Mr. W. Hankey gave a humorous sketch, and Mrs. Potter was at .the piano. Before the company . separated Mr. Richardson gave the toast of Mrs. Sherburn and the other ladies of. the school, which was greeted with . "three times three." Some bunches of flowers which graced the tables were borne off to the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRIVATE P. HAYES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE P. HAYES.</p>
        <p>11l Southport Hospital AVounded In</p>
        <p>Mouth And Neck.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Hayes, 23, Brackley-street, Stockton Heath, have received news to the effect that their son, Private Peter Hayes, of the Cheshire Regiment, was wounded in the neck and mouth in France on the 10th inst. He is now in hospital at Southport, and the latest bulletin reports, "doing nicely." Private Hayes, who is twenty-four years of age, enlisted in February this year. and has been at the Front about three months. He is an "old boy" of the Arpley-street School. and, after serving his time as a fitter at Messrs. Richmond's Works, Grappenhall, entered the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>191‘,P1)L,11. OF REFUSE.</head>
        <p>191‘,P1)L,11. OF REFUSE.</p>
        <p>Hol% Warrington Corporation Helps</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Their Neighbour.</head>
        <p>Their Neighbour.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Parochial Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough) read a letter received from the Town Clerk of Warrington in respect to the treatment of night soil. In his letter Mr. Lyon Whittle said it was reported to the Sanitary Works Committee at their meeting on October 10th, that the ten loads were still being sent in from Stockton Heath instead of six, which the Corporation had agreed to take. It was only with the greatest difficulty that the committee were taking the six, and the sole reason for doing so was to give the committee all the neighbourly assist- ance they could. The committee were informed that, owing to the present difficulty of working, it was absolutely impossible to accept more than six loads. They should be glad if instruc- tions were given the contractor to cease the extra delivery at once.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hough; said that he had told the con- tractor not to send any more than the six loads.</p>
        <p>Contractor's Account.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Pearson, chairman of the Sanitary Committee, laid on the table the account of Mr. Spellsley, sanitation contractor, being a sum of £lO5 for services during the past quarter, and at the rate of £429 per annum. There was, in addition, the cost Of extra carting to War- rington; thirteen weeks at 12s. per week, and at • their last meeting they agreed to a further charge of 10s. a week for keeping the tip E.t Naylor's in order—four weeks—a. The con- tractor had reported that he had arranged for this tip free of all charge, and as a good deal of paper came with the stuff from the ash .pails it was necessary to get somebody to destroy the rubbish. Subject to checking, Mr. Pearson be- lieved the account to be correct, and moved that it be paid. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr.-Westbrook seconded, and the account was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LAST FLICKER. Lamps In The Blues At Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>THE LAST FLICKER. Lamps In The Blues At Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath has been still further reduced in the number of glimmering rays which the authorities consider Should be sufficient to light the parish after nightfall. At Tuesday's meeting of the Parochial Com- mittee,</p>
        <p>Mr. McDermid. chairman of the Lighting Committee, said the police authorities had re- quested that the street lamps should be shaded as semn as possible. They suggested that theY be coloured a dark shade from the top to below the burner, and the lower part of the lantern tube coloured light blue. He (Mr. McDerniid) saw Inspector Breese, and afterwards the gas officials at Warrington. The latter had evidently looked after the lamps, as he noticed the lower part had been obscured. The lights were very little better than none at all, and he did not know that it was value for paying for their being lighted.</p>
        <p>It transpired, however, that only three lamps are concerned, and the Chairman's report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>AIR. C. K. POTTS.</head>
        <p>AIR. C. K. POTTS.</p>
        <p>Death And Funeral Of Respected Resident.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. K, Potts, a well-known resident of Stockton Heath, died in his 62nd year rather suddenly at his residence, 8, Bedford-street, last Friday. He was a native of Northwich, anamd subsequently resided for many years at Stre', and was a member of the choir and Parish Church there. About twenty years ago he came to Stockton Heath and was employed as a wood machinist in Mr. C. W. Davenport's joinery works. He was actively engaged until about threp years ago, when, owing to rapidly failing eyesight, he was obliged to give up his cus- tomary occupation. Although so far advanced in years, he was full of energy, and was held in the highest esteem by all who knew him. He was a member also of St. Thomas' Church, and formerly represented the Wood Machinists' Society on the Warrington Trades and Labour Council. He leaves a widow and married daughter.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Monday at St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, when the Rev. J. Collins, vicar, officiated. Two of his favourite hymns, " Jesu, lover of my soul " " How sweet the Name of Jesus sounds," were sung by the choir, Mr. F. Rose presiding at the organ. The chief mourners were: Mr. and Mrs. B. Jolley (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. J. X. Potts, Northwich (brother), Mr. P. Leicester (nephew), Mrs. J. Fox (niece), with Mr. J. POl (Sale), Mrs. J. Hill, Mr. and Mrs. J. Seddon, gr. H. Emerton (Earlestown), Mr. and Mrs. E. Jons (Patricroft), and Mr. and Mrs. J. Gibson (Trafford Park). There was also a very large congregation, and a number of wreaths included offerings of syta" pathy from the members of the choir of St' Thomas' Church, the Trades and Laboui Coun- cil, and "His friends at Stretton."</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—A concert was given in the • schoolroom on Thursday evening, the pro- gramme being by the members of the Warring- ton Wesley Guild Cycle Club which, though depleted of several of its talented male mem- bers, provided a very enjoyable entertainment. The Rev. P. meson, president of the Guild, irt opening the proceedings, introduced Mr. 3. Hamblett, a prominent ?member of the club, 0 chairman. The programme consisted of song.T.4 by Miss M. Jones, Miss J. Ward and Miss I\ • Kershaw; recitations by Miss Egerton and Mr' G. Whitfield; pianoforte solos by Miss E. Tur- ner and Miss, G. Lawton; and a reading bY Mr. C. Wilcockson (captain). The whole of the items were well given and thoroughb: appreciated by the audience. Misses Turner ancl Lawton and Mrs. Bates acted as accompanlets.</p>
        <p>How to Move a Cheshire Squire. A Wesleyan chapel in a Cheshire village was in a very bad condition, and a meeting vir called to discuss the subject of repairs. T.,_ne squire was present. He stood up, declared the place was not so bad, after all, and only needed little doing to it, promised £5, and sat down again. Scarcely had he done so when a lumP eif plaster fell from the ceiling on his bald he"- Jiimping up, he exclaimed: "The place' is worse than I thought. I Will give 240." An old man in the rear stood up and said quite audibly: " Hit 'im again! Hit 'im again!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>1.11./1.1.1.M111111.111.111.111.1...M..M.,,,,,tX.,.., —..ease._</head>
        <p>1.11./1.1.1.M111111.111.111.111.1...M..M.,,,,,tX.,.., —..ease._</p>
        <p>PRIVATE PETER HAYES Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>employment of the Warrington Tanning Co., Ltd., Mersey-street. He was a member of the congregation of St. Thomas' Church, and was connected with the Church Lads' Brigade. His brother, Jack, is serving with the Grenadier Guards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MILITARY FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Alcock Interred At Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the funeral of Lance-Corporal Alcock, who died of wounds received in action, took place at St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, with military honours, a firing party of the South Lancashire Regiment having been sent from Crosby under command of two officers. The Rev. J. Collins, vicar, conducted the ser- vice. The chief mourners were: Mr. William Alcock and Corporal J. B. Alcock (brothers), Mr. J. Hourie (brother-in-law), and Mr. J. Cat- terall (cousin). A number of former fellow workmen from Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., War- rington, layby, attended. Wreaths were sent by "His Mother and Sister," Mr. and Mrs. Wil- liam Alcock, Mr. and Mrs. J. •B. Alcock, "Cousin Joe and Eva," Scotland; "Mr. J. Gleave to Tom," Mr. and Mrs. Gleave and family, Mr. and Mrs. Williams and family, Ship Inn; Mrs. E. Scholtz, Mrs. Cartwright, " Wal- ton neighbours," R. A. Naylor,•Ltd., staff and workmen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL DAVENPORT.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL DAVENPORT.</p>
        <p>Striking Letter From His Lieutenant.</p>
        <p>Further news has been received in Stockton Heath respecting the death of Lance-Corporal Charles Davenport, King's Liverpool Regi- ment, son of Mr. C. W. Davenport, of Holly Cottage, Farrell-road, principal of the firm of building contractors, Messrs. C. W. Davenport and Sons. In our issue of the* 14th inst. we published a photograph of this young soldier, together with an account of his death. He was described in the article as Private Daven- port, but he apparently gained his stripe shortly before the engagement in which he was unfortunately. killed. The following letter has been received from Lieutenant Daniel Bain, of the same regiment I fear I can supply very little information abort Lance- Corporal Davenport. On the day he was killed most of his company officers fell, and I was ordered to take command of the company. We went over together and Lance-Corporal Daven- port was put in charge of a party of men who were evacuating wounded. They had carried out three wounded cases and were carrying out a fourth when a shell burst amongst the party, killing six of them. Lance-Corporal Davenport was struck in the head and killed instantly. He was buried at dusk in a grave behind the fire trench. His personal effects have been forwarded in the usual way. Those who knew him speak very .highly indeed of him, both as a man and as a. N.C.O. You and his relatives have my sincerest - sympathy. 'Greater love hath no man than this, is Lance-Corporal Davenport's—the hero's—epi- taph in ccmmon with so many other noble fellows."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NATIONAL MISSION AT Si'.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL MISSION AT Si'.</p>
        <p>TIIOMAS\ CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Services in connecton with the National Mission were held in St. Thomas' Parish Church, Stockton Heath, on Saturday, Sunday and Monday. The Vicar, Rev. J. Collins, had a leaflet distributed amongst his parishioners containing a letter from the Bishop's Messen- ger. In this the latter said it would be his duty to come amongst them as "an entire stranger, whose name must not be divulged." He asked them to let their preparation for the mission be sincere and to make a resolution that they would submit to the promptings of the Holy Spirit as from time to time He re- vealed Himself unto them, so that in the new England which is being purchased by so many precious lives they might one and all co-operate with God as „willing and faithful servants. On Saturday afternoon there was an inter- cessory service with address, and on Sunday the Bishop's Messenger preached morning and evening, and conducted a children's service. On Monday afternoon a further children's service was held, and a conference took place in the evening. All services were largely attended, and deep interest in the mission was observable at each gatherimg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>CHAPTER XXIL</p>
        <p>THE ROSE IN THE DUST.</p>
        <p>Pandemonium reigned within the great circus tent. While those nearest rushed to the aid of Santley, dead by the avenging hand of Splinters, the bulk of the audience, imagining that the animals were loose, rushed 'madly for the exits. Once again Esther and Arthur were parted. Durand, fearing that his victim would meet the girl, pointed to the struggling mass around them, and suggested that they should make their way out; and Quabba, fearing for the safety of his young mistress, fought to force a way clear. Showmen at the performers' entrance, hearing the niatnl shot and the hoarse cries .of the crowd as it rose to its feet and surged to the exits, imagined a, murderous altercation between town roughs and the circus men had broken loose. The circus men's sallying cry was raised. The canvas men, the swindlers, the acrobats, all those who knew a tragedy had happened but had not sensed what it was, seized tent pegs and stakes, drew forth brass knuckles and slungshots, and struck sickening blows right and left at every head in sight. Roughs cut the ropes and tore down the half-vacated seats, and added to the general frenzy of fright, destruction and panic. But from the first tragic happening, the death of La Belle under the lion's paw, a greedy eye had been upon the blazing Diamond from the Sky. As the paw of Lancelot the lion drew away from the scarred, dead breast, of his mistress, the chain to the diamond locket clung to his cruel blood-stained claws. Foremost in the throng, seeking escape from the scene of horror, was Sankey, the stableman, where Qualiba 'kept his outfit. Sankey was holidaying here at the circus, as was half of the town. He had seen the diamond blazing on the breast of the fair and faithless La Belle. He had seen the lion strike her down &amp;ad drag tht diamond to the cage door. Sankey passed across the bodies of the dead men outside the cage, snatched at the bloody bauble, and drew it from the claws of the lion. Then he fought his way out of the melee. Of those who had seen him seize the diamond from the lion's paw, several had been separated from him in the struggle and confusion, and others had been struck down by the circus-men shouting the circus- men's battle-cry. '"Hey! Rube r. Hiding the diamond in the pocket of his coat, his hand clasped upon it, Sankey seemed to be but one of the many frightened, fleeing spectators speeding from the scene of tragedy and riot. Panting, he gained the street, and made his way back to ihe mean neighbourhood where his stable was. Here he threw the nervous monkey Clarence, tied to this stall of Quablzres pony, into fits as he rushed into the place, clambered up the ladder, and hid his booty, 'The Diamond from the Sky, beneath the hay in a corner of the loft. - - _ .</p>
        <p>_ Quabba had seen Esther safely to her hotel and had returned. • At the entrance to the stable yard he met the policeman, who had already heard of the riot call that had brought the reserves to the scene of tragedy and battle at the circus. The Policeman discussed the whole exciting, affair with Quabba, who had been a spectator. The two came down the alley together, and Sankey, the stable- man, Peering panic-stricken from a crack in The loft wall, saw them, and his guilty conscience Prompted the harrowing thought that he had been identified as the thief who had dragged the diamond from the claws of death, and that the search for him was on. The hunchback and the policeman called his name,' but Sankey did not answer. He lay trembling in the loft in an ague of fear. They left the stable without looking in the loft, and walked up the alley to the street, and Sankey dropped down the ladder, climbed the back fence, and hid in an- other part of the town, leaving the diamond be- neath the hay.</p>
        <p>Parting from the policeman, Quabba returned to the stable, angrily uttering maledictions on the absent Sankey, for Clarence the monkey was chat- tering with hunger. The pony hid not been watered and the hayrack was empty. Quabba attended to these duties, then clambered to the loft and pushed hay down into the empty rack. He gave full measure of hay to the pony. He did not know that in the generous measure of hay he shoved down into the rack there fell and lodged, hidden at the bottom just over the manger, the precious diamond. Reaching home, Arthur demanded an explanation .of Durand for his action in hustling him away from the theatre. He insisted that he saw Esther among the spectators. "You must be crazy," sneered Durand. "You see the girl everywhere; waking or sleeping it is always Esther." "I am not crazy. I saw Esther there, and I want her fund." "The best thing to do in your present_ state of mind—and remember you are not strong yet—is to rest. Try and forget all about the girl. Search for her when you are better," was Durand's advice. Although Arthur could not probe the depths of Durand's vallainy he felt that the truth was being kept frcm him. Distrusting himself, and suspicious of those around him, he wrote a telegram to Tom Blake, the Richmond detective. Of all with whom he had dealt Arthur had the most confidence in this astute and secretive confidential agent of his. Arthur also felt he could trust his English butler, Parker. Taking advantage of a moment when he was alone, Arthur had scribbled his telegram, which read: "Answer at once. Is Esther Harding in Los Angeles? Also wire condition of Hagar Harding." The telegram was signed with the name he was known by in the west, "John Powell." He handed this to Parker, who faithfully promised he would send the telegram in secret, and he kept his word.</p>
        <p>When Blake received this strange message he was puzzled, for he knew Esther had been in the west for several weeks. But he had learnt of Arthur's injuries, and deemed that Esther was kept from the injured man, for Blake knew, too, that Blair and Vivian were in Los Angeles, and he suspected them. Blake telegraphed promptly. "Answering your wire, Miss Esther Harding is in Los Angeles, Hagar Harding continues to improve." This telegram was delivered at the offices of the Fair Hope Oil Company. Blair, in charge, re- ceived all business and personal communications in the absence of the injured Arthur. Blair opened the envelope cautiously at the suggestion of Vivian,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>t„,_rol_ w(4.Q(4+</head>
        <p>t„,_rol_ w(4.Q(4+</p>
        <p>who was present. They were dumbfounded for a moment, and then Vivian pointed to the first line of the message, which was typewritten. "There is a purple ribbon on the typewriter over there," she whispered. "It seems the same type of machine this telegram was copied on, in purple, also. If the word 'not' were added at the end of the.first line, the message would read: "'Answering your wire, Miss Esther Harding is not in Los Angeles. Hagar Harding continues to improve.' " "Vi, you are a genius," cried Blair.</p>
        <p>They placed the telegram in the typewriter, Vivian firmly struck the keys, and the entire import of the message was changed. Then Blair carefully sealed the envelope and sent it by the office boy to Mr. Powell at his home.</p>
        <p>"That will convince him he is dotty," said Blair. "But he must be pretty cunning, at that, to get a telegram out to Blake without Durand or de Vaux knowing of it, for they watch him like hawks."</p>
        <p>Arthur was clay in the hands of the conspira- tors again when the doctored message from Blair reached him. Arthur believed now 'that it was true, as those around him inferred—his obsession that Esther was near was a symptom of recurrent insanity. He shuddered and grew sick at the thought.</p>
        <p>"Kind Fates, spare me from madness," he prayed in agony. "Let me recover in body and mind to make a man of myself, to return to Esther whole and sound and clean and honourable, as I promised her and my poor mother I would. "Base, unworthy profligate as I have been, my gipsy mother sacrificed her youth and every hap- piness in life, and she now lies in a madhouse, as I lie mad in a mansion."</p>
        <p>Then in his weakness and in his strength he battled with the drug desire that clutched him by the soul—he battled and lost.</p>
        <p>Still searching for the missing heir, Marmaduke Smythe called at the offices of the Oil Company. Here he met Blair, in charge, and Vivian Marston. They gave him no clue that the man he sought as "John Powell" for information regarding Arthur Stanleigh was Arthur Stanleigh himself. Smythe had never met Arthur Stanleigh except once briefly in the wilderness, and here taciturnity on both sides in a casual meeting had prevented either knowing the purpose of the other. He discussed the kindred subjects of the Stanleigh Earldom and 'The Diamond from the Sky. Smythe had no interest in the diamond; it was but an heirloom of doubtful value in his eyes, an heirloom pertaining to the American Stanleighs only. But the English title concerned him much, and he informed Blair that, unless it was proved definitely that the fugitive Arthur Stanleigh was dead, the claim of Blair to both the diamond and the Stanleigh Earldom could not be allowed. But Blair, Smythe admitted was next of kin and next in succession.</p>
        <p>Arthur's escape from the circus haying been noted by the newspaper men the journals again turned their attentions to the doings of the "Golden Man," as he was termed. Mr. Powell's friends it was confidently stated would be pleased to hear that he was recovering from the accident which befell him at Mammoth.</p>
        <p>"This afternoon," read one of the papers, "Mr. Powell will go to the races with his closest friends. These are his private physician, the eminent Dr. Frank Durand; his cousin, Mr. Blair Stanleigh, an eastern capitalist associated with Mr. Powell and managing his affairs during his convalescence; the Count de Vaux, of Paris, and Miss Vivian Marston, who, it is rumoured, is a young woman of splendid family from New York, who has taken up nursing. She was called into the case by Dr. Durand, and aided that skilled physician to restore his patient to health again." All this and much more Esther read. These were fine friends, she thought. Fine friends, indeed, and he, the gipsy, changeling, lorded it well among them. For the first time a sense of injustice and indignation burnt within Esther's bosom. She would make the test and prove him what he was. He was the gipsy and she the true Stanleigh. She would go as the gipsy and confront the so-called gentleman, who once again bare a. name that was not his own. She donned her gipsy dress and took her tambourine and walked afoot beside Quabba, the humble mountebank. The daughtpr of the Stan- leighs, the fair young mistress of Stanleigh Hall, walked in ,the dust with a mountebank and a monkey beside the mountebank's pony and street organ. _</p>
        <p>Towar s them came the tally-ho. Arthur, in high spirits. despite his recent injuries, demanded to drive the horses, n•i,l sitting beside Vivian displaced Blair. Vivian smiled at Arthur • handed him a rose from the bunch at her belt. And then Esther stepped bY the wheelhorse and called to aim "Arthur."</p>
        <p>He drew the horses to a halt. A look of glad wild joy came to his eyes, succeeded by a glare of horror. Vivian lashed the off horse with the whip she had seized, the rose fell from Arthur's nerve- less hand, the horses dashed away, and the coach disappeared in a cloud of dust. In the dust of the road lay, the rose. Quabba stooped and lifted it from the dust, handing it to the broken-hearted girl, who leant in her gipsy finery against the pony cart and wept. The rose in the dust to a rose in the dust.</p>
        <p>Far down the road racegoers in other equippages saw a ragged man ,jelinging to a. tally-ho. "Whip behind," was the cry. The lash flew back, and Luke Lovell, returned for blackmail and revenge. dropped from the 'coach to which he had run and clung and ahook his fist and cursed after the more exalted gipsy who was master of a coach and four. But the =titer of a coach and four had sunk back fainting among his friends. What use was wealth to a madman? He had seen a flower by the wayside and a rose in the dust, and he deemed what he had seen were but the visions of a mind diseased.</p>
        <p>That night Quabba's pony in his stall munched at his hay and knew naught of human heartaches. The rats scampered and annoyed him, and some- thing fell half from the hayrack and dangled at his nose. The pony nibbled at it, hut it was not good to eat. A foolish thing indeed the pony re- garded it, and 'vet it as that baleful thing for which bauble-loving men and women had bartered and taken life.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
