      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1916.</p>
        <p>• 44.,...*****41.46.+.404).4,444.44,4•• • •</p>
        <p>increase their lead; but failed to -pierce the visitors' defence again. Towards the close of the game the Recs. made a strong rally to get on equal terms, Brown and Green in the centre positions working desperately, but the Saints' defence prevailed, and the final score was: St. Eipbin's, 1 goal; Latchford Recs., nil.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY UNITED v. WIDNES JUN:ORS. In Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Howley: Walters; Foster, Rowlinson; Hoarty, Miller. Hind; Humphries, Boyle, Davies, Fitzgerald and Hibbert.</p>
        <p>WiOnes Juniors: Royle; Marsh, Fearn; Cox, Mooney, Hodgkiss•' Baldwin, McConnell, Magee, Martin and A. N. Other. Referee, Mr. Holt.</p>
        <p>The United made the first attack, and Fitzgerald forced •a corner, which proved fruitless. They were more successful in a second attack, which resulted in a scramble, during which Humphries scored. The Juniors improved greatly, and Baldwin put the leather nicely across the goalmouth, and several of the visitors missed an open goal. Half-timc: Howley, 1 goal; Widnes Juniors, nil. In the second half the defenders proved too good for the attack, and the final score was: Rowley, 1 goal; Widnes Juniors', nil.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL v. APPLETON HORNETS.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former Grappenhall: Hadkiss; Aikenhead, Thornton, Jones, Lewis; Kenwright, Rigby, Smith and Willoughby. Appleton: Millar; Harrison, Fozard; Johnson, Howard, Roberts; Smith, Tegg, Brown, Green and Woolley. Referee, Mr. Burgess. The visitors made the first attack and were re- pulsed by Jones, then coming again, and faulty play by Williams nearly caused the downfall of the home goal. The home team took up the attack and good defensive play. by Harrison and Fozard caused them to retire to midfield. After a spell of even play the visitors rushed to the home goal, and a good shot by Green was only partially .cleared by Hadkiss, and rebounded from the upright through the goal. From the centre kick the home forwards led an attack on the visitors' goal, but keen tack- ling by the visitors' half-backs relieved the pres- sure, and play was transferred to the home end. Fine work by Jones and Aikenhead kept the visit- ors at bay for a while, but they stuck to their work, and Brown succeeded in increasing the lead. Net- tled by these reverses the home team played more strenuously and forced a corner kick. This was well placed by Willoughby and went through off the visitors' goalkeeper. Half-time: Appleton, 2 goals; Grappenhall, 1 goal. The second portion opened very quietly, but the visitors settled down to their game and made an attack on the home goal without result. The home forwards then made several half-hearted raids, and were easily repulsed. A fine shot by Rigby was well cleared by Millar, the visitors' goalkeeper, and after this the visitors had the hest of the exchanges, and farther goals were added by Brown and Woolley. Result: Appleton Hornets 4 goals; Grap- penhall, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>Williams; Ashcroft,</p>
        <p>WIDNES ST. MARY'S v. LATCHFORD CADETS. On the County ground, Widnes.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's: Mercer; Evans, Gahan; Crewe, Cooper, Thompson; Bickers, Bennett, McAlister, Wright and Gregory.</p>
        <p>Cadets: Miller; Crannigan, Jones; Massey, Gib- son, Trevor; Bradshaw, Davenport, F. Plumb, San- key and Mayers. Referee, Mr. J. Smith. The game opened very quietly and the visitors were the first aggressors, a long shot by Gibson being nicely saved by Mercer. Gregory and Wright were showing a good understanding and the visitors' defence had to be alert to clear their</p>
        <p>lines. Several long shots were well directed by Gibson, the visitors' centre-half, but Mercer proved very safe. The home team made a sustained attack on the Cadets' citadel, which resulted in Rickers opening the score for the " Saints " after fifteen minutes' play. Half-time: St. Mary's, 1 goal; Cadets, nil. The visitors now tried the long passing move- ments and several long shpts were sent in, but did not meet with success. Following an attack upon the home goal, the visitors were awarded a penalty kick, from which Gibson made the scores equal. Gregory and Wright led several raids on the visitors' goal, but the chances of scoring were not accepted. In the final stages of the game the visitors attacked strongly and gained another penalty for "hands " against Gahan. This had to be taken a second time, which was very fortu- nate for the Cadets, as Mercer saved the first shot, but the second attempt beat him. Result: Cadets, 2 goals; St. Mary's, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
