      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>I WIN. BUT POOR PLAY.</p>
        <p>Uninteresting Exhibition With St.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Helens.</head>
        <p>Helens.</p>
        <p>STAN. YOUNG'S MISADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>Through two tries scored by Dainteth and Trantei—the one in the first half and the other just before the final curtain—Warrington were successful against St. Helens, who were unable to augment a dropped goal by Halsall notched in the earlier stages of the game at Wilders- pool, on Saturday, but we have nothing else to boast about. We ought just to record the fact and say no more, but it was never thus. One's impressions on leaving the ground was that comment was superfluous, for a more lifeless and uninteresting game it would be hard to parallel. Long before the end one had had enough. The haphazard, easy-going style, of play by Warrington suggested a holiday en- counter. Perhaps it was the fact that it was St. Ilelens' last match of the season which tended to make the home team more charitable —a swan song is usually a sentimental affair— for throughout the game, with the exception of the last quarter of an hour, there seemed to be no attempt on the part of the home team, at any rate, to force matters. Happily, such can- not be said of the visitors; they did give themselves to the game and one repeatedly heard remarks of ".Keep it up, 'Saints' 1" passed between the players themselves. If something like that spirit had permeated their opponents the spectators generally would have been better pleased.</p>
        <p>All through Warrington appeared a very, dis- jointed lot: to put it at its highest their dis- play was only mediocre, and all the good that one had felt they had in previous matches was absent. The virtue that was in them seemed to have departed. There was no cohesion or swing in the back division at all. One hardly knows who to blame for this, but if the ball had been got away from the pack in a smarter manner it would have given the threequarters a chance of showing whether they were out for points. To say the least, the futile rushing and kicking of the halves was most annoying. As one has said before, in commenting upon previous matches, these tactics have their merits, but if they are not intelligently applied, then it is a mere waste of time running about the field. At the same time, of course, one must not overlook the fact that much of the attempted combination on the part of the home team—should one say ''much" amid so little?—was proken up through the offside tactics of the visiting halves and for- wards. But, then, that is another story. The referee is there to remedy those matters, and one can only criticise the team over the way in which they attempted to surmount their difficulties. There have been times when the team have been faced with similar conditions and have triumphed over them simply by intelligence and skilful manceuvring. Why not on Saturday against St Helens?</p>
        <p>One has said that Stan Young would be greatly missed. With him in the team things would have gone better. One hopes he will be able to lay aside the. khaki tunic for the jersey now and again and play with his old club. But, really, judging from last week's match he should have been given con- ditional exemption by the Tribunal—he is in- dispensable to the team. Stan had a sore dis- appointment in not being with the team, for on Saturday morning everything portended that he should be. He was given permission by his officer, to leave his training quarters, and looked up a train for Warrington. He thought he espied a very suitable one, but on reaching ,the station discovered that it ran every day of the week barring Saturday. So Stan, much- chagrined, arrived just as the match concluded. In training and could not spot a train!</p>
        <p>. A custom of singling out players for special mention must this week—and let one •be optimistic enough to hope that it will be only for this week—be postponed, but one may give a word of encouragement to our young for- wards. They played their_ part well and it was no fault of theirs that the game lacked interest, but one might add that the last line of forwards might be even more effective if they would heel the ball out more smartly instead of holding it so long. That is just a friendly tip while patting them on the back, you know. On one or two occasions, through the ball coming out of the pack rather slowly, the halves were severely handicapped.</p>
        <p>By thc way, Chesters was moving about the pavilion in mufti, having been discharged from the Army on the previous day. He has a leather strap to support his left leg, which was badly upset.</p>
        <p>Teams :</p>
        <p>Warrington.—llorrocks; Brookes, Tranter, Potter, Griffiths; Douglas, Dainteth; Harmer, Cunliffe, Glegg, Matthews, Wall, Jones. St. Helens.—Wilmott; Greenall. Woods, Col- lins, Aspinwall; Ha'sail, Wilson; Brighton, Hol- land, Sephton, Fletcher, Jones, Daniels.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON AT BROUGHTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AT BROUGHTON</p>
        <p>TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington are going to Broughton on the 1.50 to-day, the team being selected from the following:— Jolley, Horrocks; Potter, Tranter, Brookes, Griffiths; Young, ,Nicholas, Dainteth, Douglas; Harmer, Green, Cuilliffe, Glegg, Matthews, Jones, Wall and Dumbell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>IeVARRINGTON 'WORKING MEN'S ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The conditions this last fortnight at week-ends have been most wretched, and angling has suffered to a great extent. Important matches have taken members away from their usual course at this time of the year. The Gowy and ,Llangollen have, there- fore, been poorly patronised, and the back-end specimens are lacking as a result. The match at Norton and the one at Poynton yielded very poor results, and we may venture tr.; suggest that match fishing in canals is about fin- ished for this season. The officials will do well an- other year to have their open matches somewhat earlier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Norton Match,</head>
        <p>The Norton Match,</p>
        <p>The entry for this match was up to enSectations, especially after the wretched conditions. It was really surprising to find 201 at their pegs on the day of the contest, but many will face any weather to take part in an open match. Others turned up for the good of the cause. The officials were half an hour late in getting the competitors off, and if there bd been great weights caught they would have been handicapped in the weighing. However, things turned out a success, and the winner was found in H. Coffin, with an eel of 9oz. 4dr.; .2, H. Pennington, an eel, soz. 2dr.; 3, A. Ellison, roach, 4oz. 10dr.; and so on down to W. Makin with loz. Bdr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Poynton Match.</head>
        <p>The Poynton Match.</p>
        <p>This match was arranged by the Manchester Societies in aid of the Red Cross Society. As far as the entries were concerned it was a great success. well over 900 turned up to their pegs on the day, but what another wretched day it was!• We saw anglers who had journeyed forty miles to take part in the match, walk up to their pegs, have a smoke, and then decide not to unpack, and many only fished for thirty minutes. Those who saw the time out were in spots that were screened off by trees or high ground. There was not one from Warring- ton who caught a fish or saw time out, but we all liked the canal, and if a match is fished in decent weather and earlier on in the season, more entries will come from Warrington way. We were asked how it was that we could not command such an entry in Warrington. Well, it is not for not offer- ing the prizes, because we have offered £5 for first in an open match on the 3rd of July last, and the entry was 256. Manchester is more central for such a contest, but the one great thing is this: that a Sunday match is more convenient for the working man angler, as in a Saturday contest those who wish to take part in it and have to travel some dis- tance, would have to leave work early to be•in tinie for the match, and things have become rather ex- pensive. when we don't want angling to interfere with work; well, not at present, while we have the war on. • AM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. (Lancashire Combination.)</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. (Lancashire Combination.)</p>
        <p>TOXIN PLAY SHORT-HANDED AND ARE BEATEN.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town visited Walkden on Satur- day to play the return match, the teams having met the previous week, when the game resulted in a draw of two goals each: The " Colliers " were the only,team to hold an unbeaten certi- ficate, and they maintained their reputation by beating the Town by five goals to nil.</p>
        <p>The meeting of the clubs was looked forward to by many, and it was generally thought the Walkden. Club would lose their unbeaten certi- ficate, over 1,000 spectators witnessing the heavy defeat of the visitors.</p>
        <p>While the Walkden Club deserved to win, there .was nothing like five goals between the teams. Walkden were at full strength and had the assistance of Kay, the ex-Bolton Wanderers' goalkeeper, between the sticks, while Warring- ton were without Leah and Deakin, who had missed the train, and were consequently left with only nine men.</p>
        <p>During the first half matters were going very evenly, each side in turn attacking, and from breakaways three goals were scored by Walkden, which looked to be decidedly "off-side." .</p>
        <p>After the interval the visitors did practically all the attacking, except for occasional break- aways, when the other two goals were regis- tered against the Town.</p>
        <p>The weakness of the Town forwards in front ef goal was mainly responsible for what on paper was a heavy defeat.</p>
        <p>Walkden took the lead after about 30 minutes' play. The goal was got from a breakaway on the home left, some smart footwork by Brooks and Hays, who centred, but Stephens inter- vened • and dribbled up the field, leaving the three Wall-den players offside. Millward appealed for offside, but the centre-forward ran on uninterruptedly and scored an easy goal. The second and third goals, scored before the interval, were something similar.</p>
        <p>The game restarting, savis the Town going in determined fashion. It looked not very long before they would equalise, but Kay kept goal in quite his old form, and made many excel- lent saves. It seemed absolutely certain that Siddall, with a hook shot over his head, would beat him, but the ball struck his head and went round the Post. Berry and Marsden were a sound pair, Cookson and Cord being the pick of the half-backs. Flaherty led well, and Brooks was exceptionally good.</p>
        <p>Of the Town team, Kite was very unsafe, not showing a glimpse of his old form. Stephens and Millward were a clever pair, and tackled strongly. Whitfield and Ditchfield were the best half-backs on the field, and did plenty of work. Of the four forwards, Aspinall and Siddall were the pick, Woodhouse and Banner combining well. The former was on a "trial" with the Town, having been brought from the local Junior League, at inside-left,, and very well he played, his beautifully judged ,passes, excellent dribbling and a good shot, mark him as a " coming " lad, and I wish him luck.</p>
        <p>The teams were</p>
        <p>Walkden: Kay; Berry and Marsden; Cook- son, Cord and Caldwell; Miller , Meehan, Flaherty, Brooks and Hays. Warrington Town: Kite; Stephens and Mill- ward; Ditch&amp;ld, Whitfield and A. N. Other; A. N. Other, Siddall, Aspinall, Woodhouse and Banner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AT ROOD-LANE</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AT ROOD-LANE</p>
        <p>TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>A grand match should be witnessed to-day at Hood-lane, when Warrington Town meet Liverpool. The kick-off is timed for 3.15, and the home team will be selected from :—Kite; Leah, Millward; Stevens, Standfast, Whitfield; Ditchfield, Eccleston, Heywood, Aspinall, Woodhouse and Banner. It will be noticed that Siddall is missing. He is going to assist Manchester United, who are short of players. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Local Association football enthusiast tii will be in- terested to hear that the Town are going to run a reserve teaan in the St. Helens and'District League. The first match will take place at Hood-lahe on November 4th, when Earlestown West will be their opponents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>GOALS.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. b. F. A. P. Howley United St. Elphin's Orford Juniors Latchford Recs. ..... 7 3 2 2 16 12 8 Penketh Juniors Bewsey A.S 7 2 3 2 18 18 6</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets Apuleton Hornets GrEtTpenhall .1 1 0 5 7 2 Widnes St. Mary's Highfield Juniors Widnes Juniors</p>
        <p>HICHFIELD JUNIORS V. ORFZRD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former before a fair number of spectators.</p>
        <p>_ Highfield: Thornhill; Worrall, Bell; Barnes, Han- key, Wishlade; Tomlinson, Ford, Dunne, Peattie and Moulsdale.</p>
        <p>Orford: Conley; Gibson, Griffiths; Austin, Witton, Bothwell, Chisholm, Buckley, Hurst, Clare and Belshaw.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. T. Smith. The home goal was soon in danger, and Thornhill had several good shots to clear. Owing to the slackness of the home backs several corner kicks were tonceded, but eventually Bell cleared to mid- field. The visitors quickly returned to the attack, and Thornhill was excellent in goal, making several fine saves, After midfield play the visitors attacked very keenly, and Clare scored after 15 minutes' play. The home team retaliated strongly, and Con- ley conceded a corner kick. This was well placed, and Hankey was unfortunate not to equalise. From the goal kick the visitors attacked again, and were awarded a penalty kick, which Thornhill was suc- cessful in saving. Buckley scored the visitors' second goal in fine style. Then the home team became desperate and gained a penalty kick, from which Ford reduced the arrears. Chisholm scored the third and fourth goals. Half-time: Orford, 4 goals; Highfield, 1 goal. On resuming, good combination by the home for- wards placed the home goal in danger. Weak shooting enabled the visitors' defence to clear, and a visit to the home end resulted in Belshaw adding another goal. Moulsdale centred finely, and Tom- linson scored the second point for the home team. During another attack Bell and Worrall defended well, and a visit to the opposite end saw a fine opening missed, Ford being badly supported. A fine centre from Chisholm was converted by Buck- ley, for the visitors. Result: Orford Jaiiors, 6 goals; Highfielsls Juniors, 3 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S v. LATCHFORD RECS.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. LATCHFORD RECS.</p>
        <p>On the Steel-street ground</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's: Povey-: Tunstall, Furness; Cooper, Miller, Gleave; Watson, England, Lee, Jones and Holden.</p>
        <p>Recs.: Lockhart; Bra ndwood, Willcock; Dutton, Green, James; Heesom, Warburton, Brown, Cooper and Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. P. Gosling. The visitors won the toss and Lee kicked off, for the Saints before a good crowd of spectators. The game opened at a fast pace and immediately the Recs. made an attack on the Saints' goal, Povey clearing a fine shot by Dutton. Jones and Holden initiated an attack on the Recs.' goal, but Willcock cleared. The visitors made another attack, and Brown was unfortunate with a splendid effort which passed on the wrong side of the post. The Saints were unable to make much impression on the visitors' defence, and they were soon driven back to their cwn half. Cooper and Broadhurst Combined well and forced Tunstall to concede a corner. This was well got away by Miller, and play was trans- ferred to the opposite end, where Holden tested Lockhart with a fine shot, which was well saved. Heesom was conspicuous on the visitors' right, but failed to score. Half-time: No score.</p>
        <p>The second half opened very keenly, and the pace was well kept up. Brown and Green were neatly repulsed by Furness, who transferred to Holden on the left, and the latter centring accurately, Lee accepted the opening and scored f•r the Saints. The visitors played more keenly, and the home defence was severely tested, several corner kicks being concecled, but the visitors failed to improve upon them. The Saints' forwards tried hard to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>BEWSEY A.S. v. PENKETH JUNIORS..</head>
        <p>BEWSEY A.S. v. PENKETH JUNIORS..</p>
        <p>On. the Lovely-lane ground. Ainsworth scored for the• Juniors, but from the centre-kick the home team had the best of the exchanges, and Robinson made the scores. equal. Half-time :—Bewsey, 1; Penketh, 1. In the second half the home team gained a penalty kick, but failed to score. The final score was :—Bewsey, 1; Penketh, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Saturday was an ideal day for the Association game, and a full complement of games was played in the Junior League, and in several instances some surprising results were obtained. Grappenhall and Appleton Hornets, both new members, made their first acquaintance with each other on the Thelwall-lane ground. •Owing to the fine performance of the home team of the previous week and the inclusion of Williams in their ranks, theyt naturally were confident of victory, but the 'visitors having suffered defeat previously, made several alterations in their team, and with good effects. The game opened very evenly, but after twenty minutes' play the .visitors began to show their superiority, and were leading at half-time. The second half was chiefly in favour of the visi- tors, and they found the net twice without any reply from the home team. Williams did not play up. to his reputation for his new club, and the home team were best served by Jones, Aikenhead and Willoughby. The visitors' team played well throughout, and thoroughly deserved their victory.</p>
        <p>Highfield and Orford Juniors had their best teams out, but the Juniors from Orford proved to be the strongest, and found the net on six occasions. The home team scored three times.</p>
        <p>Buckley and Chisholm, late of St. Elphin's, but now of Orford, proved to be in deadly form, and scored twice each for their new club.</p>
        <p>The Highfield Juniors are improving greatly, as their ability to score three times against their opponents indicates.</p>
        <p>Austin, Witton, Gibson, Buckley and Gibson all showed good farm. Thornhill, in goal for the home team, .did very well, in spite of the goals- Scored against him. Ford did • not receive much assistance from his colleagues. Bell and Hankey, worked hard in defence.</p>
        <p>Both teams had a penalty-kick. Ford con- i-erted his, and Thornhill succeeded in saving the other.</p>
        <p>A very keen game was witnessed at Steel- street ground, where the Recs. provided the opposition. The respective defences proved too strong for the attack, with one exception, when the " Saints " scored the only goal of the match, Lee doing the needful from a grand centre by Holden f Povey has given a good exhibition of custodianship since his introduction between the uprights. Tunstall and Furness are prov- ing reliable in the back position. Miller, at centre-half, is always in the thick of the fight. I though he lacks height and weight. He is small, but good.</p>
        <p>Jones and Holden were the pick of the " Saints' " forward line. Lockhart gave a fine display against his old club mates. Brandwood and Miller were strong defenders. Green is improving at centre-half. Brown, Cooper and Broadhurst were the Recs.' best forwardsl</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Mary's played their first home fixture, and had the Cadets visitors to the, County Ground. Both teams gained their first point the ,pfeyious. week. The Cadets gained their .first victory, and there was much excite- ment in their camp. The " Saints " did not relish their defeat, especially after holding the lead at. the interval. The Cadets scored twice from penalty-kicks, Gibson being successful from the spot. Gahan unnecessarily gave away the second penalty. Mercer showed good form in goal for the "Saints." Crewe played a re- sourceful gaine at half-back. McAlister will improve at' the centre-forward position. Gregory. who is captain of the team, is the "Saints'" most clashing forward, and his col- leagues would do well to follow his example. Then goals will come quickly. The Cadets are now beginning to show a marked improvement on their previous displays. the result of intro- ducing fresh talent. Gibson was the most prominent, both in defence and attack.</p>
        <p>Widnes Juniors made their first appearance in the Park last week, in opposition to Howley 'United, and they brought their, strongest team, which showed good all-round form. Royle proved himself a capable custodian, and Mooney was very good at centre-half, and</p>
        <p>Baldwin and Magee were prominent in the for- ward line.</p>
        <p>The United forwards were rearranged owing to the absence of Woodhouse, who was having trial with the Town team at Walkden, Boyle filling the vacancy. % Foster and Rowlinson are sterling defenders, and defeated the efforts of the Juniors' forwards to pierce their lines.</p>
        <p>Hind, at half-back, is improving every week. Miller keeps up his reputation as a strenuous worker. Humphries and Fitzgerald were the pick of a moderate forward line.</p>
        <p>A very keen game was witnessed at Lovely- lane between Bewsey Adulth and Penketh Juniors, the scores at the interval being 'one goal each. In the second half the home team missed the lead from a penalty-kick, and the game ended in a division of points.</p>
        <p>The fixture list for the Junior League to-day includes what should prove to be some exciting encounters, as in two or three instances keen rivals are in opposition to each other. On the Steel-street ground there should be a keen struggle between St: ElPhin's and Tiowley United in their return fixture. The " Saints" have now got together a very useful team, and are intent on inflicting the first defeat on the United. And the latter are expecting to be able to put their best team out, and will fight hard to retain their unbeaten certificate. - _ .</p>
        <p>The meeting in• the Park between the Cadets and Grappenhall should prove a good match. There will be both old and new friends in opposition. Widnes Juniors and Widnes St. Mary's meet on the Tanhouse ground, and there is sure to be a keen fight for the points, as both are ,anxious to gain the maximum. Penketh Juniors and Appleton Hornets are also near neighbours, and a good game should be witnessed on the Rec.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Cartwright, who has charge of the collection fund for the aid of C. Price, one of the Recs.' players who, unfortunately, got his leg broken a few weeks ago, would be pleased if all the club secretaries would forward their balances as early as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES. WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHOOL v. INDUSTRIAL SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHOOL v. INDUSTRIAL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The above match was played on the ground of the latter on Saturday at Padgate, in fine weather. Tilling having won the toss for the visitors, Vava- sor kicked off and the home team made a raid on the visitors' goal, but were repulsed by Lloyd. In return the visitors rushed away and sent in several good shots. They were unable to penetrate Woods's sound defence. A pass from Dennett to George en- abled the latter to make tracks for goal, and after a tussle Vavasor rushed the ball through. Another goal was added for the home team by Gresty, and soon after the whistle blew for half-time. In the second half, Aishen, taking a fine pass from Dover, scored with a fine shot for the visitors. The Second- ary School again pressed, and tried hard to equal- ise, but were unequal to the task, and were de- feated by two goals to ene. Teams:—</p>
        <p>Warrington Secondary School: Critchley; Davies, Lloyd; Jones (D.). Tilling (capt.), Deakin; Dover, Edgar, Aishen, Healey and Simcock. Industrial School: Woods; Perkins, Cross (S.); Jones (A.), Watson, Gornell; Dennett, George, Vavasor, Gresty and Cross (J.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v., EMMANUEL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v., EMMANUEL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Teams representing the above clubs met on their new playing pitch in Victoria •Park last Saturday. The Emmanuel won the toss and the Central kicked off against a slight breeze and glaring sun, and led an attack on the Institute goal. Pritchard shooting wide. Even play followed for a while, when the Central again attacked, and Gilbert skimmed the crossbar with a grand shot. Then the Institute had to concede a corner, _which Parr cleared, and set his forwards going, but they found Glover and San- key too good for them, and had to retire to their own half. From a throw-in near the centre line the home forwards gained, possession, and Hands placing well to Gilbert, enabled the latter to score the first goal. Half-time: Central, 1 goal; Insti- tute, nil. On resuming the Central attacked keenly and forced several corner kicks without effect. Hallam set his forwards going, and Lister was called upon to save good shots in quick succession from Parr and Knight. The Central defenders raised the siege and good passing between Davies, Gilbert and Leah enabled the latter to score the second goal for the Central. From the centre kick the Institute had the best of the exchanges and set up a strong attack on the Central goal, Eaton being just wide with a long shot. The Central goalkeeper was penalised for handling outside the penalty area, and Parr -^nred from the free kick. Result: Central, 2 goals; Institute, 1 goal. The supporters of these clubs will be pleased to learn that a further appeal to the Town Council for a playing pitch has been successful, and in future the teams will play on what is called the No. 3 pitch.</p>
        <p>-Mll-</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in boxes, labelled Is-3d and 3s-Od.</p>
      </div>
