      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>"THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER" AND WALKING STU FUND.</head>
        <p>"THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER" AND WALKING STU FUND.</p>
        <p>Contributions From Afar.</p>
        <p>As an instance cf how widely "The Examiner', circulates abroad, we mentioned quite recently that a money order had reached Mr. Stewart Royston, of Paddington, for his Wounded Soldiers Walking Stick Fund all the way from Sydney, New South Wales, Australia. This week we are informed that a contribution of 10s. has been sent to him from Mr. and Mrs. Sharples, Box 299, Niagara, on the Lake Ontario, Canada, as they had seen the. Walk- ing Stick Fund mentioned and would like to have it acknowledged in the columns. of `-`The Examiner." We hope our readers will continue to post their copies, or, better still, extra copies, to relatives and friends abroad, and show them how well their wounded are being cared for in the Mother Country. _ _ _</p>
        <p>From far away California a fourth donation of 10s. has come from Mr. A. W. H. Peyton for the Wounded Soldiers' Walking Stick Fund, and he asks Mr. Royston "Have your friends been telling you that you do not 'stick at trifles and things like that,' I wonder?" A generous Scot, with brothers fighting in France and East Coast of Africa, sends a fifth 10s. for the walking sticks, and he declares the success of the fund shows how much it ap- peals to all. With the object of helping to increase the fund which is providing our brave wounded with walking . sticks, Mr. Norman B. .J. 'merlon; author of a booklet of poems, "Sapphire Sheen throUill Ebon Clouds," and who is a private in the Anti-Aircraft Battery, in the North of. Scotland, has very kindly pro- mised fifty copies free, "the proceeds of his threepenny booklet to go to the Walking Stick Fund." Thirty copies were sold by Mr. Roy- ston before receiving them, one Padgate lady, who spends much of her time doing acts of kindnesses among the wounded soldiers at Winwick, being first to order these pretty booklets of touching verses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Appreciation from the Lord Derby Hospital.</head>
        <p>Appreciation from the Lord Derby Hospital.</p>
        <p>During the month of September 500 walking sticks were sent to the Lord Derby War Hos- pital, and in reply Lieutenant-Col. Simpson, R.A.M.C., the administrator, writes to Mr. Royston:— _ _</p>
        <p>""It must be very gratifytng. to you to know that -your appeals meet with such a hearty. response, and that in spite of the heavy biirden you have to carry every day, your continued unflagging efforts are such a success and are so much appreciated by the brave boys who have fought, or are fighting, our country's battles."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>PRIVATE CHARLES DUKES</head>
        <p>PRIVATE CHARLES DUKES</p>
        <p>His Sentence ; Protest Against Proposed Inquiry.</p>
        <p>Sentences of two years' imprisonment, with hard labour, have been promulgated in the cases of seven conscientious objectors, privates in the 3rd Cheshire Regiment, who were tried by District Court Martial at Birkenhead on October 11th and 12th. The men tried in- cluded Private Charles Dukes, of Warrington, secretary of the local Trades and Labour Council, and Stanley B. Bodle, Latchford Without. _</p>
        <p>An inquiry has been directed to be held into the complaints of ill-treatment lodged against the Military Authorities preferred by Dukes and another pfivate named Beardsworth. Dukes, 'writing to Wiirririgton, states that as the inquiry is to be held in private, he sees no object to be gained by being present. Mr. Chancellor, M.P., has given notice of a question to• be asked in Parliament next Tuesday on this subject. On Thursday evening a specially summoned meeting of all the members of branches•of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held •in the Gas Workers' Union Offices, Regent-street. A fesolution was adopted pro- testing against the manner in which the inquiry into the charges made by Privates Dukes and Beardsworth had been arranged, and calling upon the Government to take im- mediate steps to institute a full and proper inquiry and to grant to these men facilities for the preparation of their case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>HIS POSITION AS COUNCILLOR AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>HIS POSITION AS COUNCILLOR AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>At the meeting, of the StoCkton Heath Parochial Committee, Mr. George Pilling. pre- siding, and Messrs. J. W. Clarke, F. Pearson, H. J. Westbrook, J. McDermid and C. W. Hallows being present, 'Mr. -Clarke .remarked that they noticed the absence .of Mr. Dukes.- (Laughter.) Mr. Westbrook said he intended to give notice to ask the 'Clerk at their next meeting the fol- lowing question :—"Whethet assuming the news- paper report to be true as to the sentence passed upon Mr. Charles Dukes, an elected Councillor for this parish, be is now disqualified from act- ing as such Councillor and as a member of this committee, vond if he is so disqualified whether the Council was now in a position to declare the position vacuilt and to proceed to fill the casual vacancy in the usual way." The Clerk (Mr. Hough) : I can answer the question now. I do not think he is lisqualified by sentence of Court-Martial. It must be a summary conviction, or a conviction by indict- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook said he had purposely asked the question. The Clerk's view was in accord- ance with his own opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>VICAR OF ST. JAMES'.</head>
        <p>VICAR OF ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. B. Firth Accepts Living At Stockport.</p>
        <p>"I have been so very, very happy here that it will come as a great wrench to have to leave Warrington," were the words used by the Rev. H. B. Firth, vicar of St. James' Parish, when our representative called on that: gentleman yesterday. , The reverend gentleman has been highly re- 'spected- and, honoured during his vicariate of , St. James' Church during his 'thirteen years' ministry, which terminates at the coming Christmas. He was formerly curate at ‘the Runcorn Parish Church, when • Archdeacon Maitland Wood, rector of Bowdon, was vicar. The announcement of his departure to the living of St. Matthew's, Stockport, is a matte, of widespread regret amongst the people in Latchford, and this excellent feeling of appre- ciation will follow him into his new sphere of labour. The Parish Church and its organ- isations, now in a highly efficient state, have progressed in a remarkable way since his in- stitution. He organised the -Mission of St. Hilda' s to meet the spiritual requirements of an increasing population on the Knutsford- road portion of the parish, and the new build- ing in Slater-street was opened within the past two years. About the same period a new' organ was erected in St. Japaes' Church, at a cost of over £4oo—half of which was obtained from the Carnegie Trust—and it has been an immense help in the musical portions of the services. Last Christmas saw the installation of electric lighting in the building, at a cost of nearly £lOO. To the Vicar's energy is due this splendid development in parochial organ- isations, and his departure at the close of the year—to even a more fruitful field of labour— is the source of widespread regret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CLOSING AT 7 P.M.</head>
        <p>CLOSING AT 7 P.M.</p>
        <p>Shopkeepers Who Are Keeping To Original Order.</p>
        <p>When it was announced that from Monday next all shops would have to close at seven o'clock in the evening except on Fridays and Saturdays, when the hour was to be eight and nine o'clock respectively, the majority of shop- keepers in Warrington glowed with satisfac- tion, and despitsi the fact that the Home Secretary has decined to extend the limit by an hour, many of them intend closing at seven o'clock. We hoi)e that all shop will follow suit, for it ii generally recognised that, ow ing to the vstricted lighting, very little business is done after that hour. and to remain open is unnecessary to the public, barely profitable to the proprietor, and exceedingly tiresome to the assistants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Money.</head>
        <p>Money.</p>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM GENERAL.</p>
        <p>Tribunal Delayed In Consid- ering Same.</p>
        <p>Although called for 10.15 a.m., Thursday's Tribunal did not begin until an hour later, when Dr. Joseph (deputy-mayor), Alderman Tinnion and Mr. J. J. Whitley .took their seats.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph said, on behalf of the Tribunal, he was sorry for the delay in the commence- ment of their proceedings. Sometimes it was difficult to. obtain a quorum. Furthermore, they had to consider a communication from the General in. Command of the Western Dis- trict, and it had taken up some of their time. The majority of the cases heard were appli- cations by employers for further exemptions in the case of men already granted various periods, and there were also instances of de- badged single men. . Clock Minder and Repairer.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Eustance and Co., watchmakers and jewellers, were granted exemption for a fur- ther month in respect Of a clock minder and repairer, in order to give him an opportunity if being examined by the Medical Board. The Chairman said the firm should make, e.cry effort in the meantime to get a substitute. De-badged Single Men.,</p>
        <p>A moulder at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s, aged 24, 'single, who had been de-badged, applied for exemption on domestic grounds.— The appeal was refused. A single man, aged 31 years, who had been de-badged, appealed on domestic grounds and held that he was entitled• to as much consider- ation as a married man, as he had to keep the home together and an Aged father and mother to support. He had been de-badged while married men of all ages were not affected.</p>
        <p>-In reply to the. Chairman, he admitted that his parents were receiving the old-age pension, but said that the pension would be affected if he went into the Army and made an allow- ance.</p>
        <p>The point was a new nut for the Tribunal to crack and they reserved their decision .in order, to get information on the subject</p>
        <p>Tea Company's Manager.</p>
        <p>Messrs. R. H. Smith &amp; Co., tea merchants, appealed for further exemption in the case of ttieir manager, who had been passed in "C" class for home service. The firm, it was stated, were trying to instal a lady manager, and it would require some time to coach her in the duties. They asked for three months' exemption.—The Tribunal granted until the end of the year. Big Grocery Firm's Order Clerk.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Henry Milling and Co., wholesale and retail grocers, &amp;c., appealed on behalf of an assistant in the order department, 39 years of age, a married man with five children. It was stated that the man had been with the firm for the past twenty years and was inti- mately acquainted with the prices and classes of all goods wanted by various customers. He had been passed in B (i.) class. Out of a former staff of 65, 35 had enlisted or been called up.—The case was adjourned for con- sideration. •</p>
        <p>Laundry Hands.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swallow, solicitor, appeared for the Warrington and Latchford -Laundry Company, who appealed in respect of two of their em- ployes—an engineer, aged 36, passed for C (i.), and a motor man, 34, passed in Class A. The firm have a large number: of contraets for laundry work in connection with military hospitals and auxiliary hospitals. • The Military Representative (Capt. Hackett) pointed out, with reference to the engineer, that he had taken the place of a man over military age.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted one month's exemp- tion in the case of the motor man, and ad- journed the other appeal for further inquiry. Carter Exempted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Willett,. solicitor, appearing for Messrs. Clare and Ridgway, builders' merchants, secured conditional exemption for a career, a married man, aged 37 and having a family of five children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Travelling Showman.</head>
        <p>A Travelling Showman.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cooke, a travelling showman, round- abouts, &amp;c., asked that his son, a married man, aged 35, with five children, should be exempted. Applicant had only partial sight of one 'eye, and his son drove the traction engine and worked the shows, which supported both families. £2,700 had been 'invested in the business. Applicant's two other sons were serving in the Army.—Conditional exemption was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>In The Forties.</head>
        <p>In The Forties.</p>
        <p>Conditional exemption was granted to a painter's vanman, aged 40, married,' with five children.</p>
        <p>An applicant, a married man, with two children, reached his 41st birthday on May 2nd of this year. He stated that he had attested and one of the members remarked it was a pity he had done so, as he was in a worse position than if he had not. Dr. Joseph, in announcing the decision, said the Tribunal thought he should be in as good a position as an unattested man, and they would accordingly exempt him. Another applicant, married man, aged 41 last February, was exempted on 'a like ruling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>APPLETON FARMER'S APPEAL.</head>
        <p>APPLETON FARMER'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>Big Business And Large Turnover</p>
        <p>Have No Effect At -Chester.</p>
        <p>At the Ghester Appeal Tribunal on Thursday an Appleton farmer and shepherd, who was joint owner with his brother of a flock of sheep averaging 650 all the year • round, ap- pealed against the refusal of the Dutton Tribunal to grant him 'exemption. Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington, appeared for the appellant, and it was stated that from September to the Christmas sales the appellant and his brother had, approximately, 1,000 sheep, exclusive of lambs. They sold 600 fat sheep between October and March •lasts and for the next six months they were sending be- tween 30 and 40 fat sheep to the local markets each week. They rented 350 acres for grazing and grew four acres of swede turnips for feed- ing the sheep during the winter months. Last year the Government took their hay, and this year they were taking over 35 v.cres of hay, together with the wool. They had only one man, who helped in his spare time, and he had now been called into the Army. The appellant was a skilled shepherd and he claimed that under the regulations he was entitled to conditional exemption, as he was in a certified occapation. In answer to the Military Itoresentative, the appellant stated that it would be impos- sible for his brother to manage, the sheep, a!one.</p>
        <p>The appeal was' refused, •-,s was also an a!,plieation for leave to apply to the Central Tribunal on a legal .questicn arising under the Statutory .Rules and: Orders. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Weleo Cocoa.</head>
        <p>Weleo Cocoa.</p>
        <p>Welco Cocoa, which has the additional recom- mendation of being prepared locally, is a fine nourishing drink, and its price has not been raised to an exorbitant war-time standard. As prices go nowadays, in fact, this cocoa at gd. the quarter pound tin is remarkaTe value, especially when the rich flavour of the cocoa is taken into consideration. Mr. John Roth- well, of Golborne, is the manufacturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre,</p>
        <p>Intensely interested in.everything with which he was associated, Sir Joseph Beecham, who passed away with painful Suddenness on Mon- day morning, was a not infrequent visitor to the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, of which he had been proprietor since November 7th. 1908. He usually occupied the top right hand box, and, in winter time, when he came in with his magnificent fur coat, -which he bought whilst in Canada, he looked a distinguished figure,</p>
        <p>As has been emphasised in obituary notices, Sir Joseph had It passion for opera. When the Harrison-Frewin Opera Company were here last time he came over to hear . Miss Evangeline Florence and .Mr. John Harrison in "Faust."</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at St. Helens vesterday, and among those present was Mr. Ha'rry War- burton, the manager of the Royal Court.</p>
        <p>' By the way, the Harrison-Frew-in Opera Com- pany are due at the Royal Court for the week commencing November 6th. The company almost identical with that which appeared be- fore, excepting that other distinguished artistes have been added. These include Miss Nora D' Argel, the famous New Zealand soprano; Miss Christine Oliver, the great Belgian contralto, who was recently with the Carl Rosa Opera Company; and Mr. Robert Parke, lately a prin_ cipal baritone with the Quinlan Opera Com- pany. Of course, the operas will be once-nightly productions, and there is to be no increase in prices of admission.</p>
        <p>That 'brilliant Criterion Theatre success, "A Pair of Silk Stockings," is this week holding favour with those Who esteem high-class comedies. Mr. Charles Steuart and his company make the most of -a witty and clever play with innumerable dramatic incidents, notably in the bedroom scene.</p>
        <p>It is a coterie of distinguished artistes who' appear; including the Royal Court Theatre in their itinerary round the principal theatres in the provinces. Mr. Steuart is a past master in the art of presenting fine productions. When war broke out he had been on the road with "A Pair of Silk Stockings" for a month and, though for a few- months business suffered, the merits of the play have ever since drawn good houses. Of eight members of Mr. Steuart's com- pany who have joined the service's,- two have been killed. Mr. Steuart himself is expecting to be called up early in the New Year, and, in this connection, it is interesting to note that he is a nephew of the late Sir Redvers Bullet., and • a son of an 'officer in the Seaforth High- landers.</p>
        <p>Miss Lilian Constance, who is playing the role of leading lady—Molly Thornhill—with verve, vivacity and charm, left musical comedy to come into this production. Silk stockings every time!</p>
        <p>" Kisses " will be given once nightly, and at Monday and Thursday matinees in the Royal Court during the coming week. It is an ex- treinely mirthful production,. full of fun and melody. Presented by Mr. J. W. Jackson, it is hound to have a successful run. A powerful company includes Albert Bruno, late of Karno's, and one of the most irresistible comedians on the stage. Mr. Bruno is great fun. Once seen, always remembered. The com- pany surrounding him includes Ernest „Har- greaves, the eminent tenor, and a coterie • of beautiful girls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>When it was said man in his lifetime plays many parts, it only referred to seven moultings. Mr. Pip Powell, who does such a lot of hard work in that bright and entertaining revue, " Honi Soit," which has filled the Hippodrome during the week, played seven parts and changed his costumes 28 times and his sex once in the slime day.</p>
        <p>Although panting at the close of Monday's matinee, having vigorously bombarded the trenches in front with 'snowballs and been thercileSsly fusilladed by 'wounded ' soldiers in return, Mr. Pip Powell, , who has had o crowded life of theatrical experiences, was able to give me an interesting resume. He was born in Glasgow in 1874, his father being the famous circus manager and ring master, Mr. William Henry Powell, manager for Mr. Charles Hengler, and who married Miss Hengler.</p>
        <p>Pip appeared in public when six years of age, dancing a hornpipe in "Red Riding Hood." He never took properly to circus work, and he was sent to Chester to be educated for civil engineer_ ing. He captured some trophies in examina- tions, but at 16 threw .'up the whole bag of tricks and turned to the stage.</p>
        <p>He wanted to play Shakespeare, but Mr. Benson advised Margate, where Miss Sarah Thorne gave him a trial, and befoie six months was up he displayed so much ability that he got a low comedian's part in a drama. It was rapid movement in the three years that followed, 'and during that time he played every conceivable part.</p>
        <p>Four hundred and fifty plays may be esti- nfated ; drama, Shakespearian; farce, old English comedy, Irish plays, &amp;c. But although ex- tremely versatile, Mr. Pip Powell is a big hit in pantomime. The coming pantomime in the New Queen's, Manchester, when he will be the dame, will be his twenty-third consecutive pantomime, with the exception of two years, when he was at the Gaiety Theatre, London.</p>
        <p>Ile was introduced by George Thorne to Macready, Chute, so popular as Jimmy Chute, one of the " real gentleman managers," who engaged him for a pantomime in Bristol, which ran 14 or 15 weeks. In the cast were also George P. Huntley, Bessie Wentworth and Evie Greene, the latter being the fairy queen.</p>
        <p>When the time came for everyone to take to the halls—those who wished to make money— he made three attempts, and had such a suc- cess with "A Perfect,Woman " at Leeds that agents chased him for bookings. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Powell has been to Australia, Tasmania and New Zealand, spending 16 weeks in the Antipodes, and becoming immensely popular. At the end of the Manchester (New Queen's) pantomime, he goes to Mr. Oswald Stoll for a three years',. engagement. He is a member of the Volunteer Force, Westminster Guards.</p>
        <p>Mystery, mirth and music is the motto for tha Hippodrqme next week. Giordano, the Italian illusionist and entertainer, holds a conspicuous place on the bill, and the American "Speed Boys," who describe themselves as "The Three Scamps," are also given a prominent position. Jock Miles, the famous Scotch humorist and raconteur pays us a return visit, and will doubtless have a great reception. Colley and Scott in comedy - "Tit-Bits," and Norman Field, in a catchy piece, promise exceptional fun. Alberta Flahey, violiniste; and Myra Norman, contralto vocalist, are also on a most. attractive programme.</p>
        <p>The Kinemas. "Out of the Drifts," a fine picture featuring Margaret Clarke, is now showing at the Star Kinema House, where next week there will be "The Golden Claw," a magnificent society drama, during the first half of the week, and "Audrey," from the famous novel of that name, during the latter half.</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric. Theatre, Bridge Foot, "Moth and Flame" is attracting large "houses.' Booked for next week are "Sins of Society" an.] the twenty-second episode Of the "Diamond From The Sky" during the first portion, and during the latter part "Her Great Hour," the fifth episode of "Peg o' the 'Ring," and Charlie Chaplin in " His Trysting Place."</p>
        <p>"The Juggernaut" is 'the title of a first-rate picture at present being screened at the Pavilion, Lovely-lane, where next week we are to have a fine historical drama, " Barnaby Rudge," and the twenty-second episode of the "Diamond From The Sky" during the first half, and "The Night Birds of London" and the fourth episode of "Peg o' the Ring" during the latt6r half.</p>
        <p>&lt;</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>ii</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>SEPARATION ALLOWANCE GRANTED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Ann Clay, 27, Guardian-street, sought a separation order from the magistrate at .Tuesday's Borough Police Court against her huSband, Isaiah Clay, 20, Edward-street, on the gr4nd Of cruelty and ,threat. d At -a .former Colift defendant had been fine., £lO for •assaulting his wife. • Mrs. Clay 11,-"„ stated that he threatened to strangle her. had ill-treated her since she married hi°. There was a child five years old. Defendant stated that his wife left him ftr years ago, when the baby was ten months .01' His sister had to rear the child. The Chief Constable said that defendant sv.:l,! a fighting man. On the 15th October, 1914, °- was finedb2os..for fighting—"not in the trench°' but in the, streets." There were other convie- tions. The Court granted a separation order an, fixed an allowance at the rate of 12s. Gd• P 6 week.</p>
        <p>LIGHTS AND THE COUNTRY'S INTEREST' The following fines were inflicted at TueS- 'day's 'Borough Police Courtin cases where Per_; sons had failed to obscure light,. after schedule" time :—.Teremiali. Hammond, Market Gate, j°s';- James Critchley, 45, Sankey-street, 10s.; Hewitt, 112, Bridge-street, 10s.; and Willia Thoina' Bennett, 81. Lovely-lane, ss. of One trader having • pleaded exigencies business, the Chairman (Councillor gellr' the Roberts) said that the genet welfare of country was far more iniportant than the terests of anyone else. The police force was few in number, and they had to depend verL much on voluntary effort. People must she" the every Consideration in complying with Order. At Thursday's Police-Court the following were fined for infringing the Lighting Ord Robert Tickie, 52, Buttermarket-street, 205.; .insenh Stgrkey, c/a W. Fletcher, Ltd., 19z Pinners Brow, 10s.; Robt. li. Pearson, Bridge-street, •10s.; ,Joseph Henry. Houghton, 4", ° Mersey.-street; 10s.; Richard Clifton, 33, Mersa street, 10s.; and Peter Coleman, 60, 'Tanners- lane, ss. .</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and co. (Warrington) Limited, at the EXA.MINEJO General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, Wa rington; also published by, them at the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster; and at Church-street, Runcorn; in the CountY of Chester. - -</p>
      </div>
