        <p>• SIGNALLER G. H. HAZLEHITRST. • Died from burns.</p>
        <p>DRIVER E. BUTTERWORTH. Wounded. inquired about him, but no news of his where- abouts has reached us. . . . He 7/as much liked by the boys of his old battery, and he was always cheerful." . . • Enlisting in September, 1914, Driver Butter- worth has been at the Front fourteen .months. He attended Christ Church-Bible Class, and was educated at that school, his -age being 20. He was' formerly employed by the Latchford Steam Laundry C0.,-Ltd., and has twelve cousins in the Army. A brother, Private Walter Butter- worth*, is with the Transport. Section, South Lancashire Regiment. RIFLEMAN ERNEST APPLETON. ,Ex-Railway Fireman Sustains Gun- Shot Wound. Sustaining a gunshot wound on September 15th, Rifleman Ernest Appleton, of the King's Royal Rifles, and of 15, Earl-street, is now get- ting on nicely in a\cosy Birmingham hospital. He has written to his wife to that effect, adding that he is now able to walk about, although his calf, where the missile struck him, is far from being free from pain. , Rifleman Appleton was a fireman on the L.</p>
